hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1201","1066 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zurara ibn Karim ibn al-Harith ibn 'Amr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather al-Harith, that he met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, the fara'i' and the 'ata'ir? He said: Whoever wishes may do the fara', and whoever wishes may not do the fara'; and whoever wishes may do the 'atira, and whoever wishes may not do the 'atira. For sheep, its sacrifice. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith uncovered for us what we sought in what preceded from us in this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"1202","Chapter 164: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the fara'a. Abu Ja'far said: We reported in what preceded from us in these chapters from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the fara' what we reported from him in it. We wanted to know what that fara' is. Al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Abdullah, meaning al-Shafi'i, said in the interpretation of the fara'a: It is something by which the people of the Jahiliyya sought blessing in their wealth. One of them would slaughter the firstborn of his she-camel or sheep and not rear it, hoping for blessing in what comes after it. They asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Do the fara' if you wish - meaning slaughter if you wish. They used to ask him about what they did in the Jahiliyya, fearing it would be disliked in Islam. He informed them that there is nothing disliked upon them in it, and commanded them by way of choice to rear it, then carry loads upon it in the path of Allah Almighty."
"1203","Chapter 165: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his asking Allah Almighty to return the sun to him after its setting, and Allah Almighty returning it to him, and what was reported from him that suggests to one who imagines opposition to that. 1067 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: al-Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan, from Fatima bint al-Husayn, from Asma' bint 'Umays, who said: Revelation was coming to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while his head was in the lap of 'Ali, and he did not pray 'Asr until the sun had set. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you prayed, O 'Ali? He said: No. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, he was in obedience to You and obedience to Your Messenger, so return the sun to him. Asma' said: I saw it set, then I saw it rise after it had set."
"1204","1068 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musa narrated to me, from 'Awn ibn Muhammad, from his mother Umm Ja'far, from Asma' bint 'Umays, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr at al-Sahba', then sent 'Ali, peace be upon him, on an errand. He returned, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed 'Asr. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his head in the lap of 'Ali and did not move it until the sun had set. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, Your servant 'Ali detained himself for Your Prophet, so return its east (light) to him. Asma' said: The sun rose until it fell upon the mountains and upon the earth. Then 'Ali stood, performed ablution, and prayed 'Asr, then it set. That was at al-Sahba' in the campaign of Khaybar. Abu Ja'far said: We needed to know who Muhammad ibn Musa mentioned in the chain of this hadith is; he is Muhammad ibn Musa al-Madani, known as al-Fitri, praiseworthy in his narration. We needed to know who 'Awn ibn Muhammad mentioned in it is; he is 'Awn ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib. We needed to know who his mother is from whom he reported this hadith; she is Umm Ja'far bint Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Talib. Someone said: How do you accept this when you report from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what refutes it? He mentioned what:"
"1205","1069 - 'Ali ibn al-Husayn Abu 'Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Fadl ibn Sahl al-A'raj narrated to us, he said: Shadhan al-Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun was never held back for anyone except for Yusha'."
"1206","1070 - And what Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Hayyawayh al-Naysaburi Abu Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: Fadl ibn Sahl al-A'raj narrated to us, he said: Shadhan al-Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun was never returned since it was returned for Yusha' ibn Nun on the nights he marched to Bayt al-Maqdis. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that this hadith was reported to us differently by its two narrators, as we mentioned from each of them what he reported to us upon it. As for what 'Ali ibn al-Husayn reported to us upon it, it is that the sun was never held back for anyone except for Yusha'. If the reality of the hadith is so, there is no contradiction in it to what is in the first two hadiths, because what is in it is the holding back of the sun from setting, while what is in the first two hadiths is its return after setting. As for what Yahya ibn Zakariyya reported to us from him, it is that it was never returned since it was returned for Yusha' ibn Nun until the time in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them this saying. That does not preclude that it was not returned until that day, then returned after that. And this is not objectionable among the acts of Allah Almighty. And there was reported about its holding back from setting for a purpose needed by one of the prophets of Allah Almighty, that it remain for him because of it."
"1207","1071 - As Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar ibn Maysara, meaning al-Qawariri, narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Qatada, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A prophet among the prophets raided with his companions and said to them: No man who built a house and did not dwell in it, or married a woman and did not consummate with her, or has a need to return, shall follow me. He saw the enemy at the setting of the sun and said to them: It (the sun) is commanded, and I am commanded, until judgment is passed between me and them. He said: So Allah held it back for him, and he conquered, and they took booty, but fire did not consume it. When they took booty, Allah would send fire upon it and consume it. Their prophet said to them: You have misappropriated; let a man from every tribe come and pledge to me. They came and pledged to him. The hand of a man among them stuck to his hand, and he said to him: Your companions have misappropriated; let them come and pledge to me. They came and pledged to him, and the hands of two men among them stuck to his hand. He said to them: You two have misappropriated. They said: Yes, we misappropriated the image of a cow's head of gold. They brought it and cast it into the booty, and Allah sent fire upon it and consumed it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said at that: Allah Almighty fed us the booty as a mercy with which He had mercy on us, and lightening for what He knew of our weakness. Abu Ja'far said: All these hadiths are among the signs of prophethood. 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughira related to me, from Ahmad ibn Salih, that he used to say: It is not fitting for one whose path is knowledge to fall short of memorizing the hadith of Asma' which he reported to us from him, because it is among the greatest signs of prophethood. Abu Ja'far said: It is as he said. In it is, for one for whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated Allah Almighty with what he supplicated for him, that great measure and lofty rank; because that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so that he pray that prayer of his for which he detained himself for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until the sun set in its time, without missing it. In that is what indicates severity about missing 'Asr. And from that is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1208","1072 - As 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever misses the 'Asr prayer, it is as if his family and wealth were taken from him. Abu Ja'far said: So Allah Almighty protected 'Ali, peace be upon him, from that, due to his obedience to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In this hadith is what must be noted: the permissibility of sleeping after 'Asr, since some people held that disliked."
"1209","1073 - As Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Fulayh al-Khuza'i Abu 'Abdullah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: I saw al-Layth ibn Sa'd going to the mosque close to Maghrib prayer. Bakr ibn Mudar said to him: Why do I see you, O Abu al-Harith, with an excited face? He said: I prayed 'Asr, then returned to my house and slept, then went at this hour. Bakr said to him: Did you not know what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about sleeping after 'Asr? Al-Layth said: No. Bakr said: 'Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sleeps after 'Asr and his mind is snatched, let him blame none but himself. Al-Layth said: I never heard this from the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith was interrupted (munqati'). What we reported before it was preferable to it, due to its connection to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1210","And as Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Jabir al-Rashidi Abu 'Abdullah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yahya al-Burullusi narrated to us, he said: Haywa and Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, they said: 'Amr ibn Ziyad al-Hadrami informed us that Abu Firas informed him that he heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As saying: Sleep is of three kinds: foolish sleep, natural sleep, and stupid sleep. As for foolish sleep, it is the morning nap while people attend to their needs and he is asleep. As for natural sleep, it is the midday nap. As for stupid sleep, it is sleep when prayers are due. Abu Ja'far said: Except that some people reconciled what is in the hadith of Asma' and what is in the hadith of 'Uqayl, though it is interrupted, since it was their way to accept the interrupted as sound for them, upon that each of them has a meaning other than the other hadith. They made the hadith of Asma' upon that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not by his choice; it was only what Allah Almighty detained for him to obligate to him, and that is not sleep at all. They made the hadith of 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon sleep itself, so they disliked thereby sleeping after 'Asr. What we reported about it from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, and what was reported about it from Khawwat ibn Jubayr, strengthened that for them."
"1211","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from Thabit ibn 'Ubayd, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Khawwat ibn Jubayr, who said: Sleep at the beginning of the day is foolishness, in the middle is natural, and at the end is stupidity."
"1212","And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to us, he said: al-Nu'man ibn Mundhir narrated to us, he said: I was sleeping after 'Asr at Dabiq, when Makhul came to me and kicked me with his foot, then said: Stand, for you have been punished. I said: What is that, O Abu 'Abdullah? He said: This hour is when the people come out and spread - meaning the jinn - and in this nap is madness. If someone says: There was reported about sleeping in the day something requiring dislike besides what you mentioned, it is said to him: That was reported from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan."
"1213","1074 - What 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Musa ibn 'Imran ibn Mannah, from Aban ibn 'Uthman, from 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Morning sleep prevents some provision. Abu Ja'far said: Except that the people of chains weaken this chain, because it is from Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash from other than the people of his land, though they do not avoid reporting it. If he says: Is there anything in that from any of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? It is said to him: There was reported in that from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr what:"
"1214","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, that 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr said: O 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, do you not know that the earth cried out to its Lord Almighty from the morning sleep of the scholars, fearing heedlessness upon them? In what we mentioned is what requires avoiding what contains this point we mentioned and what else in what we mentioned in it. And Allah we ask for success."
"1215","Chapter 166: Derived from the hadith of 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, which some of its narrators raise to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and some stop at Ibn 'Abbas, about what is meant by the saying of Allah Almighty: And We joined their offspring with them in faith, We joined their offspring with them. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Murra narrated to us, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about this verse: And those who believed, and We joined their offspring with them in faith. He said: Ibn 'Abbas said: The believer's offspring are raised for him so that Allah Almighty delights his eye, even if they are below him in deeds. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Shu'ba narrates this hadith from 'Amr ibn Murra, not going beyond Ibn 'Abbas with it. As for al-Thawri, he used to narrate it from a shaykh of his called Sama'a, from 'Amr ibn Murra. Muhammad ibn Bishr al-'Abdi reports from him that he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi reports from him that he stopped it at Ibn 'Abbas."
"1216","1075 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Shukayb al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sama'a, from 'Amr ibn Murra, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty raises the offspring of the believer with him in his rank, even if he did not reach it in deeds, to delight his eye with them. Then he recited: And those who believed, and We joined their offspring with them - the verse."
"1217","And as 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Sama'a narrated to me, he said: 'Amr ibn Murra narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas - and he did not raise it - he said: Allah Almighty raises the offspring of the believer in his ranks to delight his eye with them, even if they are below him in deeds. Abu Ja'far said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' al-Asadi also reported this hadith from 'Amr ibn Murra, and did not go beyond Ibn 'Abbas with it."
"1218","As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murra, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, then he mentioned the like of his hadith, from al-Firyabi, from Sufyan, from Sama'a, and added: Then he recited: And those who believed, and We joined their offspring with them in faith - the verse. Abu Ja'far said: As for this hadith, we know with certainty, even if we found none of its narrators raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Ibn 'Abbas took it only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), since what is in it is information about Allah Almighty of His intent in the verse mentioned in it, and that is not taken from other than the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then we examined what is in this hadith, and found in it Allah Almighty's raising of the offspring of the believer who followed him in faith, with the believer whose offspring they are, to delight his eye with them, and His joining them to him. We found other than the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the believers included in that. By that we understood that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more included in that than them, and that in Allah Almighty's joining to him his offspring who followed him in faith, to delight his eye thereby, he is preferable to the rest of the believers besides him. That was for the rest of the believers besides him to delight their eyes thereby; for him in his offspring who followed him in faith it was preferable, and they were thereby worthier of him. And Allah we ask for success."
"1219","Chapter 167: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the most abject of names, which of them it is. 1076 - 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyayna narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayra, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The most abject of names to Allah is a man named with the name of the King of kings. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith to establish what is intended by it. We found khun' means abasement and submission. It is said: the man khana'a khunu'an when he submitted and was abased. The submission and abasement in this fell upon the bearer of the name, not the name itself, because no blame or praise attaches to a name. That was like His saying Almighty: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High - meaning by His name. Like His saying Almighty in the story of His prophet Lut, peace be upon him: And We saved him from the town that used to do abominations - He does not mean thereby the town itself, but its people who used to do abominations. And like His saying Almighty: And Allah struck the example of a town that was secure and tranquil, its provision coming to it abundantly from every place, then it denied Allah's favors, so Allah made it taste the garment of hunger and fear for what they used to do - He means its people, not itself. Then Allah Almighty clarified His intent thereby in it by His saying: And there came to them a messenger from among them. What is meant by the King of kings is Allah Almighty. So one named with a name from His names Almighty was arrogant, and Allah Almighty returned him thereby to submission and abasement; since the greatest of His names Almighty are His attributes by which He Almighty distinguishes Himself from His creation - of mercy, of might, of greatness, of majesty, and of what else Almighty. With other than that of His names Almighty, like His greatest name about which Allah Almighty said: Do you know any namesake for Him? He restricted creation from that and was alone with it Almighty, and attributed His names to Himself, saying Almighty: And to Allah belong the most beautiful names, so call upon Him by them. And with Allah is success."
"1220","Chapter 168: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Satan makes lawful the food of the people when they do not mention Allah's name over it - what is meant by that making lawful? 1077 - 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Ma'mar, he said: Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani, from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a large bowl was brought. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held back from it, and we would not place our hands until he placed his hand. A Bedouin came as if driven, falling upon the bowl and eating from it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his hand and seated him. Then a slave girl came, reaching with her hand to eat. He took her hand and seated her. Then he said: Satan makes lawful the food of the people when they do not mention Allah's name over it. When he saw you holding back, he brought the Bedouin to make it lawful thereby, then brought the slave girl to make it lawful thereby. By Allah besides whom there is no god, his hand is in my hand along with their hands. Abu Ja'far said: All the people of knowledge of hadith say Ma'mar erred in the chain of this hadith from al-A'mash, and the correct in its chain is:"
"1221","1078 - What Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Khaythama, from Abu Hudhayfa, from Hudhayfa, who said: When we were present with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for food, we would not place our hands in it until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand. We were present with him for food, and a Bedouin came as if pushed, going to strike his hand in the food. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his hand. Then a slave girl came as if pushed, going to strike her hand in the food. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took her hand. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Satan makes lawful food over which Allah Almighty's name is not mentioned. He brought this Bedouin and this slave girl to make your food lawful thereby. By Him in whose hand is my soul, his hand is with their hands in my hand at this hour."
"1222","1079 - And what Fahd also narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakha'i narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Khaythama narrated to me, he said: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, who said: When we were invited with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to food, we held back our hands until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand. We were invited to food, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held back his hand, so we held back our hands. A Bedouin came as if driven, reaching with his hand. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his hand and seated him. Then a slave girl came as if driven, reaching with her hand. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took her hand and seated her. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When we denied him that Allah Almighty's name be mentioned, he brought this Bedouin - as if meaning a devil - to make our food lawful thereby, so I took his hand and seated him. Then he brought this slave girl to make our food lawful thereby, so I took her hand and seated her. By Him in whose hand is my soul, his hand is in my hand along with their hands. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named Allah and ate. Abu Ja'far said: We needed to know who this Abu Hudhayfa is from whom this hadith is reported. We looked into that."
"1223","1080 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn 'Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from 'Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Hudhayfa - and he was among the companions of 'Abdullah - from 'A'isha, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Safiyya is a woman - and she gestured with her hand, meaning she is short. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have mixed it with a word that, had you mixed the sea with it, would have mixed it. She said: And I imitated a man before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It would not please me to imitate a man even if I had such and such. Abu Ja'far said: By that we established that he is among the companions of 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and in that was what indicates the greatness of his rank and loftiness of his station. Then we sought the tribe he is from, and found al-Bukhari mentioned him in his history, saying: His name is Salama ibn Suhayba al-Arhabi, and Arhab is from Hamdan. Then we examined the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Satan makes lawful the food of the people when they do not mention Allah's name over it, to establish what that making lawful is. We found halal is the released thing, and haram is the thing prohibited. Whoever does a prohibited thing thereby releases for himself what he did of that, so by his doing that he makes lawful, by releasing for himself what he released for it until he did it. From that is the saying of Allah Almighty in the verse in which al-nasi' is mentioned: They make it lawful one year and prohibit it one year to match the number Allah prohibited, so they make lawful what Allah prohibited - meaning to release for themselves what Allah Almighty prohibited to them of that. From it is people's saying: So-and-so made my blood lawful, and so-and-so made my wealth lawful - meaning he released for himself my blood and released for himself my wealth. Then after that we examined what is in this hadith of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Satan makes lawful the food of the people when they do not mention Allah's name over it. We found it was reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding naming over things when placing them, so that be a barrier to Satan from them."
"1224","1081 - As Yunus and al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, they said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us, he said: My father and Shu'ayb informed me, then they all agreed and said: From al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cover the vessel, tie the waterskin, close the door, and extinguish the lamp; for Satan does not untie a waterskin, nor open a door, nor uncover a vessel. If one of you finds nothing but to place a stick across his vessel and mention Allah's name over it, let him do so; for the little mouse sets fire to the people's house upon them."
"1225","1082 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from 'Ata', from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When night falls, restrain your children until an hour of the night passes, then let them go; for devils spread then. Close your doors and mention Allah Almighty's name; for Satan does not open a closed door. Tie your waterskins and mention Allah Almighty's name. Cover your vessels and mention Allah Almighty's name, even if you place a stick across it. He said: And 'Amr informed me, from Jabir, with something like this, but did not mention his saying: So mention Allah."
"1226","1083 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close the door, tie the waterskin, invert the vessel or cover the vessel, and extinguish the lamp; for Satan does not open a lock, nor untie a knot, nor uncover a vessel. The little mouse sets fire to people's houses. Abu Ja'far said: It is possible that the naming over food when placed by its placer, or when covered with what covers it, is the naming that bars Satan from it thereafter forever. We found it reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith which we reported at the beginning of this chapter, his saying: Satan makes lawful the food of the people when they do not mention Allah's name over it at their eating it. By that we understood that the naming at its covering or at its tying only preserves it while tied or covered, until its people attempt to eat it. When they attempt that, they need to name Allah. Then we sought: what should they do when naming escapes them, at their attempting to eat it? What should they do so Satan does not benefit by what he ate of it before that, and so it be a cause preventing him from its remainder?"
"1227","1084 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Budayl al-'Uqayli, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from Umm Kulthum, from 'A'isha, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was eating food among some of his companions or in his house. A Bedouin came and ate it in two mouthfuls. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had he mentioned Allah Almighty's name, it would have sufficed you. When one of you eats and forgets to mention Allah Almighty's name, then remembers, let him say: In the name of Allah, its beginning and its end. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is what he should say upon remembering, when he did not name Allah Almighty at the beginning of his eating. Then we found reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith what happens from Satan at that."
"1228","1085 - As Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya, meaning Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Subh, he said: al-Muthanna ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khuza'i narrated to me, he said: I accompanied him to Wasit. He would name at the beginning of his food, and at the last morsel say: In the name of Allah, its beginning and its end. He said: You name at the beginning of your food, then say at the end of your food: In the name of Allah Almighty, its beginning and its end? He said: Shall I inform you? My grandfather Umayya ibn Makhshi, who was among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - I heard him saying: A man was eating while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched, and did not name until the last morsel, when he said: In the name of Allah Almighty, its beginning and its end. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Satan kept eating with you until you named; nothing remained in his belly but he cast it out."
"1229","1086 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar al-Barra' narrated to us - Abu Ja'far said: he is Yusuf ibn Yazid - he said: Jabir ibn Subh narrated to us, he said: al-Muthanna ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khuza'i narrated to us, when al-Hajjaj died, from his grandfather, his mother's father, Umayya ibn Makhshi - and we accompanied each other for four months. When he placed his food he would name, and we ate, until when only one morsel remained of his lunch or dinner he said: In the name of Allah Almighty, its beginning and its end, until he ate it. I said: Why, O Abu 'Abdullah, did you name? When the last morsel remains you say: In the name of Allah Almighty, its beginning and its end? He said: I inform you. I heard my grandfather Umayya ibn Makhshi, who was among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: While we were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and a man was eating, when he finished the last morsel he named, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed or smiled. We asked him, and he said: He named Allah Almighty, its beginning and its end. By Him in whose hand is my soul, he - as if meaning Satan - kept eating with him until when he named, nothing remained in his belly but he cast it out. Abu Ja'far said: By that we established what befalls Satan by the eater's saying, who did not name at the beginning of his food, upon his noting that from himself: In the name of Allah, its beginning and its end. And with Allah is success."
"1230","Chapter 169: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the prayer he called khidaj - what is it? What is its ruling in that: is it its corruption and obligation to repeat, or other than that? 1087 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from his father 'Abbad, from 'A'isha, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every prayer in which Umm al-Qur'an is not recited is khidaj."
"1231","1088 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1232","1089 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Abu al-Sa'ib, freedman of Hisham ibn Zuhra, saying: I heard Abu Hurayra saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite Umm al-Qur'an, it is khidaj, it is khidaj, it is khidaj - incomplete."
"1233","1090 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, they said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1234","1091 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan informed us, from al-'Ala', from his father, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: We wanted to look into khidaj, what it is. We looked into that and found it deficiency in creation, and found it deficiency in the duration of pregnancy. It is said of one deficient in his creation or deficient in the duration of pregnancy with him: he is khidaj, and it is said thereby: he is mukhdaj. From it al-Dhu al-Thudayya was called al-Mukhdaj. Then we found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called another prayer khidaj for a meaning other than the meaning for which he called this prayer khidaj."
"1235","1092 - As 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Shu'ba, he said: I heard Ibn Sa'id, meaning 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, narrating, from Anas ibn Abi Anas of the people of Egypt, from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya', from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from al-Muttalib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Prayer is two by two; tashahhud in every two rak'as; humbling, neediness, raising your hands, saying: O Allah, O Allah! Whoever does not do that, it is khidaj."
"1236","1093 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from Anas ibn Abi Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya', from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'a, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1237","1094 - And as Abu Qurra Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya', from Rabi'a ibn al-Harith, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it, except that he said: Whoever does not do that, it is khidaj."
"1238","1095 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Nasr ibn Suwayd informed us, he said: 'Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, from Layth, he said: 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya', from Rabi'a ibn al-Harith, from al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it, except that he said: And you raise your hands - meaning: you lift them to your Lord Almighty, facing your face with their palms, saying: O Lord, O Lord! Whoever does not do that, such and such - meaning: it is khidaj."
"1239","1096 - And as Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la and Malik ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sayf al-Tujibi narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya', from Rabi'a ibn al-Harith, from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Qurra from 'Abdullah ibn Salih verbatim. Abu Ja'far said: Since this disagreement occurred in the chain of this hadith as we mentioned, and we found it revolving upon 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, then those who disagreed from him in it are Shu'ba, al-Layth, and Ibn Lahi'a. Shu'ba says in it: from Anas ibn Abi Anas, while al-Layth and Ibn Lahi'a say in it in place of that: from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas. It was reasonable in that that it is as al-Layth and Ibn Lahi'a said in it, not as Shu'ba said in it, because 'Imran ibn Abi Anas is a known man from whom hadiths other than this were reported, and because Anas ibn Abi Anas is not known - especially since some narrators of this hadith returned this Ibn Abi Anas to being from the people of Egypt. By that we understood that the people of Egypt know his lineage better than others. Then after that we found them differing about the man from whom 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya' reports: Shu'ba says he is 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, and the one 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith reports from is al-Muttalib. Al-Layth and Ibn Lahi'a say in place of that: from Rabi'a ibn al-Harith in place of 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith in Shu'ba's hadith, and from al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas in place of al-Muttalib in Shu'ba's hadith. We examined that and found Rabi'a ibn al-Harith is Rabi'a ibn al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim, kunya Abu Arwa. His death was in the caliphate of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in Medina. He was two years older than his uncle al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, and he has a son who reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1240","1097 - What Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn 'Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'a, who said: al-'Abbas came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) angry. He said: What is the matter with you, O uncle of the Messenger of Allah? He said: What is with us and Quraysh? He said: What is with you and them - good! He said: Some of us meet others with bright faces, but when they meet us they meet us with other than that. He said: He became angry until a vein between his eyes filled. When it cleared from him he said: By Him in whose hand is Muhammad's soul, faith does not enter a man's heart until he loves you for Allah and His Messenger. Then he said: What is with men who harm me regarding al-'Abbas? A man's uncle is the like of his father! Abu Ja'far said: This al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'a is the narrator of the hadith of charities which:"
"1241","1098 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: Juwayriya ibn Asma' narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Nawfal ibn al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib narrated to him that 'Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'a ibn al-Harith narrated to him, he said: Rabi'a ibn al-Harith and al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib gathered and said: If only we sent these two boys - mine and al-Fadl ibn al-'Abbas - for charity collection, they would render what people render and attain what people attain. Then he mentioned the hadith. We needed to mention this from it to establish who al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'a is. In this hadith his mention was as 'Abd al-Muttalib, while in the hadith of Yazid ibn Sinan his mention was as al-Muttalib. It is as if he was named 'Abd al-Muttalib in the Jahiliyya, then returned in Islam to al-Muttalib. Abu Ja'far said: By that we understood that it is impossible that 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya' met Rabi'a ibn al-Harith, while it was conceivable that he met 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith. It was impossible that Rabi'a ibn al-Harith reports from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, whose age is above his father's age. So the correct in what Shu'ba, al-Layth, and Ibn Lahi'a differed in the chain of this hadith, in what is after 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-'Amya', is as Shu'ba said in it - and Allah knows best. In this hadith and in the hadith before it which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter is the description of those two prayers as khidaj. Some people said: Whoever prays and does not recite the Fatiha of the Book in every rak'a of it, it does not suffice him. They made the deficiency that entered it, until it became khidaj, invalidate it. A group opposed them in that, among them Abu Hanifa and his companions. They made it valid but deficient, by its prayer's abandoning the Fatiha of the Book in it. They went to the view that khidaj does not remove the thing named by it; it only diminishes it. The prayer we mentioned, when its deficiency became necessary, was not nonexistent but existent and deficient. Not everyone whose prayer is diminished by his abandoning something in it must have it invalidated. We saw him, by abandoning completing its bowing and completing its prostration, diminish it without it being invalid requiring repetition. It is not denied that by abandoning reciting the Fatiha of the Book in it, it is deficient with a deficiency that does not require its repetition. We found from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that, and it is what:"
"1242","1099 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. And what Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Bakkar narrated to us. And what Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. They all said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Arqam ibn Shurahbil, who said: I traveled with Ibn 'Abbas from Medina to Sham. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he fell ill with the illness in which he died, was in the house of 'A'isha. He said: Call 'Ali for me! She said: Shall we not call Abu Bakr for you? He said: Call him. Hafsa said: Shall we not call 'Umar for you? He said: Call him. Umm al-Fadl said: Shall we not call al-'Abbas, your uncle, for you? He said: Call him. When they were present he raised his head, then said: Let Abu Bakr lead the people in prayer. Abu Bakr stepped forward leading the people in prayer. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found lightness in himself and came out supported between two men. When Abu Bakr perceived him they glorified Allah, and Abu Bakr went to step back. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him: Your place. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed from where Abu Bakr had reached of the recitation, with Abu Bakr standing and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting. Abu Bakr followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people followed Abu Bakr. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed from where Abu Bakr had reached of the recitation. That is not free of one of two aspects: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the recitation while Abu Bakr had recited the Fatiha of the Book or recited part of it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not recite the Fatiha of the Book nor anything of it, and that prayer of his sufficed him thereby. In that was evidence that abandoning reciting the Fatiha of the Book or part of it does not invalidate prayer, as those who say that say. Correcting this hadith and the first hadith without differing is that reciting the Fatiha of the Book in prayer should not be abandoned, but abandoning it does not invalidate, as others said, so the two hadiths agree and do not differ. Then we found the people of the first view, who invalidate prayer by abandoning reciting the Fatiha of the Book, equate therein the imam and the follower together. We found them all agreeing about one who enters the imam's prayer while he is bowing: he says takbir for entering it, then says takbir for bowing and bows, without reciting the Fatiha of the Book for fear the rak'a escape him if he recites it - that he counts that rak'a. That indicates that prayer may suffice without reciting the Fatiha of the Book. If they say: That was only due to necessity to that, their opponent says to them: Does necessity drop an obligation? We found this entrant in this prayer, under this necessity, if he bowed without standing before it a standing - his prayer does not suffice him; he must have a standing before bowing for it, however short. Had reciting the Fatiha of the Book been likewise, he must recite it, and necessity would not repel from him its obligation, just as it did not repel from him the obligation of the standing we mentioned. In that is evidence of what we described. And with Allah is success."
"1243","Chapter 170: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported about the virtue of congregational prayer over the prayer of the lone person. 1100 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi', from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Congregational prayer exceeds the prayer of the lone person by twenty-seven degrees."
"1244","1101 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1245","1102 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Congregational prayer is better than the prayer of one of you alone by twenty-five parts. Abu Ja'far said: Someone said: These two hadiths contradict one another, because in one of them the congregational prayer exceeds the lone prayer by twenty-seven degrees, while in the other it exceeds it by twenty-five parts. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is there is no contradiction in them; it is possible that what Allah Almighty first made of virtue for congregational prayer over the lone prayer was twenty-five degrees, as in the hadith of Abu Hurayra of the two, then Allah Almighty increased in its virtue over the lone prayer two further parts, as in the hadith of Ibn 'Umar. That was an increase, not a contradiction. And with Allah is success."
"1246","Chapter 171: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: A man prays prayer, and nothing is written for him of it but its tenth, or what else he mentioned of its parts. 1103 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Rushdin narrated to us, from Haywa ibn Shurayh, from Ibn 'Ajlan, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Anama al-Muzani, from 'Ammar ibn Yasir, that he prayed a prayer and lightened it. He said to him: You prayed a prayer and lightened it? He said: Did you see me diminish anything of its limits? I said: No. 'Ammar said: I hastened against the whispering of Satan; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A servant turns from his prayer, and nothing is written for him of it but its tenth, or its ninth, or its eighth, or its seventh, or its sixth, or its fifth, or its quarter, or its third, or its half."
"1247","1104 - Muhammad also narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Marzuq al-Ka'bi narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, from Ibn al-'Ajlan, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Anama, from 'Ammar ibn Yasir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1248","1105 - Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-'Ajlan narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Anama al-Muzani, that he said: I saw 'Ammar ibn Yasir enter the mosque and pray a prayer he lightened. I came to him and said to him: You lightened it, O Abu al-Yaqzan? He said: Did you see me diminish anything of its limits? I said: No. He said: I hastened with it against Satan's desire; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A man prays prayer, and nothing is written for him but its tenth, its ninth, its eighth, its seventh, its sixth, its fifth, its quarter, its third, its half."
"1249","1106 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from 'Amr, and he is Ibn al-Harith, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam al-Ansari, from Abu al-Yasar, companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among you is one who prays prayer complete, and among you is one who prays the half, the third, the quarter, the fifth, until he reached the tenth. Abu Ja'far said: Abu al-Yasar is Ka'b ibn 'Amr."
"1250","1107 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr said: Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal said: 'Umar ibn al-Hakam al-Ansari narrated to me, from Abu al-Yasar, companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Someone asked about the meaning of this hadith. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, after our examining it, is that what is intended thereby in our view - and Allah knows best - is that when a man prays prayer as he was commanded to pray it - completing its standing, bowing, prostration, sitting, recitation in it, remembrance of Allah Almighty commanded in it, humility in it, attention to it, and leaving distraction from it by anything else calling him to fall short of completing it - Allah Almighty gives him upon that what He wills to give him upon it, for his striving in it upon what was from him in it. When he falls short of what we mentioned in it, a falling short that does not take him out of it, but by it he diminishes of it what he ought not diminish of it, of remembrance and of its like of making it difficult upon what he brought of it - by the measure of what He would give him had he brought it complete, upon what he is commanded in it, of the reward He gives him upon that, of its small parts and its large. And Allah knows best what His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended in that. And Him we ask for success."
"1251","Chapter 172: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Muslims cutting the palms of Banu al-Nadir and burning them, and about the reason for which was revealed: Whatever you cut of palm trees or left standing upon their roots. 1108 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Musa ibn 'Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut the palms of Banu al-Nadir and burned."
"1252","1109 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me, from Nafi', from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) burned the palms of Banu al-Nadir and cut, and it is al-Buwayra. About it Hassan ibn Thabit says his verse. Allah Almighty said: Whatever you cut of palm trees or left standing upon their roots, it is by Allah's permission, and to disgrace the transgressors."
"1253","1110 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Juwayriya ibn Asma' narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) burned the palms of Banu al-Nadir, and about it Hassan ibn Thabit says his verse, and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith answered him. Abu Ja'far said: Someone said: In the hadith of Yunus which you reported of these hadiths is what indicates that the revelation of His saying Almighty: Whatever you cut of palm trees - the verse - was only after there was from them of cutting and burning what was. This indicates this hadith is impossible, because Allah Almighty does not send down upon His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything except what benefits His community thereby - meaning for them to apply it in His obligations upon them and in His worship of them. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is this hadith did not encompass the reason for which this verse was revealed, and there was from the Muslims before its revelation what was, in whose revelation in it upon them is the greatest benefit. We found it only in a hadith reported from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas."
"1254","1111 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb ibn 'Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us, from 'Affan, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn Abi 'Amra narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, about the saying of Allah Almighty: Whatever you cut of palm trees or left standing upon their roots. He said: Al-lina is palms. And to disgrace the transgressors, he said: They brought them down from their fortresses, and they were commanded to cut the palms. It rankled in their chests, and the Muslims said: We cut some and left some; we shall ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Is there for us in what we cut any reward? And what is upon us in what we left of sin? So Allah Almighty revealed: Whatever you cut of palm trees or left standing upon their roots - the verse. al-Hasan ibn Muhammad said: 'Affan used to narrate to us this hadith from 'Abd al-Wahid, from Habib, then returned and narrated it to us from Hafs. Abu Ja'far said: By that we understood that Allah Almighty sent down this verse upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so the Muslims know that what was of their cutting what they cut of the palms of Banu al-Nadir and burning them is permissible for them, with no sin upon them in it, and what they left of them without cutting or burning is permissible for them, with no sin upon them in it. Thereby appeared the place of benefit in the revelation of this verse. Another speaker said: There was reported from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, in what he instructed the commanders of the armies when he directed them to Sham, what indicates contrary to what is in these hadiths from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas."
"1255","And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, when he sent the commanders of the troops toward Sham - Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, 'Amr ibn al-'As, and Shurahbil ibn Hasana - said: I enjoin upon you fear of Allah Almighty. Raid in Allah's path. Fight whoever disbelieves in Allah; Allah will aid His religion. Do not misappropriate, nor betray, nor be cowardly, nor spread corruption in the land. Do not drown palms nor burn them. Do not hamstring an animal nor a fruit-bearing tree, nor demolish a church. This speaker said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had read this verse, and the commanders to whom he instructed what he instructed in this hadith had read it. That was in the presence of others besides them of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who also read this verse. In that was what indicates this verse was not revealed in the meaning mentioned in the two hadiths of Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas, that its revelation was in it. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is what is in those two hadiths of the reason for which this verse was revealed is as in them, and what is in this hadith of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, does not contradict that. He was upon knowledge of Sham's return to their hands, of their conquering it, and of their overcoming the Romans upon it, by what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had informed them of that."
"1256","1112 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Yemen will be conquered, and a people will come driving, carrying with their families and whoever obeys them, while Medina is better for them had they known. Iraq will be conquered, and a people will come driving, carrying with their families and whoever obeys them, while Medina is better for them had they known. Sham will be conquered, and a people will come driving, carrying with their families and whoever obeys them, while Medina is better for them had they known."
"1257","1113 - And as Abu Umayya narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, then he mentioned this hadith. Except that he said: Then Iraq will be conquered. And added: 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr said: Then it reached me that Sufyan was at the season (hajj), so I came to him and asked him about this hadith. He said: I bear witness I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he repeated it from him as he narrated to me."
"1258","1114 - And as Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn 'Alqama narrated to us, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from 'Abdullah ibn Hawala, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and complained to him of poverty, nakedness, and lack of things. He said: Rejoice! By Allah, I fear for you abundance of things more than its lack. By Allah, this affair will remain among you until there is opened to you the land of Persia and Rome and the land of Himyar, and until you are three armies: an army in Sham, an army in Iraq, and an army in Yemen. Until a man is given a hundred dinars and dislikes it. Ibn Hawala said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, who can manage Sham with the Romans of horned might in it? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, Allah will make you successors in it until a group among them, white their shirts, shaven their napes, stand over the black man among you, the shaven. Today there are men in it in whose eyes you are more contemptible than ticks on camels' rumps. Ibn Hawala said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, choose for me if I reach that! He said: I choose Sham for you; it is Allah's choicest of His lands, and Allah gathers His choicest of His servants to the people of Islam. Upon you is Sham; the choicest of Allah from the earth is Sham. Whoever refuses, let him drink from the ponds of Yemen; Allah has guaranteed for me Sham and its people. I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr saying: The companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recognized the description of this hadith in Juz' ibn Suhayl al-Sulami, who was in charge of the non-Arabs. He was short and dark; they would pass while those non-Arabs stood, doing whatever he commanded them, marveling at this hadith. Abu Ja'far said: Abu Bakr's, may Allah be pleased with him, commanding the army commanders with what he commanded them in his hadith which we reported was for this meaning in these hadiths, and for what he urged them of prayer at Iliya', and of binding mounts to it from what our mention preceded in this book of ours, and for what was reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: And Sham withheld its mudd and dinar - meaning it will withhold its mudd and dinar due in its land, and that is only after their conquering it and overcoming upon it. We shall mention this hadith later in this book of ours, Allah willing. And Allah we ask for success."
"1259","Chapter 173: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about men standing for one another. 1115 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurra ibn Abi Khalifa narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salama ibn Salama al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said: And 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b informed me that 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b said: I heard Ka'b ibn Malik narrating the hadith of his repentance. He said: I set out making for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). People met me group after group congratulating me on repentance, saying: May Allah Almighty's repentance upon you delight you. Until I entered the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting in the mosque with people around him. Talha ibn 'Ubaydullah stood to me hurrying until he shook my hand and congratulated me. By Allah, no man of the Muhajirin stood besides him. Ka'b never forgot it for Talha."
"1260","1116 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: 'Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1261","1117 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b - and he was his father's guide when he became blind - he said: I asked Ka'b about his hadith when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the campaign of Tabuk, then he mentioned this hadith."
"1262","1118 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, from his grandfather Ka'b, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when my repentance was revealed. Talha ibn 'Ubaydullah met me hurrying, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"1263","1119 - 'Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, and he mentioned the like of it."
"1264","1120 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqama narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said: When Sa'd ibn Mu'adh appeared before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Banu Qurayza had come down upon his judgment, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand to your chief - or to your best."
"1265","1121 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Dinar al-Tammar and Ma'n ibn 'Isa and 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Salih, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from 'Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, that Sa'd ibn Mu'adh entered the mosque after he judged concerning Banu Qurayza what he judged in them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand to your chief."
"1266","1122 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn 'Isa al-Madini narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Hilal, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to enter his house, we would stand."
"1267","1123 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslama al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hilal narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1268","1124 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hilal narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, who said: We would sit with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mornings. When he stood to his house, we remained standing until he entered his house. Someone said: How do you accept these hadiths when you report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts them?"
"1269","1125 - He mentioned what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: al-Mughira ibn Muslim narrated to me, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Burayda narrated to us, he said: I heard Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves that men stand for him, the Fire is due for him. Abu Ja'far said: This al-Mughira is al-Qasmali, also called al-Sarraj, one of the firm ones. 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali is his brother, and al-Mughira is above him. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is this hadith in our view does not contradict the first hadiths we reported in this chapter. The first hadiths we reported in this chapter contain the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting men standing for one another by the standers' choice of that, without mention of love by those stood for of it from them. In this hadith you mentioned is love by the one stood for of that from those who stand for him. Correcting these two meanings is that the first hadiths be upon what has no love in it for the one stood for, and this hadith be upon love by the one stood for of that standing. By what we mentioned it appeared that each kind of these two kinds bears what we carried it upon from what we mentioned. No contradiction of one kind of these two kinds to the other appeared, by Allah's praise and favor."
"1270","1126 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, who said: No person was more beloved to them than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When they saw him they would not stand for him, due to what they knew of his dislike of that from them. Abu Ja'far said: What is in this hadith indicates that the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only left standing for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) due to their knowledge of his dislike of that from them. In that is what indicates that had it not been his dislike of that from them, they would have stood for him. His dislike of that from them may be by way of humility from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not because it is unlawful for them to do that for him. How is it thought unlawful for them when he commanded them to stand to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, and Talha ibn 'Ubaydullah stood in his presence to Ka'b ibn Malik upon the revelation of his repentance, congratulating him thereby, and he did not forbid him from it?"
"1271","1127 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubada narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn al-Shahid narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: Mu'awiya entered a house in which were 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr and 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir. Ibn 'Amir stood while Ibn al-Zubayr remained seated - and he was the weightier of the two. Mu'awiya said: Sit, O Ibn 'Amir; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever loves that men stand before him, let him take his seat in the Fire. That indicates what is disliked of what we mentioned is love by some men of that from others. That love may be with standing to them, or without standing to them. That indicates dislike in that is only for the love we mentioned, not for standing with which there is no love. Some who profess language claimed the hadith of Mu'awiya reported from him by Ibn Burayda is: Whoever loves that men stand long for him - upon the standing the non-Arabs do for their great ones, of standing over their heads and prolonging that until they stand long with him, meaning until their smells change for him due to their prolonging that standing. Abu Ja'far said: This in our view is impossible, because the hadith reported in that revolves only upon Mu'awiya, with no outlet besides him. In it was his addressing 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir with what was, without prolonging by Ibn 'Amir for him therein a standing. That indicates the negation of this interpretation; in its negation is the affirmation of the first interpretation."
"1272","Chapter 174: Explanation of the difficulty of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by cursing the wasila and mustawsila. 1128 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the wasila and mawsula."
"1273","1129 - Muhammad ibn 'Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salama ibn Rawh narrated to us, from 'Uqayl, he said: Aban ibn Salih narrated to me that al-Hasan ibn Muslim narrated to him that Safiyya bint Shayba ibn 'Uthman narrated to him that a woman asked 'A'isha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about a woman joining her head with hair. 'A'isha said: Allah's mercy upon the women of the Muhajirat and Ansar! How intense was their understanding in their religion, and how eager for their Hereafter. When this verse was revealed: And let them draw their veils over their bosoms - they took to the edges of their garments and tore veils from them. Then 'A'isha refused to inform her about what she asked her. Then 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I married my daughter to a man; she complained, her hair fell out, and her husband wants to take her to him. Shall I place upon her head something to beautify her with? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah cursed the wasila and mustawsila."
"1274","1130 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from Fatima bint al-Mundhir, from Asma' bint Abi Bakr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the wasila and mustawsila."
"1275","1131 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from Fatima bint al-Mundhir, from Asma', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1276","1132 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Fatima bint al-Mundhir, from her grandmother Asma', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1277","1133 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a woman join anything to her head. Abu Ja'far said: Then we found all the people of knowledge after the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting joining hair with other than hair, of wool and its like. They report in that from those before them what:"
"1278","Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn 'Abdullah al-Nakha'i narrated to us, from Jabir - and he is al-Ju'fi - from Shu'ba, freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: No harm that a woman join her hair with wool."
"1279","And what Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Bukayr, from his mother, that she entered upon 'A'isha while she was a bride, with her hairdresser with her. 'A'isha said: Is this her hair? The hairdresser said: Her hair and other - I joined it with wool. Umm Bukayr said: I did not hear her deny that. Bukayr said: Only joining with hair is disliked. Abu Ja'far said: 'A'isha is one of those we reported from in this chapter the cursing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the wasila and mustawsila. It would not exclude from that except what she knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not intend by his cursing that, or that he intended it then excluded it from it. The trustworthy people of knowledge would not exclude from a hadith they reported, open to interpretation from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), anything whose apparent meaning requires its entry in it, except after their knowledge of its exclusion from it. Had it not been so, their probity would fall, and in the fall of their probity is the fall of their narrations. Far be Allah Almighty that they be so. And Allah we seek for success and ask for right guidance."
"1280","Chapter 175: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The heaven creaked, and it is right for it to creak! In one of the two hadiths reported in that: There is no place of a foot in it. And in the other of them: There is no place of four fingers in it but in it is an angel prostrating. 1134 - Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani and Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, they said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id - and he is Ibn Abi 'Aruba - informed us, from Qatada, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, that Hakim ibn Hizam said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with his companions, he said to them: Do you hear what I hear? They said: We hear nothing, O Messenger of Allah! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hear the creaking of the heaven, and it is not blamed for creaking. There is no place of a foot in it but upon it is an angel, either prostrating or standing."
"1281","1135 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Mawraq al-'Ijli, from Abu Dharr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The heaven creaked, and it is right for it to creak! There is no place of four fingers in it but in it is an angel prostrating. By Allah, had you known what I know, you would laugh little and weep much, and go out to the open roads crying out to Allah. Someone said: Do you conceive that in the place of a foot, or in the place of four fingers, there is an angel prostrating or bowing? Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is this speech is Arabic speech understood by those addressed, who grasp what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by it. The Arabs say: So-and-so is sitting upon such for what is less than him, and so-and-so is sitting upon such for what exceeds him. That exists in people's speech: they say so-and-so is sitting upon the mat, while it is short of him, while his sitting in reality is upon it and upon other than it of the earth and what else. They say: So-and-so is sitting upon the mat that exceeds him, while the reality is his sitting is upon part of it, not all of it. Since that is so, like it is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these two hadiths: There is no place of a foot in it. Or: There is no place of four fingers in it but upon it is an angel either prostrating or bowing - upon the meaning: but in it is an angel prostrating, or: but upon it is an angel bowing or prostrating - upon that his being upon it in reality is being upon it and upon other than it, as sitting upon the mat short for the sitter is sitting upon it and upon what else. And Allah we ask for success."
"1282","Chapter 176: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his correcting al-Bara' ibn 'Azib when he asked him about what he says when he goes to his bed, of his mentioning that he says in it: 'And Your Messenger whom You sent' - by his saying: 'And Your Prophet whom You sent.' 1136 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus, known as al-Susi, narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Muhammad al-'Anqazi narrated to me, from Fitr ibn Khalifa, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Bara', what do you say when you go to your bed? I said: Allah Almighty and His Messenger know best. He said: When you go to your bed pure, pillow your right side and say: O Allah, I submitted my face to You, entrusted my affair to You, and resorted my back to You, in fear and hope of You. There is no refuge nor escape from You except to You! I believed in Your Book which You sent down, and Your Prophet whom You sent. I said as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me, except that I said: And Your Messenger whom You sent. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poked with his finger in my chest and said to me: And Your Prophet whom You sent - so I did it."
"1283","1137 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubayda, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you come to your bed, perform ablution as for prayer, and let the last of what you say be: O Allah, I submitted my face to You, entrusted my affair to You, and resorted my back to You, in hope and fear of You. There is no refuge nor escape from You except to You. I believed in Your Book which You sent down, and Your Prophet whom You sent. If you die, you die upon the fitra."
"1284","1138 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Shu'ba ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Ward al-'Ataki narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confided to a man, saying: When you want to sleep, say - then he mentioned the like of it."
"1285","1139 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq informed me, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded a man to say when he goes to his bed, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith of Abu Umayya from Muhammad ibn Sabiq."
"1286","1140 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murra, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubayda, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. Someone asked about the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) corrected al-Bara' - his saying: And Your Messenger whom You sent - and his commanding him to say in place of that: And Your Prophet whom You sent - what it is. Our answer to him in that, and with Allah Almighty is success and help, is that his saying: And Your Messenger whom You sent - contains only messengership specifically, while what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) corrected upon him and commanded him to say in place of that, and it is: And Your Prophet whom You sent - combines messengership and prophethood together. It was preferable to what is upon messengership without prophethood. And Allah we ask for success!"
"1287","Chapter 177: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his commanding the wife of al-Nahham not to apply kohl to her daughter in her waiting period from her husband's death, after she informed him of her fear for her eye if she does not do that. 1141 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Umm Salama, from Umm Salama, that a woman whose husband died and whose eye became inflamed, and they feared for her eye. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought his permission for kohl. They mentioned they feared for her eye, and he said: No. One of you used to remain in the worst of her house in her worn garments - or in her worn garments in the worst of her house. When a year passed and a dog passed, she threw a dropping. So no - four months and ten days."
"1288","1142 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Umm Salama, that the daughter of al-Nahham's husband died, and her mother came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My daughter complains of her eye; shall I apply kohl to her? I fear her eye will burst? He said: Even if it bursts - meaning: do not do so. One of you used to remain after her husband's death a year, then throw from behind her a dropping."
"1289","1143 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Abi Salama, who said: I heard Umm Salama saying: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my daughter's husband died, and she complained of her eye - shall we apply kohl to her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No - twice or thrice. Each time saying: No. Then he said: It is only four and ten. In the Jahiliyya one of you would throw the dropping at the head of the year."
"1290","1144 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Umm Salama, from Umm Salama and Umm Habiba, that a woman said: O Messenger of Allah, my daughter's husband died, and I feared for her sight - shall I apply kohl to her? He said: One of you used to throw the dropping at the head of the year. It is only four months and ten days."
"1291","1145 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from ... then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1292","1146 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Habib ibn 'Arabi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya, meaning Ibn Sa'id, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab, that a woman asked Umm Salama and Umm Habiba: Should she apply kohl in her waiting period from her husband's death? They said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about that, then he mentioned the rest of this hadith."
"1293","1147 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyayna narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Humayd ibn Nafi', from Zaynab bint Umm Salama, from Umm Salama, that a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ... then he mentioned the rest of this hadith."
"1294","1148 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, he said: Humayd said: And Zaynab bint Umm Salama narrated to me, from her mother Umm Salama, that she said: A woman of Quraysh, daughter of al-Nahham, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the rest of this hadith. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the woman in waiting from her husband's death to apply kohl to her eye in her waiting period, despite her fear for her eye if she does not do that with them. Someone said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when all the people of knowledge are contrary to it, permitting kohl for one like her due to the necessity calling her to that? Our answer to him in that, and with Allah is success, is this hadith came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successively through these sound avenues which the scholars accepted. In their abandoning what is in it, after its reaching them and their applying its contrary, is what indicates its abrogation, because they are trusted with its abrogation just as they are trusted with what they reported. Since they were so, their abandoning what they reported of these praiseworthy avenues in their view was upon that they abandoned it for what requires them to abandon it, and they went to what is preferable for them of it from what abrogated it. Had it not been so, their probity would have fallen; in the fall of their probity is the fall of their narrations. Far be Allah Almighty that the reality of their affairs be so. Rather it was for what we reported, upon what we described. Then we sought: do we find in the reports what indicates anything of that?"
"1295","1149 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhrama ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, he said: I heard al-Mughira ibn al-Dahhak saying: Umm Hakim bint Asid informed me, from her mother, that her husband died and she complained, applying kohl of jila'. She sent a freedwoman of hers to Umm Salama and asked her about the kohl of jila'. She said: Do not apply kohl except due to a matter you must - it becomes severe upon you - so you apply kohl at night and wipe it in the day. Then at that Umm Salama said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me when Abu Salama died, while I had placed sabir (aloes) upon my eye. He said: What is this, O Umm Salama? I said: O Messenger of Allah, it is only sabir with no perfume in it. He said: It brightens the face. Do not place it except at night, removing it in the day. Do not comb with perfume nor henna, for it is dye. I said: With what shall I comb, O Messenger of Allah? He said: With sidr (lote); you lather your head with it. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith of Umm Salama's saying to the woman who asked her about what she asked her in this hadith: Do not do that except for what you must - though she heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts - it is impossible that was from her except that she knew of its abrogation from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because she, may Allah be pleased with her, is trusted in what she said, just as she was trusted in what she reported. And Allah we ask for success."
"1296","Chapter 178: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his names. 1150 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have five names: I am Muhammad, I am Ahmad, I am al-Mahi by whom Allah Almighty effaces disbelief, I am al-Hashir at whose feet Allah gathers people, and I am al-'Aqib - and al-'Aqib is he after whom there is none. And Allah Almighty named him Ra'uf Rahim (Compassionate, Merciful). Abu Ja'far said: What is in this hadith of Allah Almighty's naming him Ra'uf Rahim is either from the speech of Jubayr or from the speech of other than him of its narrators."
"1297","1151 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Khalid - and he is Ibn Yazid - from Ibn Abi Hilal - and he is Sa'id - from 'Utba ibn Muslim, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, that he entered upon 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan and he said to him: Do you enumerate the names of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which Jubayr ibn Mut'im used to count? He said: Yes. They are six: Muhammad, Ahmad, Khatam, Hashir, 'Aqib, and Mahi. As for al-Hashir, he was sent with the Hour as a warner to you before severe punishment. As for 'Aqib, he followed the prophets, blessings of Allah upon them. As for Mahi, Allah Almighty effaced thereby the evils of whoever followed him. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is an additional name over the names mentioned in the hadith we mentioned before it, and it is Khatam."
"1298","1152 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murra, from Abu 'Ubayda, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named himself to us with names, saying: I am Muhammad and Ahmad and al-Muqaffi and al-Hashir and the Prophet of repentance and the Prophet of battle. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith of his names is al-Muqaffi, whose meaning is the meaning of al-'Aqib mentioned in the two hadiths we reported before it. In it of his names are two other names besides the names mentioned in them, and they are: Prophet of repentance, and Prophet of battle. Someone asked about the meaning by which some of what is in these hadiths exceeds what else of them. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help: Names are only designations for things intended to distinguish them and separate some from others. Names divide into two kinds: a kind in which names are without cause - like stone, like mountain, and like else of what was not named for a meaning in it. And among them what is named for a meaning in it of its attributes - like Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from praise, and like Ahmad from praise also. These two names were of his names (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), two names Allah Almighty mentioned in His Book, saying: Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and those with him are stern against disbelievers. And He said in what 'Isa son of Maryam, peace be upon him, addressed his people: I am Allah's Messenger to you, confirming what is before me of the Torah, and giving glad tidings of a messenger coming after me whose name is Ahmad. These two names were of his attributes (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). By that we established it is permissible to be named by his attributes besides praise, as he was named by praise which is of his attributes. So he was named al-Mahi, because Allah Almighty effaces disbelief by him. He was named al-Hashir, because people are gathered at his feet. He was named al-'Aqib, because he followed those before him of the prophets, blessings of Allah upon them. He was named Khatam, because he is the seal of the prophets. Allah Almighty mentioned that in His Book, saying: Muhammad was not the father of any of your men, but the Messenger of Allah and the seal of the prophets. He was named al-Muqaffi, because he followed those before him of the prophets. He was named Prophet of repentance, because Allah Almighty turned in repentance by him upon whoever repented of His servants. He mentioned that in His Book in His saying Almighty: Allah turned upon the Prophet and the Muhajirin and Ansar who followed him in the hour of hardship, after the hearts of a party of them nearly deviated, then He turned upon them. He was named Prophet of battle, because he is the cause of fighting, which is battle. All these names are derived from his attributes (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In the hadith of Muhammad ibn Jubayr: And Allah Almighty named him Ra'uf Rahim - deriving thereby from the saying of Allah Almighty: There came to you a messenger from among yourselves, grievous to him what you suffer, eager for you, to the believers compassionate, merciful. That indicates it is permissible to be named by all his attributes, and what he was named of that joins his names with which he was named before that - as joined the names of 'Ali, peace be upon him, the name the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him when he became dusty with dust, by his saying to him: Rise, O Abu Turab. Sahl ibn Sa'd said: No name was more beloved to him than it. We shall mention that hadith and what enters its meaning in its place in this book of ours, Allah willing. It was permissible to mention some of his names, and aiming at some of them is no proof he has no names besides them. Upon this meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - came these reports upon what they came with of what is in them. And Allah we ask for success."
"1299","Chapter 179: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the two Eids gathering in one day. 1153 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr al-Kirmani narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn al-Mughira, he said: I heard Iyas ibn Abi Ramla, he said: I heard Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan asking Zayd ibn Arqam, saying: Did you witness with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two Eids gathering in one day? He said: Yes. He said: How did he do? He said: He prayed, then gave concession in the Friday prayer, saying: Whoever wishes to pray, let him pray. Abu Ja'far said: This 'Uthman is the cousin of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf."
"1300","1154 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, author of al-Tayalisa, narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Mughira ibn Abi Zur'a of the family of Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Abi Ramla al-Shami, who said: I witnessed Mu'awiya asking Zayd ibn Arqam: Did you witness two Eids with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathering? He said: Yes. He said: What did he do? He said: He prayed the Eid, and gave concession in the Friday prayer: whoever wishes to sit, let him sit. Someone asked about what is intended by what is in these two hadiths, after deeming great what is in them of concession in leaving the Friday prayer, denying that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: How can anyone stay back from the Friday prayer with the saying of Allah Almighty: O you who believed, when called for prayer on Friday, hasten to Allah's remembrance - the verse? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help: Those intended by the concession in leaving the Friday prayer in these two hadiths are the people of al-'Awali, whose dwellings are outside Medina, for whom Friday is not obligatory, because they are not in a city of the cities. Friday is only obligatory upon the people of the cities. In the cities, apart from else, as was reported from 'Ali, peace be upon him, in that, of which we know with certainty he did not say it by opinion, since the like of it is not said by opinion, and he said it only by instruction - and no instruction is found in that except from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1301","And it is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Iyami, he said: I heard Sa'd ibn 'Ubayda, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Ali, peace be upon him, who said: No Friday and no tashriq except in a city of the cities."
"1302","Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubayda, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Ali, who said: No Friday and no tashriq except in a congregational city. Abu Ja'far said: The people of al-'Awali who are not in a city of the cities may stay back from Fridays. Whoever may stay back from Fridays may stay back from congregations besides them, in Eid prayers and what else. When they attend the cities for Eid prayers, they are thereby in a place upon whose people attending that prayer is due - meaning the Friday prayer - and what else of Eid prayers. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them in these two hadiths that they need not remain in their place which they attended for the Eid prayer until the Friday time enters upon them while therein, so Friday becomes obligatory upon them as upon the people of that place, because it is a city of the cities. He gave them to remain in it by choice until they pray the Friday in it, or turn from it to their places, leaving remaining for the Friday. Their return to their places is a return to places upon whose people there is no Friday. He said: You also reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning a hadith more astonishing than this."
"1303","1155 - Meaning what Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn 'Abd Rabbihi al-Jurjusi narrated to us, he said: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Mughira, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, who said: Two Eids gathered in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one day. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever you wish suffices you. He said: In this hadith is his referring the choice to them in coming to the Eid prayer and leaving coming to other than it of the Friday prayer, or coming to the Friday and leaving what is before it of the Eid prayer. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed them with that before the Eid day, for them to do it on the Eid day. He informed thereby the people of al-'Awali that they may stay back from the Eid prayer and attend for the Friday prayer, or attend for the Eid prayer and pray it then turn to their places, not attending the Friday, since the people of those places have no Friday upon them, because they are not in a city of the cities. This hadith was reported with wordings more indicative of this meaning than the hadith of Muhammad ibn 'Ali which we mentioned."
"1304","1156 - As Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Abu 'Amir narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rufay', from Dhakwan, who said: Two Eids gathered in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: You attained good and remembrance. We shall gather; whoever wishes to gather, let him gather, and whoever wishes to return, let him return. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the uncovering of the meaning we mentioned the first hadith bears. There was reported from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, that he commanded the people of al-'Awali with the like of that on a day in which two Eids gathered in his days."
"1305","As Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubada narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed us, from Abu 'Ubayd, freedman of Ibn Azhar, who said: I witnessed the Eid with 'Uthman on Friday. He came, prayed, then turned and preached, saying: Two Eids gathered for you in this day of yours. Whoever of the people of al-'Aliya loves to wait for the Friday, let him wait for it. Whoever loves to return, let him return - I permitted him. And as:"
"1306","Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Ubayd the freedman of Ibn Azhar, who said: I attended the Eid with Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and it coincided with Friday, so he began with the prayer before the sermon, then said: This is a day in which two Eids have gathered for you; whoever is here from the people of al-Awali, we have permitted him, and whoever loves to remain, let him remain. And in what we have mentioned is a clarification of what we described before regarding the possible meaning of what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. And we ask Allah for success."
"1307","Chapter 180: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the reason for which Abdullah ibn Masud said: There was only four years between our Islam and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, reproaching us with His saying: {Has the time not come for those who believed that their hearts should become humble for the remembrance of Allah} - the verse. Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Awn ibn Abdullah, meaning Ibn Utbah ibn Masud, from his father, that Ibn Masud said: There was only four years between our Islam and Allah reproaching us with this verse: {Has the time not come for those who believed that their hearts should become humble for the remembrance of Allah}."
"1308","Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, and Yazid ibn Sinan also narrated to us, each one of them said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai informed us, he said: Abu Hazim narrated to me, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he informed him from his father, that Abdullah ibn Masud informed him that he said: There was only four years between their Islam and the revelation of this verse by which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, reproached them: {And let them not be like those who were given the Book before, and the term was prolonged for them and their hearts hardened, and many of them are defiantly disobedient}. Abu Jafar said: So we sought the reason for which they were reproached with what is in this verse."
"1309","1157 - So we found Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi had narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Khallad al-Saffar narrated to us, from Amr ibn Qays al-Mulai, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Musab ibn Sad, from Sad, concerning the saying of Allah, Majestic and Mighty: {We relate to you the best of stories through what We have revealed to you of this Quran} - the verse. He said: Allah sent it down upon His Messenger and he recited it to them for a time, so they said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would tell us stories? So Allah, Majestic and Mighty, sent down: {We relate to you the best of stories} - the verse. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it to them, and they said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would narrate to us? So Allah sent down: {Allah has sent down the best discourse, a Book, its parts resembling each other and repeated}. He said: In all of that they were commanded to hold to the Quran. Khallad said: And another added in it, he said: They said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would remind us? So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: {Has the time not come for those who believed that their hearts should become humble for the remembrance of Allah}. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith was their asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to tell them stories so that their hearts would soften thereby, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down upon him: {We relate to you the best of stories}, thus informing them that they have no need for stories besides the Quran, because nothing more beneficial than it would be related to them, then they asked him to narrate to them, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down upon him concerning that what He sent down as mentioned in this hadith, and all of that returns them to the Quran, because they will not return to anything in which they find what they find in the Quran. And success is from Allah."
"1310","Chapter 181: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his saying about Abu Musa: He has indeed been given from the psalms of the family of Dawud. 1158 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the recitation of Abu Musa and said: This one has indeed been given from the psalms of the family of Dawud."
"1311","1159 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1312","1160 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him that Abu Salamah informed him that Abu Hurayrah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the recitation of Abu Musa al-Ashari and said: This one has indeed been given from the psalms of the family of Dawud."
"1313","1161 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah al-Nakhai narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father; and from Abu Ishaq, raised to Salamah ibn Qays, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abu Musa al-Ashari while he was reciting, and said: This one has indeed been given a flute from the flutes of the family of Dawud."
"1314","1162 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Qanan ibn Abdullah al-Nahmi informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah, from al-Bara, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he heard Abu Musa reciting the Quran and said: It is as if the voice of this one is from the voices of the family of Dawud. Abu Jafar said: So in these hadiths we narrated is the attribution of what Abu Musa was given of his voice to a flute from the flutes of the family of Dawud, so the flutes therein were attributed to the family of Dawud, not to Dawud himself. So a questioner asked about the meaning of that. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, was that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, mentioned Dawud in His Book and said: {And We certainly gave Dawud favor from Us: O mountains, repeat with him} up to His saying, Majestic and Mighty: {Work, O family of Dawud, in gratitude}. What the people of knowledge say in interpreting His saying: {O mountains, repeat with him} -"
"1315","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Isa ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid."
"1316","And what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid concerning His saying: {O mountains, repeat with him} - he said: Glorify. As for the people of Arabic, among them were those who held that the intended meaning is return with him, go back with him, from returning; among them Abu Ubaydah Mamar ibn al-Muthanna, as Wallad the grammarian narrated to us from Abu Jafar al-Musadiri from him, and he makes that among the signs of the prophets. And among them al-Farra Yahya ibn Ziyad, who said concerning that: The meaning of awwibi is glorify, then he mentioned after that from some of them that they said therein the like of what we mentioned from Abu Ubaydah, and what the people of knowledge say, with whom those of the people of Arabic who agree with them agree, is worthier. And since it was thus, and glorification was caused by Dawud, and those things were commanded to glorify with him - everyone who glorified with him was a family for him, like the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {Admit the family of Pharaoh into the severest punishment}. So Allah named them a family for him because of their following him and their doing his deed until they deserved thereby of punishment the like of what he deserves for his disbelief. And from this it is said: the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so send blessings upon them with him by saying: O Allah, send blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings upon Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim, or as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrahim, according to what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that in what we will mention afterwards in this book of ours in its place, if Allah wills. So what was mentioned of the family in meaning among these meanings was only mentioned of them because of their position with the one for whom they are family, and since they deserved that through him, he was through it in deserving what they deserved through him above them. So the like of that is: Abu Musa has indeed been given a flute from the flutes of the family of Dawud, and their flutes are their glorification which used to be from them, of which Dawud was the cause. So it is understood that Dawud's being the cause of that is stronger than their causes, and that what was attributed of the flutes to them is attributed to him, and that what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Abu Musa has indeed been given a flute from the flutes of the family of Dawud is in the meaning of his saying: a flute from the flutes of Dawud. And we ask Allah for success."
"1317","Chapter 182: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what should be done with one from whom wrong is seen, and his saying concerning that: You shall surely bend him to the truth with bending. 1163 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abu Musa, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those who were before you among the Children of Israel, when a doer among them did a sin, the forbidding one forbade him by way of discipline, then on the morrow he sat with him and ate with him and drank with him as if he had not seen him upon a sin yesterday. So when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, saw that from them, He struck the hearts of some of them upon others, then cursed them upon the tongue of their prophet Dawud and Isa son of Maryam; that was because they disobeyed and used to transgress. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you shall surely command the right and surely forbid the wrong, and surely seize the hand of the fool, and surely bend him to the truth with bending - or Allah shall surely strike the hearts of some of you upon others and curse you as He cursed them."
"1318","1164 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Badhimah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know how deficiency entered the Children of Israel? They said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger know best. He said: A man among them would blame his brother for a matter he disapproved of, yet what he saw of him would not prevent him from being his eating and drinking companion, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, struck the hearts of some of them with others and sent down concerning them: {Cursed were those who disbelieved among the Children of Israel} ... four consecutive verses. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: So by the Lord of Muhammad, you shall surely command the right and surely forbid the wrong, and surely seize the hand of the wrongdoer, and bend him to the truth with bending - or Allah shall surely strike the hearts of some of you with others. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected on his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith: you shall surely bend him to the truth with bending - and we found the people of language narrate concerning that from al-Khalil ibn Ahmad that he said: It is said: I bent a thing when I folded it and turned it, and the bend of everything is its curve, like the hooked staff, the sickle, and the polo stick. And we found them narrate concerning that from al-Asmai that he said: It is said: I bent a thing and I turned it when I inclined it to you and returned it to your need. So what is in this hadith of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): you shall surely bend him to the truth with bending, means you return him to it and turn him to it and incline him to it until he is, in what you do with him of that, like the hooked staff and the sickle and like the polo stick which cannot ever get out of what was bent upon it and folded upon it and returned to it to other than that. And we ask Allah for success."
"1319","Chapter 183: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the intent of the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves; he who strayed will not harm you when you are guided}. 1165 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: You recite this verse: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves; he who strayed will not harm you when you are guided} - and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When people see the wrongdoer and do not seize his hand - Allah is about to envelop them with a punishment."
"1320","1166 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah al-Fazari narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stood upon the pulpit and said: O people, you recite this verse, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So what was in these two hadiths of what Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people with therein was that they recite this verse as he recited it to them, and that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say ... so he mentioned to them what he heard him say, he said it from these two hadiths. And we know that he, may Allah be pleased with him, with his wisdom and majesty and great standing, does not address the people with a speech containing a deficiency, and we know that whatever deficiency occurred in that is from some narrator of this hadith, not from him, then we sought from other than these two narrations."
"1321","1167 - So we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, say: O people, you recite this verse from the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, placing it upon other than what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, placed it: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves; he who strayed will not harm you when you are guided} - and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When disobedience or other than the truth is done among them, then they do not change it - Allah is about to envelop them with a punishment from Him."
"1322","1168 - And we found Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I heard Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the pulpit say: O people, you recite this verse and place it upon other than its place: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves; he who strayed will not harm you when you are guided}. Then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When people see a wrong and do not change it, Allah is about to envelop them with His punishment."
"1323","1169 - And we found Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Tamimi and Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to us, they said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Ismail ibn Abi Khalid say: Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated to me, from Abu Bakr, he said: I heard him praise Allah and extol Him, then he said: O people, then he mentioned the rest of this hadith."
"1324","1170 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Dabbi narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, recited this verse: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves; he who strayed will not harm you when you are guided}. Then he said: People place this verse upon other than its place; indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When people see the wrongdoer and do not seize his hand, or he said: the wrong and do not change it - Allah, Mighty and Majestic, envelops them with His punishment. Abu Jafar said: So in these hadiths was what was worthier of al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, that he had said it, namely his informing them that people place this verse which he recited to them upon other than its place. So we reflected on what is narrated from other than him, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning this verse, to know thereby its place, whether it is an interpretation to be adhered to or a time among times which will come, before which is what he, may Allah's pleasure be upon him, recited to them of what he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say concerning commanding the right and changing the wrong."
"1325","1171 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir Abd al-Ala ibn Mushir al-Ghassani narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn Abi Hakim narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Jariyah narrated to me, from Abu Umayyah, who said: I asked Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, I said: How do you act concerning this verse? He said: Which verse? I said: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves; he who strayed will not harm you when you are guided}. He said to me: By Allah, you have asked about it one well-informed; I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it and he said: Rather command the right and forbid one another the wrong, until when you see obeyed miserliness and followed desire and a world preferred and the admiration of every possessor of opinion with his opinion, and you see a matter from which you must - then upon you is yourself, and beware the affair of the common people; for beyond you are days of patience, patience in them like grasping upon embers; for the doer among you on that day is like the reward of fifty men doing the like of his deed."
"1326","1172 - And we found Ibn Abi Maryam had narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Yazid al-Khurasani narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Abi Hakim, from Abu Umayyah al-Shabani, and he did not mention before him Amr ibn Jariyah, he said: I asked Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, then he mentioned the like of it exactly."
"1327","1173 - And we found Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih had narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Shuayb ibn Shabur narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Abi Hakim, he said: Amr ibn Jariyah narrated to us, from Abu Umayyah, then he mentioned the like of it exactly. Abu Jafar said: So we understood by this hadith that the meaning of the saying of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: People place this verse in other than its place - he means thereby they will apply it in other than its time, and that its time in which it is applied is the time which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described in the hadith of Abu Thalabah with what he described it, and we seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from it, and that what is before it of times, the obligation of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, therein upon His servants is commanding the right and forbidding the wrong until matters return to what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded that people be upon of complying with what Allah commanded them and refraining from what He forbade them. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning this meaning of commanding the right and forbidding the wrong, and warning against the consequences of abandoning that, other than what our narration of it has preceded in this chapter."
"1328","1174 - What Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ubaydullah ibn Jarir, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no people among whom disobedience is done, mightier and more numerous than the one doing it - and it is in my view, and Allah knows best, than the one doing it - and they do not change it upon them except Allah, Mighty and Majestic, envelops them with punishment."
"1329","1175 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, he said: Sayf ibn Abi Sulayman al-Makki narrated to us, from Adi ibn Adi, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not destroy the common people for the deed of the elite, but when they see the wrong among them and do not change it, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, punishes the common and the elite. Abu Jafar said: So in what we mentioned is emphasis of commanding the right and forbidding the wrong until the time comes in which that ceases, namely the time which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described in the hadith of Abu Thalabah, in which there is no benefit in commanding right nor in forbidding wrong, nor any strength with the one who disapproves to stand with the obligation in that, so the obligation falls from him therein, and his affair therein returns to his own self and he who strayed does not harm him along with that. Thus say the people of narrations in this chapter according to what we authenticated these narrations upon, and as for others besides them among those who cling to interpretation, they hold that His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {O you who believed, upon you is yourselves} - is not upon the falling of an obligation upon them of commanding right and forbidding wrong, and that they are not guided when they do not do that, and that they only enter into His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {when you are guided}, when they do that, not when they fall short of it, and they hold that the like of it from the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the saying of Allah to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Not upon you is their guidance, but Allah guides whom He wills}. While along with this he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is obligated with striving against the enemies of Allah and fighting them until Allah returns them to His religion with which Allah sent him and commanded him to fight people for it all. And the first saying is clearer in meaning than this meaning, though this meaning is sound. And we ask Allah for success."
"1330","Chapter 184: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the time in which people must attend to their own concern and leave the common concern. 1176 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Umarah ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How will you be with a time? Or he said: A time is about to come in which people will be sifted with sifting and there remains scum of people whose covenants and trusts have become confused, and they differ and become like this, and he interlaced between his fingers. They said: How should we be, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You take what you know, and leave what you disapprove, and attend to the affair of your own concern, and leave the affair of your common concern."
"1331","1177 - Ubaydullah ibn Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr Abu al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: And Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it exactly."
"1332","1178 - And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: And Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri informed me, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention Abu Hazim in his chain, rather he said: He said: And Yaqub informed me, from Umarah."
"1333","1179 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yazid al-Attar al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Mina narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Umarah ibn Amir ibn Hazm - thus he said: Ibn Amir, but it is Ibn Amr - from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the like of it."
"1334","1180 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Fahd ibn Sulayman together narrated to us, they said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to me, from his father, from Umarah ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it exactly."
"1335","1181 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, who said: Ikrimah narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As narrated to me, he said: While we were around the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when tribulation was mentioned, or it was mentioned in his presence, he said: When you see people whose covenants have become confused, and their trusts have become light, and they are like this - and he interlaced between his fingers. I said: How should we act then, may Allah make me ransom for you? He said to me: Stick to your house, and hold your tongue, and take what you know and leave what you disapprove, and upon you is the affair of the elite, and leave from you the affair of the common people."
"1336","1182 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abdullah ibn Amr: How will you be, O Abdullah ibn Amr, when you remain among scum of people whose trusts have become confused and whose covenants have become confused, and they differ? Abdullah said: How should I be, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You act upon what you know, and leave what you disapprove, and act upon your own concern, and leave from you the common people."
"1337","1183 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it exactly."
"1338","1184 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Said, he narrated to him that Abu Waqid said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while we were sitting upon a mat: There will be tribulation. They said: How should we act, O Messenger of Allah? He said: So he returned his hand to the mat and grasped it, he said: You do like this. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned to them one day that there will be tribulation, and many of the people did not hear him, so Muadh said: Do you hear what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says? They said: What did he say? He said: He says: There will be tribulation. They said: How for us, O Messenger of Allah? Or how should we do? He said: You return to your first affair."
"1339","1185 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr, from Irbad ibn Sariyah - and Irbad was a man of Banu Sulaym from the people of al-Suffah - who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one day, stood, exhorted the people, made them desire and warned them, and said what Allah willed him to say, then said: Worship Allah, do not associate anything with Him, and obey whomsoever Allah has given charge of your affair, and do not dispute the command with its people, even if he be a black slave, and upon you is what you know of the sunnah of your Prophet and the rightly-guided rightly-guiding caliphs, and bite upon it with the molars with the truth."
"1340","1186 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Sulami, from Irbad ibn Sariyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is my sunnah and the sunnah of the rightly-guided rightly-guiding caliphs after me, and bite upon it with the molars."
"1341","1187 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Awf al-Arabi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman. Abu Jafar said: He is Ibn Amr al-Sulami - and Allah knows best - he said: I entered the mosque of Damascus or Hims, and there was a man from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrating to them, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exhorted us with an exhortation from which eyes shed tears, and skins shuddered, and hearts became fearful. So a speaker said: It is as if this is a farewell from you, O Messenger of Allah, so counsel us! He said: I counsel you with fear of Allah, and your adhering after me to my sunnah and the sunnah of the guiding rightly-guided caliphs, and bite upon it with the molars! Abu Jafar said: In these narrations is emphasis of what is in the narrations in the first chapter, and all of them confirm one another, and inform that times differ and vary, and that every time among them has its ruling which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified to his nation and informed them of it. And he taught them what they do therein, so upon people is clinging to that and adhering to it, and placing every matter in its place in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to place it, and not to go beyond that to other than it. And we ask Allah for success."
"1342","Chapter 185: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When you differ concerning a road, make it seven cubits. 1188 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you differ concerning a road, make it seven cubits."
"1343","1189 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you differ concerning a lane, make seven cubits, then build."
"1344","1190 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit narrate from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed, when people differ concerning their roads, that they are seven cubits."
"1345","1191 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Muthanna ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Bushayr ibn Kab, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you dispute concerning a road, make it seven cubits."
"1346","1192 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-Muthanna ibn Said, from Qatadah, from Bushayr ibn Kab, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you differ concerning the road, leave seven cubits."
"1347","1193 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When there is disagreement concerning the road, it is made seven cubits. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected on this hadith, and we did not find a meaning more worthy to carry it upon and to direct its aspect to than newly begun roads when those who begin them differ concerning the measure they reserve for them from the places in which they seek to make them, like people conquering a city of the enemy cities, so the imam wants to divide it among them and wants along with that to make therein roads for whoever needs to pass through them among people to other than it of lands, and he does not find them of what those conquered had already arranged therein, so he makes the width of every road among them seven cubits according to what is in these narrations. And the like of that also is dead land which the imam grants a man, giving him its revival and making a road from it for people's passage through it from it to other than it, so that road is likewise, its width this measure. And we did not find for this hadith a meaning worthier of it than these two meanings, and Allah knows best the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from them. And Him we ask for success."
"1348","Chapter 186: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: And rise from the belly of Uranah, meaning in the standing. 1194 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-Ijli narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Sad, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Mabad, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All of Arafah is a standing place, and rise from the belly of Uranah. And all of Muzdalifah is a standing place, and rise from the belly of Muhassir. And the ravines of Mina are all a slaughter place. Abu Jafar said: And we did not find this hadith from the narration of any of the companions of Ibn Uyaynah in its chain more complete than it from the narration of Abu al-Ashath. And Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated it to us deficient in its chain and in its text altogether."
"1349","1195 - Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, and he did not mention Ziyad, from Abu Mabad, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rise from Muhassir, and upon you is the pebbles of throwing. Abu Jafar said: So we needed to ascertain his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Rise from the belly of Uranah; what does he intend by it? Is it because the belly of Uranah is not of Arafah in which one stands for Hajj? Or is it for other than that?"
"1350","1196 - So we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at Arafah and said: This is Arafah and this is the standing place, and all of Arafah is a standing place, and all of Jam is a standing place."
"1351","1197 - And we found Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala had narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me that Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to him that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: All of Arafah is a standing place, and all of Muzdalifah is a standing place."
"1352","1198 - And we found Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tammam al-Kalbi Abu al-Karawwas had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Maymun ibn Yahya ibn Muslim ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me, from Makhramah ibn Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from his father, he said: I heard Usamah ibn Zayd say: I heard Abdullah ibn Abi Husayn inform from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, while Ata was sitting listening, he said: Ata said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Salami say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All of Arafah is a standing place, and all of Muzdalifah is a standing place, and all of Mina is a slaughter place, and all the roads of Makkah are a road and a slaughter place."
"1353","1199 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: We came to Jabir ibn Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he narrated to us that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All of Arafah is a standing place. Abu Jafar said: So we needed to ascertain the meaning by which rising from the belly of Uranah was commanded, what is intended by it?"
"1354","1200 - So we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Zabbar al-Kalbi narrated to us, he said: Sharqi ibn Qutami narrated to us, from Abu Talq al-Aidhi, from Sharahil ibn al-Qaqa, who said: I heard Amr ibn Madi say: We were on the evening of Arafah in the belly of Uranah fearing the jinn would snatch us, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Pass on to them; for if they have submitted, they are your brothers. Abu Jafar said: Thus Abu Umayyah narrated it to us: for if they have submitted, they are your brothers, and in our view - and Allah knows best - it is: for since they submitted, they are your brothers, meaning since they became Muslims. So in this hadith was that they used to stand on the evening of Arafah in the belly of Uranah fearing for themselves that the jinn would snatch them, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to pass on to them, meaning to what is besides the belly of Uranah of Arafah, namely the places in which the jinn were before that, and they feared if they stood therein from their harms what they used to fear, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them that they are their brothers since they have submitted. And in that is what indicated that the Prophet's commanding people with that was after the submission of the jinn. So if a speaker says: Is it permissible that the jinn used to perform Hajj before their submission? It is said to him: What do you deny of that? The disbelieving humans used to perform Hajj as the Muslims perform Hajj until Allah abrogated that with His saying: {The polytheists are only impure, so let them not approach the Sacred Mosque after this year of theirs}. And that abrogation was of what was of the warning with which they were warned in the Hajj which Abu Bakr performed, and we shall mention that and what was narrated from him concerning it in its place of what follows of this book of ours, if Allah wills. And we ask Allah for success."
"1355","Chapter 187: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the proof of the intent of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, by His saying: {Then when you pour forth from Arafat, remember Allah at al-Mashar al-Haram} - the verse. 1201 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, in his hadith about the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prayed the dawn on the day of Arafah at Mina, remained a little until the sun rose, then rode and ordered a tent of hair, so it was pitched for him at Namirah, then he proceeded, and Quraysh did not doubt but that he would stand at al-Mashar al-Haram as Quraysh used to do in ignorance, so he passed on until he came to Arafah and found the tent had been pitched for him at Namirah, so he alighted therein until when the sun declined he ordered al-Qaswa, so it was saddled for him and he rode until when he came to the belly of the valley, he addressed the people. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that Quraysh in ignorance used to stand on the day of Arafah in other than the place in which people stand today at Arafah for their Hajj, and that in our view - and Allah knows best - is because Arafah is not of the sanctuary, and Quraysh would not go beyond the sanctuary, nor stand for their Hajj on the day of Arafah except in a place of the sanctuary, and the place in which they stood on that day therein was Muzdalifah."
"1356","1202 - As Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr, from his father, who said: I went seeking a camel of mine on the day of Arafah, so I went out and there was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at Arafah among the people. I said: This one is of the Hums, so why has he gone out of the sanctuary? Meaning by al-Hums Quraysh, and Quraysh used to stand at Muzdalifah and say: We are the Hums, we do not go beyond the sanctuary."
"1357","1203 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Rahawayh, narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Aishah, who said: Quraysh used to stand at Muzdalifah and call themselves al-Hums, and the rest of the Arabs stood at Arafah, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to stand at Arafah then to pour forth from it, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: {Then pour forth from where the people poured forth}. Abu Jafar said: So these two hadiths indicated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been in ignorance, by Allah's guiding him and His care for him, standing on the day of Arafah where people stand besides Quraysh, and the saying of Allah, Majestic and Mighty: {Then when you pour forth from Arafat, remember Allah at al-Mashar al-Haram and remember Him as He guided you, though you were before it among the astray * Then pour forth from where the people poured forth} - was proof that the pouring forth from that place had been preceded by their standing therein. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning this meaning."
"1358","1204 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Amr ibn Abdullah ibn Safwan, from Yazid ibn Shayban, who said: Ibn Mirba al-Ansari came to us at Arafah while we were in a place of the standing far, which Amr makes distant, and said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you, saying: Be upon these rites of yours; for you are upon an inheritance from the inheritance of Ibrahim. Thus Yunus narrated to us."
"1359","1205 - And al-Muzani had narrated it to us before that, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abdullah ibn Safwan, and he did not mention Amr, from an uncle of his, who said: We were in a standing place of ours at Arafah, then he mentioned the rest of this hadith. Abu Jafar said: So that indicated that Arafah had been among the standing places of Ibrahim in Hajj where people stand today for their Hajj, and as for his command (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Ibn Abbas to rise from Muhassir, while Muhassir is of Muzdalifah - that is for a meaning besides this meaning which may be because of its going out from the rites of Ibrahim, so he commanded people to rise from it and to return to the rites of Ibrahim, and Allah knows best His intent in that (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and success is from Allah."
"1360","Chapter 188: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in interpreting the saying of Allah, Majestic and Mighty: {And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated and the great Quran}. 1206 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, he said: I heard Hafs ibn Asim ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab narrate from Abu Said ibn al-Mualla that he was in the mosque standing praying, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him, and when he prayed he came to him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What prevented you from answering me? Did you not hear Allah, Mighty and Majestic, say: {O you who believed, respond to Allah and to the Messenger when he calls you}? - the verse, then he said to me: Shall I not teach you a surah, the greatest surah in the Quran, before I go out of the mosque? So I walked with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he was about to reach the door of the mosque, then I reminded him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Opening of the Book is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran which I was given."
"1361","1207 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Abu Said ibn al-Mualla al-Ansari, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him while he was praying, then he prayed, then came to him and said: What prevented you from answering me when I called you? He said: I was praying! He said: Did Allah, Mighty and Majestic, not say: {O you who believed, respond to Allah and to the Messenger when he calls you} - the verse. Then he said: Shall I not teach you the greatest surah in the Quran? As if he forgot it or was made to forget; I said: O Messenger of Allah, what you said? He said: {Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds}, it is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran which I was given."
"1362","1208 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: Jahdam ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, there is a surah the like of which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has not sent down upon me, so Ubayy asked him about it, and he said: I hope you do not go out of the door until you know it, so I began to delay, then Ubayy asked him about it, and he said: How do you recite when you stand in your prayer? I said: The Mother of the Book. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has not sent down in the Torah nor in the Gospel and the Quran - or he said: the Furqan - the like of it; it is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran which I was given."
"1363","1209 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - and he recited to Ubayy ibn Kab the Mother of the Quran - and said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, Allah has not sent down in the Torah, nor in the Gospel, nor in the Psalms, nor in the Furqan the like of it; it is indeed seven of the oft-repeated and the great Quran which I was given."
"1364","1210 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Said, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Praise be to Allah is the Mother of the Quran, and the seven oft-repeated, and the great Quran. Abu Jafar said: So in these narrations is that the Opening of the Book is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran."
"1365","And there has been narrated from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, concerning that: what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: My father narrated to me that Said ibn Jubayr informed him that Ibn Abbas said: {And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated and the great Quran}. He said: And Said ibn Jubayr recited it to me: {In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate} ... the seventh verse. And Said ibn Jubayr said: Ibn Abbas said to me: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has brought it forth for you, and He did not bring it forth for anyone before you. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith of the speech of Ibn Abbas is that the Opening of the Book is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran."
"1366","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from his father, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: {And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated}. He said: The Opening of the Book, then Ibn Abbas recited: {In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate}, and said: It is the seventh verse. And Said ibn Jubayr recited to me as Ibn Abbas recited to him. Abu Jafar said: So what is in this hadith was contrary to what is in the hadith of Ibn Marzuq, namely that in the hadith of Ibn Marzuq is that it is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran. And in this hadith of Bakkar is that it is the seven of the oft-repeated, and he did not mention other than that. So it is possible that the meaning of the saying of Ibn Abbas: {And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated}: the Opening of the Book intended by that it is the seven oft-repeated, and that the meaning of: {and the great Quran} is: and We gave you the great Quran, and the proof of that is that it came with the accusative and did not come with the genitive, though there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas concerning the seven oft-repeated what Mujahid narrated from him that they are the seven long ones."
"1367","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: {And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated}. He said: The seven long ones. And there has been narrated from him from the narration of Said ibn Jubayr from him what agrees with what Mujahid narrated from him of what we mentioned. And it contradicts what Ibn Jurayj narrated, from his father, from Said ibn Jubayr from him."
"1368","As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Qudamah narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim - meaning al-Batin - from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given seven of the oft-repeated, the long ones."
"1369","Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas concerning His saying, Majestic and Mighty: {seven of the oft-repeated}, he said: The seven long ones. Abu Jafar said: And worthier of what was narrated from Ibn Abbas concerning that, since there was disagreement therein from Said ibn Jubayr from him, is what Mujahid narrated from him, and there has been narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib that it is the Opening of the Book. As."
"1370","Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Suddi, who said: I heard Abd Khayr al-Hamdani say: I heard Ali say concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: {And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated}, he said: The Opening of the Book. Then we returned to seeking the meaning of what is in the hadith of Abu Said ibn al-Mualla, and what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah concerning the Opening of the Book - that it is the seven oft-repeated and the great Quran which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given - and we found that possible to be intended thereby that it is the whole Quran, meaning: in reward for it that it is like the reward for the whole Quran, as has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning {Say, He is Allah, One} that the reward for it is like the reward for a third of the Quran. And it was released in some narrations that it is a third of the Quran."
"1371","1211 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mudrik, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from al-Rabi ibn Khuthaym, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is one of you unable to recite a third of the Quran every night? They said: Who can bear that? He said: {Say, He is Allah, One}."
"1372","1212 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: Bashir Abu Ismail narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: Shall I recite to you a third of the Quran? So he recited {Say, He is Allah, One}, until he completed it."
"1373","1213 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah divided the Quran into three parts; {Say, He is Allah, One} is a part of it."
"1374","1214 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abu Masud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is one of you unable to recite a third of the Quran every night? So that was grave in their souls. He said: Allah, the One, the Eternal, is a third of the Quran."
"1375","1215 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Abu Qays Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, from Abu Masud al-Ansari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Is one of you unable or overcome to recite every night a third of the Quran? As if it was heavy upon them, so he said: Allah, the One, the Eternal {He neither begets nor is born * And there is none like unto Him} is a third of the Quran."
"1376","1216 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abu Masud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is one of you unable to recite a third of the Quran every night? So that was grave in their souls. He said: Allah, the One, the Eternal, is a third of the Quran. Abu Jafar said: So in these hadiths is that {Say, He is Allah, One} is a third of the Quran in the meaning that it is a third of the Quran in reward for it, and it has been narrated that it equals a third of the Quran."
"1377","1217 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sasah, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that a man heard a man reciting {Say, He is Allah, One} repeating it. When morning came he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and it was as if the man deemed it little, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It indeed equals a third of the Quran."
"1378","1218 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar Ismail ibn Ibrahim al-Hudhali al-Qatii narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sasah, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, who said: My brother Qatadah ibn al-Numan informed me that a man in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited {Say, He is Allah, One}, repeating it, neither adding to it nor decreasing. When morning came a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: So-and-so stood in the night and recited {Say, He is Allah, One}, repeating it, neither adding to it nor decreasing! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it indeed equals a third of the Quran."
"1379","1219 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Musa al-Saghir, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites {Say, He is Allah, One}, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran."
"1380","1220 - And as Abu Umayyah and al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi together narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Muslim, nephew of al-Zuhri, narrated to us, from his uncle Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about reciting {Say, He is Allah, One} and said: A third of the Quran, or it equals it."
"1381","1221 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Say, He is Allah, One} equals a third of the Quran."
"1382","1222 - And as Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1383","1223 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya, namely Ibn Said, narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Kaysan, who said: Abu Hazim narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gather; for I shall recite to you a third of the Quran. So whoever gathered gathered, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and recited {Say, He is Allah, One}. Then he entered, and some of us said to others: I think this is news that came to him from heaven, and that is what made him enter. Then he came out and said: I said to you: I shall recite to you a third of the Quran; indeed it equals a third of the Quran. Abu Jafar said: So the meaning of what is in these hadiths that {Say, He is Allah, One} equals a third of the Quran is the meaning of the hadiths we narrated before them concerning {Say, He is Allah, One} that it is a third of the Quran, and as it is permissible that that be in {Say, He is Allah, One} in the meaning that it in reward is like a third of the Quran, it is permissible in the Opening of the Book also in the narrations narrated therein which our mention of them preceded in this chapter that it is the Quran, that the meaning of that be that it in reward for it is like the reward for the whole Quran. And we ask Allah for success."
"1384","Chapter 189: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his command to people to follow Abu Bakr and Umar and to be guided by the guidance of Ammar and to hold to the covenant of Ibn Umm Abd, may Allah be pleased with them. 1224 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from a freedman of Ribi ibn Hirash, from Ribi ibn Hirash, who said: One whom I do not suspect, meaning Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow the two after me: Abu Bakr and Umar. And be guided by the guidance of Ammar, and hold to the covenant of Ibn Umm Abd."
"1385","1225 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah, and Ibrahim did not mention in his hadith: from a freedman of Ribi - then he mentioned the like of it."
"1386","1226 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ribi, from Hudhayfah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"1387","1227 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow the two after me: Abu Bakr and Umar."
"1388","1228 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1389","1229 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us more than once, from Abd al-Malik, from Ribi. And he narrated it to me another time and said: Zaidah informed me, from Abd al-Malik. Then he mentioned the like of it exactly in its chain and in its text."
"1390","1230 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Musab ibn Abdullah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Hilal, freedman of Ribi, from Ribi, from Hudhayfah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the like of it."
"1391","1231 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal, freedman of Ribi, from Ribi, from Hudhayfah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Ibn Abi Dawud said to us: And thus it was in his book, meaning al-Uwaysi: from Mansur, not from Abd al-Malik."
"1392","1232 - Abu Jafar said: Then Ibn Abi Dawud narrated it to me another time and said: al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Hilal, freedman of Ribi, from Ribi, from Hudhayfah. Then he mentioned the like of it exactly."
"1393","1233 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and al-Rabi ibn Sulayman together narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: Salim Abu al-Ala narrated to us, from Amr ibn Harim, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow the two after me: Abu Bakr and Umar. And upon you is the guidance of Ammar, and the covenant of Ibn Umm Abd. Abu Jafar said: This Salim Abu al-Ala is a man of Kufah called al-Anumi, and he is trustworthy, his narration accepted. And Abu Nuaym narrated from him and said: He is Salim ibn al-Ala Abu al-Ala. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected on this hadith, and what therein the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded people to follow Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is to comply with what they are upon, and to follow their example in what is from them in the matter of religion, and not to go beyond it to other than it. Then we reflected on what he commanded them of being guided by the guidance of Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, and we found guidance is drawing near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, with righteous deeds, and Ammar was of its people, so he commanded them to be guided by what he is upon thereof, and to be therein as he is therein. And that does not exclude others of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from that station; because intending thereby the one of its people does not negate the rest of its people being therein like him, as a man says: The place of so-and-so in worship is the place which ought to be adhered to, and in that is nothing negating that there be others in worship like him, or above him, among those who ought to be in being guided by them therein like being guided by him therein."
"1394","1234 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Qabus, namely Ibn Abi Zibyan, narrated to us that Abu Zibyan narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Guidance, good demeanor, and moderation are a part of twenty-five parts of prophethood. Abu Jafar said: So that guidance mentioned in this hadith was of righteous deeds in the place it is in of the parts of prophethood, and the guidance intended in this hadith is drawing near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, with righteous deeds, and that was present in Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded people to be guided by him in that, and to make him an imam for them therein, not upon excluding from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) others of his companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, from being therein as he is."
"1395","1235 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Uyaynah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jawshan narrated to us, from his father, from Buraydah al-Aslami, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking together, when we were by a man before us praying, multiplying bowing and prostration, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is moderate guidance - he said it three times; for whoever contends with this religion, it overcomes him. So the moderate guidance in this was in the things intended thereby to draw near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded therein moderation, so that it last from its people, and that indicated that guidance is the deed by which one draws near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Then we reflected on his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And hold to the covenant of Ibn Umm Abd - what did he intend by it? So we found Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said in His Book: {Among the believers are men who were true to what they covenanted with Allah; among them is he who fulfilled his vow, and among them is he who awaits, and they did not alter with alteration}. And Ibn Umm Abd, may Allah be pleased with him, was of them, and he was along with that of guidance. As."
"1396","Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, was likened to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his manner, guidance, and demeanor, and Alqamah was likened to Abdullah. And as."
"1397","Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Jami, from Shaqiq, who said: Hudhayfah saw Abdullah ibn Masud when he came out of his house and said: I have not seen anyone more resembling in manner the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from when he comes out of his house until he enters it than the owner of this house, and the preserved ones among the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew that he is of the nearest of them to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in means on the Day of Resurrection. And as."
"1398","Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Ghundar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Hudhayfah, who said: The preserved ones among the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew that Ibn Umm Abd is of the nearest of them to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in means. Abu Jafar said: And since Abdullah was in this station of guidance and manner in this world, and of nearness of means to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, on the Day of Resurrection - he was worthy to be held to his covenant which he covenanted with Allah, then never ceased from it until he meets Him with it on the Day of Resurrection. And that does not prevent that among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) be one whose station is this in this world and whose station is that in the Hereafter, and among those deserving of holding to his covenant the like of what Ibn Umm Abd deserved thereof. And we ask Allah for success."
"1399","Chapter 190: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: For every deed there is enthusiasm. 1236 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every deed has enthusiasm, and every enthusiasm has lassitude; either to a sunnah, or to an innovation; so whoever's lassitude is to my sunnah has been guided, and whoever's lassitude is to other than that has perished."
"1400","1237 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: For every deed there is enthusiasm, and for every enthusiasm there is lassitude; so whoever's lassitude is to a sunnah has succeeded, and whoever's lassitude is to other than that has perished."
"1401","1238 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya, meaning Ibn Said, narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Jadah ibn Hubayrah, who said: A freedwoman of Banu Abd al-Muttalib was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), praying and not sleeping, and fasting and not breaking, so he said: I pray and sleep, and fast and break. And for every deed there is enthusiasm, and for every enthusiasm there is lassitude; so whoever's lassitude is to a sunnah has been guided, and whoever's is to other than that has strayed."
"1402","1239 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: I entered, I and Yahya ibn Jadah, upon a man of the Ansar from the companions of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: There was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a freedwoman of Banu Abd al-Muttalib, then he mentioned the rest of this hadith."
"1403","1240 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abidah ibn Humayd al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: I entered, I and Yahya ibn Jadah, upon a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, a freedwoman of the freedmen of Banu Abd al-Muttalib, then he mentioned the like of it. And he added: And whoever turns away from my sunnah is not of me."
"1404","1241 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi informed us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Muslim al-Awar, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every deed has enthusiasm, then its enthusiasm turns to lassitude; so if his lassitude is to my sunnah he has been guided, and whoever's lassitude is to other than that has strayed. I stand and sleep, and fast and break; so whoever turns away from my sunnah is not of me."
"1405","1242 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every deed has enthusiasm, and every enthusiasm has lassitude; so if its companion is right and near, then hope for him, and if he is pointed at with fingers, then do not count him. Abu Jafar said: So we sought the meaning of this enthusiasm mentioned in these narrations, what it is?"
"1406","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, who said: Exertion was mentioned and it was said: That is the sharpness of Islam and its enthusiasm, and for every enthusiasm there is lassitude; so whoever's lassitude is to a sunnah has been guided, and whoever's lassitude is to an innovation or misguidance has strayed. Abu Jafar said: So we ascertained thereby that it is the sharpness in the matters which Muslims want from themselves in their deeds by which they draw near to their Lord, Mighty and Majestic, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) loved from them therein what is below the sharpness from which they must fall short and go out to other than it, and commanded them to hold of righteous deeds to what may last upon them, and their adhering to it until they meet their Lord, Mighty and Majestic, upon it. And there has been narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in uncovering that meaning that the most beloved deeds to Allah are the most lasting though little. And we mentioned that and what was narrated in other than this place of what preceded from us in this book of ours, so we sufficed thereby from repeating it, and we ask Allah for success."
"1407","Chapter 191: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The Muslims, their bloods are equal, and the lowest of them strives with their protection, and they are a hand against others; a believer is not killed for a disbeliever, nor one with a covenant during his covenant. 1243 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, who said: Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: I set out, I and al-Ashtar, to Ali, and we said: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenant to you a covenant he did not covenant to people generally? He said: No, except what was in this book of mine. So he brought out a book from the scabbard of his sword, and in it was: The believers, their bloods are equal, and the lowest of them strives with their protection, and they are a hand against others. A believer is not killed for a disbeliever, nor one with a covenant during his covenant, and whoever innovates an innovation, it is upon himself, and whoever innovates an innovation or shelters an innovator, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected on the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The believers, their bloods are equal. And we found the people of knowledge all do not differ in interpreting that that it is upon equality in retaliation and blood-moneys, and that it negates that a noble have over a lowly merit in that, and that it was a refutation of the people of ignorance in their abandoning killing the noble for his killing the lowly, and in that is what we understood thereby that women in the course of that are as men, and that a man is killed for a woman as a woman is killed for a man. Then we reflected on his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the lowest of them strives with their protection, and we found the protection intended in this place is the giving of safety, and that when a man of the Muslims gives the enemy safety, it is binding upon all Muslims; they may not violate it. And the like of this is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the safety given by Zaynab, his daughter, to Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi who was her husband."
"1408","1244 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Luhayah narrated to me, from Musa ibn Jubayr, from Irak ibn Malik al-Ghifari, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) captive, so he sent to his wife to take for him protection from her father. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the dawn prayer, Zaynab brought out her face and said: I am Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I have given safety to Abu al-As. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he said: This is a matter I did not know of until now, and the lowest of the Muslims gives protection over them."
"1409","1245 - And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Shabib al-Rabai Abu Said narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said and Salih, meaning Ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, that Zaynab migrated to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while her husband was a disbeliever, then her husband joined Syria. So the Muslims captured Abu al-As, and Zaynab said: I have given protection to Abu al-As. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have given him protection. And he said: The lowest of the Muslims gives protection over them. Abu Jafar said: So what we mentioned indicated that the protection of some Muslims is as the protection of all of them, so it is possible that his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And the lowest of the Muslims gives protection over them - be that intending thereby that the lowest of them is the woman, and possible that the lowest of them is the slave, and since he was the lowest of them and his safety was binding upon them, the free Muslim woman be thereby worthier of it than him, and what was of the Prophet addressing Muslims with what he addressed them therewith be informing them that since that protection may be from the Muslim slave, it be likelier to be from the free Muslim woman. Then we reflected on his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A believer is not killed for a disbeliever, nor one with a covenant during his covenant - and we found the people of knowledge in interpreting that upon two differing schools: A group of them say: That is upon advancement and delay in meaning: a believer is not killed, nor one with a covenant during his covenant, for a disbeliever, so the disbeliever intended thereby is the disbeliever without covenant, and they are those who say: The believer is killed for the dhimmi when he kills him deliberately. And among those who say that of the people of knowledge are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And a group of them say: The disbeliever who the mentioned one is not killed for in this hadith is the covenanted disbeliever; he is not killed during his covenant, upon speech resumed after a believer is not killed for a disbeliever, and after the break of its meaning. And among those who went to that of them, and interpreted this hadith upon this meaning, is al-Shafii, so he did not kill the believer for the covenanted disbeliever, and Malik ibn Anas used to go to this meaning that a believer is not killed for a covenanted disbeliever. As for interpreting the hadith we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that a believer is not killed for a disbeliever, nor one with a covenant by his covenant, we do not narrate from him concerning that anything. And when this meaning we described became difficult and the disagreement we mentioned occurred therein, we reflected on that, and we found his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): nor one with a covenant during his covenant - does not escape one of two aspects: to be conjoined to what is before it as Abu Hanifah and his companions went to therein, or upon resumed speech in the meaning: nor is one with a covenant killed during his covenant, as al-Shafii said, so we found them not differing that one with a covenant may be killed for whoever kills him in retaliation for him, so in that was what indicated that his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): nor one with a covenant during his covenant - was not upon negating killing from him; because if it were thus, he must not be killed in any state while in his covenant. And since he must be killed in his covenant in some state, we understood thereby that what is intended by his not being killed during his covenant is only that he not be killed in a special meaning, and there is no special in this except the warring disbeliever; because it was conjoined to it, so what is intended by his not being killed is: with what the mentioned believer is not killed for in this hadith. And the meaning of his saying returned: A believer is not killed for a disbeliever, nor one with a covenant during his covenant - to: A believer is not killed, nor one with a covenant during his covenant, for a disbeliever, while one with a covenant is a disbeliever - so that indicated that the disbeliever intended in this hadith is the disbeliever without covenant, and that his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned from him is upon advancement and delay in its meaning, as if he said: A believer is not killed, nor one with a covenant during his covenant, for a disbeliever. Like the saying of Allah, Majestic and Mighty, in His Book: {And those who despaired of menstruation among your women, if you doubted, their waiting period is three months, and those who did not menstruate}. In the meaning: And those who despaired of menstruation among your women, if you doubted, and those who did not menstruate, their waiting period is three months, and this is a saying which reflection necessitates, and analogy strengthens; because we saw one with a covenant, his blood became forbidden by his covenant as his property became forbidden by his covenant, while before that he was lawful in blood, lawful in property, then he became by the covenant forbidden in blood, forbidden in property, and whoever stole of his property what cutting is due in the like of, he is cut therein though he be a Muslim, as he is cut in the like of that when he steals it of Muslim property. So the forbiddance of property by covenant was as its forbiddance by Islam in what we mentioned, alike, or the punishment upon its violator was as the punishment upon the violator of the like thereof of what became forbidden by Islam. And since it was thus in properties, it must be in bloods likewise, and the blood which became forbidden by covenant be as the blood which became forbidden by Islam, and the punishment for violating it for its forbiddance by covenant be as the punishment for violating the like thereof for its forbiddance by Islam. Rather we saw the forbiddance of bloods in this above the forbiddance of properties; because we saw the slave steal his master's property and he is not cut, though he stole it from a guarded place, while we saw him kill his master deliberately and he is killed; so blood was in what we mentioned in forbiddance weightier than property in what we mentioned in forbiddance. And since it was thus, and the punishment therein altogether in other than the weightier was alike, the punishment in the weightier of them in what became forbidden by Islam and in what became forbidden by dhimmah is likelier to be alike, or the punishment for violating bloods forbidden by creed and by dhimmah be alike as the punishment in properties forbidden by creed and dhimmah which were made alike. So a speaker said: Has this saying concerning killing the believer for the covenanted disbeliever been narrated from any of the companions of the Messenger of Allah? It was said to him: Yes. It has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"1410","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, who said: A man of the Muslims killed a man of the worshippers, so his brother went to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and Umar wrote that he be killed, so they began saying: Kill, Hunayn. And he would say: Until anger comes. He said: So he wrote that he be given blood-money and not be killed. Abu Jafar said: So this Umar in this hadith ordered that the Muslim be killed for the covenanted disbeliever, so a speaker said: How did Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, write after that that he be given blood-money and not be killed? It was said to him: That in our view - and Allah knows best - was from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, because of what was from the brother of the slain when killing the killer of his brother for his brother was permitted to him, for he would say at that: Until anger comes, so there entered thereby a doubt from him which bore that what was from him be in the meaning of pardoning the killer of his brother before anger comes, so that pardon be in that state nullifying his right therein and in what is after it. And it bore to be upon other than that of what has no pardon therein nor in what is after it, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote at that doubt with averting retaliation and obligating blood-money in its place, for thus ought to be done at the entry of doubts with averting retaliation, and blood-moneys obligated in their place. And we ask Allah for success."
"1411","Chapter 192: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying in answer to Ibn Umar when he asked him about taking dinars for dirhams and dirhams for dinars in sale: When that is of the exchange of your day and you part, with nothing between you - then no harm. 1246 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, meaning: from Simak ibn Harb, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in Hafsah's chamber, and said: O Messenger of Allah, wait, I ask you! I sell camels at al-Naqi, so I sell for dinars and take dirhams, and sell for dirhams and take dinars! So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When that is of the exchange of your day, and you part, with nothing between you - then no harm."
"1412","1247 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: No harm when you take at the price of your day."
"1413","1248 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us; and Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us; and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi, Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi, and Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrahim al-Juddi narrated to us; and Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi al-Lului narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Maslamah al-Qanabi Abu Bishr narrated to us, then they all gathered and each one of them said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar. Then they all mentioned the like of it, except that some of them brought it upon the wording of the hadith of Abu Umayyah, and some of them brought it upon the wording of the hadith of Yazid, from Muhammad ibn Kathir. So a speaker said: What is the meaning of the price of the day in which they exchange, when we see sales pass among people in the like of this at the price of its day, and at more than the price of its day, and at less than the price of its day, with no disagreement among the people of knowledge in that and in its permissibility and soundness? So why was the price of its day sought in this hadith? So our answer to him therein, by Allah's guidance, Majestic and Mighty, and His help, was that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided Abdullah ibn Umar in his asking him about what he asked him in this hadith to the scrupulousness which people must employ in what he asked him about, though had the matter run contrary to it in what he asked him about, that would not prevent the permissibility of the sale and its obligatoriness, namely that whoever had dinars upon a man or had dirhams, then came seeking them from him, and he offered him in place of dinars dirhams or in place of dirhams dinars, and called him to take them for what he has upon him of its opposite - it is possible he intends from him to reduce him of what he has upon him by giving him other than it, while necessity calls the creditor to take that, and to bear wrong therein and reduction of his debt, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught Ibn Umar what would be when he does contrary to that, namely to consider the price of his day in what his debtor gives him for what he has upon him of the opposite genus of what he gives him. For if what he gives him is the price of his day, his debtor may be pleased to turn from him with what he takes from him to other than him of sellers, so he gives him that for the like of his debt which he had upon his debtor, so he leaves fully supplied, and his taking that from other than his debtor becomes as his taking it from his debtor; because there returned to him the like of what he had upon his debtor. And taking it from other than his debtor and his taking it had he taken it from his debtor are equal. And when he gives him with other than the price of his day the opposite of his debt of what, when he turns with it to others of sellers, then seeks from him to give him thereby the like of his debt which he had upon his debtor - he does not give him that; because of what is upon him therein of reduction, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught Abdullah ibn Umar scrupulousness from that, and employing what has no wrong therein upon his debtor, and what his debtor can compensate with from others the like of his debt, not what he cannot. And this is a grave wisdom which only Allah, Mighty and Majestic, bears, and it is what transactors ought not to go beyond in their transactions to other than it of its opposites, and we ask Allah for success."
"1414","Chapter 193: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his supplication for the people of his city that blessing be given them in their sa and mudd. 1249 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik ibn Anas informed him from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless for them in their measure, and bless for them in their sa and in their mudd. Meaning: the people of Madinah."
"1415","1250 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim made Makkah sacred and supplicated for them, and I made Madinah sacred and supplicated for them with the like of what Ibrahim supplicated for the people of Makkah, that blessing be given them in their mudd and sa."
"1416","1251 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: When people saw fruit they would bring it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it, he said: O Allah, bless for us in our fruit, and bless for us in our sa, and bless for us in our mudd! O Allah, Ibrahim is Your servant, Your friend, and Your prophet, and I am Your servant and Your prophet, and he supplicated for Makkah and I supplicate You for Madinah with the like of what he supplicated You for Makkah and the like of it with it. He said: Then he would call the smallest child he sees and give him that fruit. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected on these narrations and what therein of the Messenger of Allah intending by his supplication with blessing to the sa, mudd, and measure, and that was in our view from him - and Allah knows best - intending thereby blessing in what is measured with the sa, mudd, and measure of the fruits which are the properties of the people of Madinah, and from them is the livelihood of its dwellers, and his intending thereby to the sa, mudd, and measure was intending from him to what is measured with these things. And the like of this of the speech of the Arabs is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {And ask the town in which we were and the caravan in which we came}. And Madinah was the abode of fruits, not other than it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by supplication for the people of those fruits with blessing in what they measure their fruits with, and in what they sell them with, and in what they pay their debt thereof with, and in what they maintain whom they maintain with, while it was not the abode of what other than measures is used therein of scales, so they needed supplication for them with blessing in their scales as he needed supplication for them with blessing in their measures, and we ask Allah for success."
"1417","Chapter 194: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Weight is the weight of the people of Makkah, and measure is the measure of the people of Madinah. 1252 - Abu Bishr Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Hanzalah, from Tawus, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Weight is the weight of the people of Makkah, and measure is the measure of the people of Madinah. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected on this hadith and found Makkah had no fruit nor crop then, and likewise it was before that time; do you not see the saying of Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Our Lord, I have settled of my offspring in a valley without crops}. Rather it was a land of trade to which the pilgrim comes with merchandises, selling them there with the prices with which merchandises are sold, while Madinah was contrary to that; because it is the abode of palms, and from its fruits is their life. And charities would enter it, so the due therein of charity is taken by measure. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made all towns followers of these two towns, while people need weight in the prices of what they trade, and in other than it of what they transact therein of marriages, عرض goods, payment of zakats, and other than that of what they advance therein of other than it of things they eat. And the sunnah prohibited advancing weighed for weighed and advancing measured for measured, and permitted advancing measured for weighed and weighed for measured, and prohibited selling weighed for weighed except like for like and selling measured for measured except like for like. And the weighed in that, its origin was what it was upon at Makkah the day the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: The scale is the scale of the people of Makkah. And the measured in that, its origin was what people were upon at Madinah the day the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: The measure is the measure of the people of Madinah. Not changing from that, though people change it as it was to other than it of its opposite, so they return thereby to knowledge of measured things which have the ruling of measure to what the people of measures were upon therein then. And to weighed things to what the people of the scale were upon therein then. And their rulings do not change from that, nor turn from them to their opposites. And from this Abu Hanifah and his companions took that whatever the name of a sealed measure, qafiz, makkuq, mudd, or sa adheres to is measure in which the rulings of measure run in all what we described, and that all to which adheres the name of ritl and uqiyyah is weight in all what we described. Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi al-Lului narrated to us that from their saying, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, and no disagreement was narrated therein among them."
"1418","Chapter 195: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the most beloved fasting to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. 1253 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi together narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, namely Ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn Aws, he heard Abdullah ibn Amr say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: The most beloved fasting to Allah, Majestic and Mighty, is the fasting of Dawud; he would break a day and fast a day, and the most beloved prayer to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the prayer of Dawud; he would sleep half the night and stand a third of it and sleep a sixth of it."
"1419","1254 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Amr ibn Aws informed him from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved fasting to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the fasting of Dawud; he would fast half of time, and the most beloved prayer to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the prayer of Dawud; he would sleep half the night, then stand a third of the night after its half, then sleep its end. I said to Amr ibn Dinar: Did Amr ibn Aws say: a third of the night after its half? He said: Yes. So a speaker said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you narrate from him - then he mentioned."
"1420","1255 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja al-Ghudani narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Humayd al-Himyari, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Which prayer after the prescribed is best? He said: Prayer in the middle of the night. He said: Which fasting is best? He said: The month of Allah which you call al-Muharram. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that the best fasting is the month of Allah which is called al-Muharram, so how can fasting a day and breaking a day be more beloved to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, than fasting other than it of what is the best fasting? So our answer to him therein, by Allah's guidance, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that fasting al-Muharram is the best of times in which voluntary fasting is fasted, so that was a special fast in a special time of time, while fasting a day and breaking a day was a lasting fast, and the most beloved deeds to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, are the most lasting though little. So we mentioned that from him in what preceded from us of this book of ours, so the authentication of these two hadiths altogether was upon that with fasting al-Muharram is the merit of time, while with the other fast is lasting, so thereby each one of these two hadiths was in a meaning other than the meaning in which its companion is. And it became clear thereby that the most beloved fasting to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is fasting a day and breaking a day for the lasting which is with it, and that the most beloved of times to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, which voluntary fasting is for therein is al-Muharram, and we ask Allah for success."
"1421","Chapter 196: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: You will conquer a land in which the qirat is mentioned; what is his intent with that qirat? 1256 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Imran al-Tujibi narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah al-Mahri, who said: I heard Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will conquer a land in which the qirat is mentioned, so treat its people well; for they have protection and kinship. When you see two men fighting over the place of a brick, go out of it. He said: So he passed by Rabiah and Abd al-Rahman, the two sons of Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, disputing over the place of a brick, and he went out of it. So a speaker said: How do you accept this when you find the mention of the qirat running upon the tongues of all people and mentioned in all lands besides the land to whose people that qirat in this hadith was attributed, and you find its mention also in the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned."
"1422","1257 - What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Azraqi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah did not send a prophet but a shepherd of sheep. They said: And you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, I used to pasture for qararit. And of that is what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning one who walks with a funeral until prayer is performed over it that he has a qirat, and that if he awaits its burial he has two qirats. And we shall mention that with its chains in another place than this in what follows of this book of ours, if Allah wills. And of that is what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever acquires a dog that is not a hunting dog, a qirat is deducted from his reward every day. And we shall mention that also in what follows of this book of ours, if Allah wills. So our answer to him therein, by Allah's guidance, Majestic and Mighty, and His help, was that all people in all lands in mentioning the qirat are as he described, while the qirat intended in the hadith of Abu Dharr which we narrated is not of these qirats mentioned in these narrations in anything, rather it is something present in the speech of the people of that city which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised them its conquest, and mentioned to them its people and their kinship through him and counseled them with them well, namely Egypt. And present in the speech of its people: I gave so-and-so his qararit - when he makes him hear what he dislikes and when he addresses him with what he does not love to be addressed with, and they warn one another saying: Go from me, lest I give you your qararit, meaning: your abuse and making you hear the disliked which you do not love to hear. And this is not present in the speech of the people of any city besides the people of Egypt, so the Prophet informing his companions of that of them, and his promising them the conquest of their city in which they mention that, and that their hands will fall upon it until they are a protection for them and until they employ in them what he commanded them to employ in them, was of the signs of prophethood, and success is from Allah."
"1423","Chapter 197: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the qirat deserved by prayer over the funeral: is it by prayer over it specifically? Or by other than it along with it of escorting it from its house? 1258 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd ibn Sulayman together narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever comes to the funeral at its family and walks with it until he prays over it, he has a qirat, and whoever witnesses it until it is buried, he has two qirats like Uhud."
"1424","1259 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam, from Abu Said al-Khudri, who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever comes to a funeral and follows it from its family until he prays over it, he has a qirat, and if he goes with it until it is buried, he has two qirats like Uhud."
"1425","1260 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar al-Umari, Iyad ibn Abdullah al-Fihri, and Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not say: like Uhud."
"1426","1261 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the like of it. And he added: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the two qirats? He said: Like the two great mountains. Ibn Shihab said: Salim said: Abdullah ibn Umar would pray over it, then leave. When the hadith of Abu Hurayrah reached him, he said: We have wasted many qirats."
"1427","1262 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me; and al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi say: It was said to Ibn Umar: Abu Hurayrah says: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever follows a funeral, he has a qirat of reward. So Ibn Umar said: Abu Hurayrah has been excessive upon us. Then he sent to Aishah and asked her, and she confirmed Abu Hurayrah, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say it. So Ibn Umar said: We have neglected many qirats."
"1428","1263 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Adi ibn Thabit al-Ansari, from Abu Hazim al-Ashjai, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever walks with a funeral until it is finished, he has two qirats, and whoever returns before it is finished, he has a qirat. He said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the qirat? He said: Like Uhud."
"1429","1264 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abdullah al-Tirmidhi narrated to us, he said: Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Burd ibn Abi Ziyad, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, who said: I heard al-Bara ibn Azib say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever escorts a funeral until prayer is performed over it, he has of reward a qirat, and whoever walks with a funeral until it is buried, he has of reward two qirats, and the qirat is like Uhud."
"1430","1265 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever prays over a funeral - or he said: whoever walks with a funeral - he has a qirat, and if he awaits until it is buried, he has two qirats, and the two qirats are like the two great mountains."
"1431","1266 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Muzahim al-Madani narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever follows a funeral until prayer is performed over it, he has a qirat, and whoever awaits until its burial is completed, he has two qirats. It was said: What are the two qirats, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The smaller of them is like Uhud."
"1432","1267 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to me, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ubayy ibn Kab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever follows a funeral until prayer is performed over it and it is finished, he has two qirats, and whoever follows it until prayer is performed over it, he has a qirat. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it is heavier in his scale than Uhud. Abu Jafar said: So what was in these narrations of the mentioned reward for those praying over the funeral is by escorting it from its family and praying over it along with that, not by praying over it specifically, except that in the hadith of Amr ibn Yahya is mention of walking with it from its family, so therein we encompass knowledge that one who escorts it riding with it until he prays over it, his reward is below the reward of one walking with it until he prays over it, and that in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon the rider by choice despite his ability to walk; as for the rider by necessity for his inability to walk, he is as the walker with it. So if a speaker says: There have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrations in this meaning with deserving this reward by prayer over it, not mentioned therein other than that."
"1433","1268 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Ubaydah narrated to us; and what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Ubaydah narrated to us, from his father, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever prays over a funeral, he has a qirat, and whoever follows it until it is buried, he has two qirats, and the two qirats are like Uhud."
"1434","1269 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Shubah, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan, from Thawban, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral, he has a qirat, and whoever witnesses its burial, he has two qirats, and the qirat is greater than Uhud."
"1435","1270 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn al-Mughaffal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral, he has a qirat, and whoever awaits until its matter is completed, he has two qirats."
"1436","1271 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Malik, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral and follows it, he has two qirats like Uhud, and whoever prays over it and does not follow it, he has a qirat like Uhud. So this speaker said: These narrations therein mention deserving the qirat by prayer over the funeral specifically; do you make this contradictory to what is in the first narrations of deserving that qirat that it is by walking with it from its family and praying over it, not without that? It was said to him: This is not in our view contradiction, but it is in our view - and Allah knows best - upon the preservation of some of its narrators of what the rest of them omitted, so what is sound from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what that qirat is deserved by is by walking with the funeral from its family and praying over it, and what is besides that of what has no mention therein of walking with it is omission of its narrators, and whoever preserved a thing is a proof against whoever did not preserve it. So if a speaker says: Is the part of the qirat of the thing of which it is a part known, present in any of the narrations from the Messenger of Allah? It was said to him: We found no mention of that in anything narrated from the Messenger of Allah except something of the hadith of Abu Hurayrah."
"1437","1272 - For Musa ibn al-Numan al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, from Ibn Hubayrah, from Abu Tamim al-Jayshani, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dinar is a treasure, and the dirham is a treasure, and the qirat is a treasure. They said: O Messenger of Allah, as for the dinar and dirham we know them, so what is the qirat? He said: Half a dirham, half a dirham. So that was the measure of the qirat of the thing of which it is, and that was proof that the exchange they were upon of what is the equivalent of twelve dirhams is according to what he goes to who makes the blood-money twelve thousand. As for whoever makes the blood-money of silver ten thousand dirhams, that is upon that the equivalent of the dinar of dirhams was in their view ten dirhams, and upon that the qirats, their total the dinar, was in their view twenty qirats, while the qirat thereof was half a dirham. And Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that. So if he says: Did you find for the thing of which the qirat is a mention of measure in any of the narrations? It was said to him: We found not that - and Allah knows best - what it is, and it is possible that He concealed that until its people know it when they meet Him, Mighty and Majestic: {No soul knows what is hidden for them of comfort of eyes}. And we ask Allah for success."
"1438","Chapter 198: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning breaking the bone of the dead. 1273 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Umarah narrated to us, from Amrah, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Breaking the bones of the dead is like breaking the bones of the living."
"1439","1274 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sad ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Breaking the bone of the believer dead is like breaking it alive."
"1440","1275 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sad ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah."
"1441","1276 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Harithah ibn Muhammad, from Amrah, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Breaking the bone of the dead dead is like breaking it alive. So a speaker among those with no knowledge of the interpretation of the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This hadith obliges you to make in breaking the bones of the dead the like of what you make in breaking the living. So our answer to him therein was that what he obliged us with does not oblige us; because we found the bone of the living has sanctity and therein life, obliging upon whoever was a cause for taking it out of it and returning it from life to lifelessness what is due upon him therein of retaliation and compensation, while the bone of the dead has no life therein though it has sanctity, so its breaker in violating its sanctity is as the breaker of the bone of the living in violating its sanctity, while that breaking was not taking life out of it until it returned thereby lifeless as is in breaking the bone of the living likewise, so the cause which necessitates in breaking the bone of the living what it necessitates of retaliation and blood-money was negated, so neither retaliation nor blood-money was due upon him, while its sanctity after it became lifeless, since it remained, its violator after it became lifeless was as he in violating it when it was alive, and we ask Allah for success."
"1442","Chapter 199: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When one of you stands from his sitting place then returns to it, he is worthier of it. 1277 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us; and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya, from Muhammad ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi ibn Habban, from Wahb ibn Hudhayfah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man is worthier of his sitting place, though a need appears to him and he stands to it then returns, he is worthier of his sitting place."
"1443","1278 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Jawaribi narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us. And he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1444","1279 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This Wahb ibn Hudhayfah is a man of Ghifar from the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1445","1280 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man stands from his sitting place - and once he said: whoever sat his seat - and intends to return to it - he is worthier of it than others."
"1446","1281 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When one of you stands from his sitting place, then returns to it - he is worthier of it. So a speaker said: Is this a proof that whoever stands from his sitting place, then returns to it after a day or more than that - he is worthier of it than others of people; since that only intends thereby the common sitting places which are not owned, not the special owned sitting places like mosques, deserts which people alight, and places of towns permitted to people therein? So our answer to him therein, by Allah's guidance, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that it is of what we encompass knowledge that he did not intend thereby the return between which and the standing from that place intended for return is the duration he mentioned, but it is upon the return to the sitting place from which its owner stood, the standing by which he did not intend its abandonment, rather he stood for a matter that occurred to him upon returning to it, so he returns to sitting therein as he was before his standing from it. So when it is thus, he is worthier of that sitting place of his, and when it is contrary to it, he is not thus, and he and the rest of people therein are alike; whoever precedes of them to it is worthier of it than others of them. And success is from Allah."
"1447","Chapter 200: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: There is no sarurah in Islam. 1282 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Umar ibn Ata - Abu Jafar said: He is Ibn Abi al-Khuwar - from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sarurah in Islam. Abu Jafar said: And we did not find in this chapter a hadith with connected chain to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than this hadith; as for other than it of the narrated hadiths therein, among them is what is narrated from Ibn Abbas of what does not go beyond him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1448","Of that is what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sharik narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no sarurah in Islam; a man in ignorance would strike the face of a man and say: He is sarurah. It was said to Ikrimah: What is sarurah? He said: They say: The one who did not perform Hajj, nor Umrah."
"1449","1283 - And of it is what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, and he did not mention Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sarurah in Islam. Sufyan said: The people of ignorance would say to a man when he did not perform Hajj: He is sarurah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sarurah in Islam."
"1450","And of it is what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Sahl al-Araj narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sharik narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There is no sarurah in Islam. He said: A man would strike the face of a man in ignorance, then say: I am sarurah. So it would be said: Leave sarurah and his ignorance, though he cast his weapon in his baggage. I said to Ikrimah: What is sarurah? He said: The one who did not perform Hajj nor Umrah, or he said: nor sacrificed, or as he said. And of it is what is narrated halted from Ikrimah, not going beyond him to Ibn Abbas, as."
"1451","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Ikrimah, he said: He used to dislike it being said: Sarurah (one who has never performed Hajj). Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to determine the sarurah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade to exist in Islam - what it is. We found in the hadith of Fahd, from Abu Nuaym, which we have narrated in this chapter from the words of Ibn Abbas, that in the pre-Islamic era a man would slap the face of another man and say: He is sarurah. It was possible that the one slapped is the sarurah, because he had neither performed Hajj nor Umrah, and possible that the slapper is the sarurah, so he is excused in that due to his ignorance on account of which he neither performed Hajj nor Umrah. We wanted to determine the reality of that, and we found in the hadith of Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus what indicates that the slapper is meant in that, not the one slapped."
"1452","1284 - And Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani permitted us to transmit what he mentioned to us that al-Ghallabi narrated it to him, he said: Musab ibn Abdillah, meaning al-Zubayri, narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, he said: A man would slap a man in the pre-Islamic era and say: I am sarurah, so it would be said: Leave the sarurah alone due to his ignorance, even if he throws his dung in his own baggage. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sarurah in Islam. In that was the confirmation of what we mentioned. Then we needed to determine the permissibility of this name and its use for one who has not performed Hajj, or its disliked status and the prohibition of using it. We found in the hadith of Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, which we have narrated in this chapter, the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no sarurah in Islam. It was possible that what is meant by that is the prohibition of this saying in Islam, and possible that what is meant is that no one should remain in Islam until he performs Hajj, so that this name would thereby disappear from all people in Islam. We examined that and found that a man may be unable to perform Hajj, either due to a chronic ailment in his body or due to lack of means, and he does not perform Hajj for that reason. So whoever takes the meaning of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no sarurah in Islam, to include that, that was far-fetched, because the one who stays back from Hajj did not choose that; his staying back was out of inability for what we mentioned. So it is impossible that he be blamed for that, or that this name which we mentioned, by which blame of the one named with it is intended, should apply to him. And when this interpretation was invalidated, we understood that what is meant by it is that this saying should not be said to anyone. We have narrated that in this chapter in the hadith of Ibn Khuzaymah, from Hajjaj, from Hammad, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Ikrimah, that he disliked it being said: Sarurah. And we have also narrated that from Ibn Masud with a disconnected chain, which we had not previously mentioned in this chapter."
"1453","As Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah al-Masudi, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abdillah (Ibn Masud) said: Let none of you say: I am sarurah, for the Muslim is not a sarurah. And the like of that has also been narrated from Amir al-Shabi."
"1454","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Bishr Abu Ismail, he said: I said to Amir: Sarurah? He said: What is sarurah? Sarurah is nothing. Abu Jafar said: And this is preferable in our view, because sarurah in the speech of the Arabs means persisting upon something, and from it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And they did not persist upon what they did while they knew. So whoever stays back from Hajj, not out of persistence upon not performing Hajj, but rather due to inability or the like by which the obligation of Hajj is lifted from him - his companion is not one who persists with the blameworthy persistence, and when he is not persisting he is not sarurah. As for Ata ibn Abi Rabah, permissibility of this saying has been narrated from him."
"1455","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata used to be called: Sarurah, and he would not object. Abu Jafar said: And what we mentioned of the dislike of this saying was preferable in our view, because it is describing with a blameworthy state. And Allah we ask for success."
"1456","201 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what is meant by the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: But if they are women, more than two, then for them is two-thirds of what he left. 1285 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Said ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The wife of Sad ibn al-Rabi came with her two daughters by Sad and said: O Messenger of Allah, these are the two daughters of Sad ibn al-Rabi; their father was killed with you on the day of Uhud as a martyr, and their uncle took their wealth and took it all and left no wealth for them, and they cannot be married unless they have wealth. He said: Allah will decree concerning that. Then Allah sent down the verse of inheritance, so he sent for their uncle and said: Give the two daughters of Sad two-thirds, give their mother one-eighth, and what remains is yours. Abu Jafar said: And the verse of inheritance mentioned in this hadith is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Allah instructs you concerning your children: for the male is the share of two females; but if they are women, more than two, then for them is two-thirds of what he left - the verse."
"1457","1286 - Yunus and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: And Dawud ibn Qays informed me, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil ibn Abi Talib, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the wife of Sad ibn al-Rabi said: O Messenger of Allah, Sad has perished and left his two daughters and his brother, so his brother set out and seized what Sad left, and women are only married for their wealth, but he did not answer her in that sitting. Then she came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, the two daughters of Sad. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call his brother. He came, and he said: Pay to his two daughters two-thirds, to his wife one-eighth, and what remains is yours. Abu Jafar said: We examined His saying, Mighty and Majestic: But if they are women, more than two, then for them is two-thirds of what he left. Its apparent meaning was that the two-thirds in this verse was assigned only to those daughters who are more than two, not to two of them. That was among what some people clung to, holding the view narrated from Abdillah ibn Abbas concerning two daughters, that they have half of their father's inheritance, just as one daughter has of her father's inheritance, and that the two-thirds is deserved in that only by those daughters whose number is more than two - three or more. This is a view we have not found with anyone among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides Abdillah ibn Abbas. We found the saying of the jurists of the regions after Abdillah ibn Abbas until our day contrary to what was narrated from Ibn Abbas about it. The saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: More than two, in this, in their view means: But if they are women, two. And His saying: More than, is superfluous, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: So strike above the necks, meaning: Strike the necks. And He said: When you meet those who disbelieve, then striking the necks - and they are the necks - and above is superfluous, because what is above the necks is the bones of the head, and the necks are not part of them at all. The striking intended by that and applied in it is striking the necks, nothing else. And we found what indicates what they said, of giving two daughters two-thirds, in what is at the end of the surah in which this verse is mentioned, namely Surah al-Nisa, and it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: They ask you for a ruling. Say: Allah gives you a ruling concerning kalalah: if a man perishes having no child and he has a sister, then for her is half of what he left - up to His saying, Mighty and Majestic: But if they are two, then for them is two-thirds of what he left. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, assigned to the single sister from her sibling's inheritance in this verse as He assigned to the single daughter from her father's inheritance in the other verse, while the daughter is of stronger kinship to her father than the sister is to her sibling. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: But if they are two, meaning of sisters, then for them is two-thirds of what he left, meaning what their brother left. Since two sisters have two-thirds of what their brother left, two daughters are more entitled to that in what their father left, and their deserving it from him is more proper. And Allah we ask for success."
"1458","202 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: Whoever points with a piece of iron at one of the Muslims intending to kill him with it, his blood has become due. 1287 - Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, he said: Alqamah, meaning Ibn Umm Alqamah, narrated to me, from his mother, from Aishah, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever points with a piece of iron at one of the Muslims intending to kill him, his blood has become due."
"1459","1288 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Umm Alqamah, from his mother, from Aishah, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say... then he mentioned the like of it."
"1460","1289 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abdillah al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani narrated to us, from Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever unsheathes his sword then lays it down, his blood is wasted. Al-Fadl said: Meaning he strikes with it."
"1461","1290 - Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Makhlad al-Isbahani narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. We examined the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her): Whoever points with a piece of iron at one of the Muslims intending to kill him with it, his blood has become due - what is that becoming due? We saw a man say: My debt has become due upon so-and-so, meaning his debt which was deferred but has become presently payable upon him, in the sense of his saying: My debt has become presently payable upon so-and-so. We understood thereby that His saying in this hadith: His blood has become due, means: His blood has become permissible. An objector said: Why did he not say: His blood has become permissible for him? It was said to him: Because killing him has become permissible for the one pointed at with the iron and for others among people who attempt to repel him from him and prevent his weapon from striking him. Do you not see that if the one pointed at with the iron were chronically ill or unable, by something other than chronic illness, to kill the one who pointed at him with the iron in order to kill him with it, then it is upon others who have the strength for that to kill him so that he does not complete what he did of pointing the iron at his companion to kill him with it. For that reason he did not restrict the becoming-due of the blood to the one pointed at with the iron specifically - and Allah knows best. The foundation in this chapter was that the one who pointed the iron at his companion had pointed at him something which, if completed by him upon him, would make his blood due to the one pointed at with the iron. Since his blood would become due to him thereby, it became due for him to take the blood of the one who pointed the iron at him before he carries it out upon him. This meaning is what Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) and his companions held in this chapter, explaining it with this explanation which he mentioned. As..."
"1462","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Yaqub informed us, from Abu Hanifah, concerning a man who brandishes weapons against the Muslims, he said: It is binding upon the Muslims to kill him, and there is nothing upon them. He said: And if the one who brandished the weapon were insane and brandished it against a man, and that man killed him, blood-money for him would be due upon him. And he did not report any disagreement among them in that. They held that the insane person we mentioned, had what he pointed with been completed upon the one he pointed at, his blood would not have become permissible to him thereby. Since his blood would not become permissible to him by carrying out what he pointed at him upon him, it is even more so that his blood does not become permissible to him by merely pointing at him. As for what is in the hadith of Ibn al-Zubayr of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever unsheathes his sword then lays it down, his blood is wasted, and what al-Fadl ibn Musa interpreted in his saying: Then lays it down, as laying it down upon the one against whom he unsheathed it - that is a sound interpretation, because if the one pointed at had permissibility before the pointed thing is laid down upon him, then after he lays it down upon him it is even more proper that that be permissible for him against him - and Allah knows best. And there has been narrated from Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) concerning that what some people imagined to be contrary to that, and it is what..."
"1463","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub informed us, from Abu Hanifah, concerning a man who unsheathed his sword against a man and cut off his hand with it, then the one against whom the sword was unsheathed killed him. He said: Retaliation is upon him. And he did not report any disagreement among them in that. This is not in our view, and Allah knows best, contrary to this hadith from his school; rather it is upon the basis that the one against whom the sword was unsheathed, when his hand was cut off, desisted from unsheathing it against him, so killing him thereby became forbidden for the one against whom it was unsheathed. As for if, after cutting his hand, he remained upon what he was upon of unsheathing his sword against him, then he is thereby under the same ruling as before cutting his hand, and in a worse state than it. And it is intelligible therein that the permissibility of his blood for him then is greater than the permissibility of his blood for him before cutting his hand. And Allah we ask for success."
"1464","203 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the one who bit the forearm of a man, who pulled it away so the biter's two front teeth fell out. 1291 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Said, and he is Ibn Abi Arubah, informed us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Imran ibn Husayn, that a man bit another upon his forearm, so he pulled it, and his two front teeth were pulled out. That was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Did you want to eat - or to crunch, Said doubted - the flesh of your brother as the stallion eats - or crunches? So he nullified them (i.e., denied compensation for them)."
"1465","1292 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, he heard him narrating from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that a man bit the hand of a man, so he motioned with his hand like this and pulled his hand away, and his two front teeth fell out. They disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Does one of you bite his brother as the stallion bites? There is no blood-money for you."
"1466","1293 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to me, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Yala ibn Umayyah, that a man bit the hand of a man, so he pulled his hand from his mouth, and the biter's two front teeth fell out. They both came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Does one of you bite his brother as the young camel bites? So he nullified them."
"1467","1294 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that Safwan ibn Yala ibn Umayyah narrated to him from Yala ibn Umayyah, he said: I went out on campaign with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of hardship (Tabuk), and it was the most assured of my deeds in my own estimation. I had a hired man, and he fought with a person; one of them bit his companion, so he pulled his finger and his two front teeth fell out. He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he declared his two front teeth void. Ata said: I thought Safwan said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does he leave his hand in your mouth for you to crunch it like the crunching of the camel?"
"1468","1295 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Safwan ibn Abdillah ibn Safwan, from his two uncles Salamah ibn Umayyah and Yala ibn Umayyah, they said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk, and with us was a companion of ours. He fought with a man among the Muslims; the man bit his forearm, so he pulled it from his mouth and pulled out his front tooth. The man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking blood-money. He said: One of you goes to his brother and bites him the bite of the stallion, then comes asking for blood-money? There is no blood-money for it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nullified it. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith of Ibn Ishaq, from Ata, from Safwan ibn Abdillah ibn Safwan - and this is an obvious error, because Safwan ibn Abdillah ibn Safwan is a man of Quraysh from Banu Jumah, while Yala, the narrator of this hadith, is not of Quraysh itself but is an ally of theirs, a man of Banu Tamim, long resident in Makkah. Then we examined this hadith after noting the disagreement of the people of knowledge concerning this injury mentioned in it, and that among them are those who do not nullify the blood-money of the biter's two front teeth against the bitten one, among them Ibn Abi Layla. We have mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, concerning the states of the brandisher of weapons, what we mentioned therein: that the blood of the brandisher became permissible for the one brandished against only when, had the brandisher completed upon the one he brandished against what he brandished it for, his blood would have become due. An objector said: Biting is among that in which there is no retaliation, because it is breaking the bitten bone. Do you not see the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Does he leave his hand in your mouth for you to crunch it as the stallion crunches? This is evidence that in it is breaking the bone, and in breaking bones there is no retaliation. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that the crunching mentioned in this hadith is not breaking the bone as was imagined, because crunching (qadm) in the view of the Arabs is crunching with the tips of the teeth which does not reach that; what reaches that in their view is khadm, which is crushing with the molars. Rather biting here is like a wound of the skin of the forearm or going beyond it to the flesh between it and the bone; when it goes beyond that to that, it lays bare the bone, so its meaning in the forearm returns to the meaning of the bone-laying wound in the head which lays bare the bone, and in it is retaliation by the consensus of the Muslims. Like it, the laying bare of the forearm bone also has retaliation in it. Since there is retaliation in it when that is completed, it belonged to the one aimed at thereby to remove it from himself in order to thereby attain what is due to him in what befell him of it. Had the biter been insane or a minor child, and there occurred from the bitten one in that the like of what we mentioned in this hadith, the value of his front teeth would be upon the bitten one. Thus what we carried what is in this chapter upon agrees with what we carried what is in the previous chapter upon. And Allah we ask for success."
"1469","204 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning weighed things being like measured things in the entering of usury into them as it enters into measured things. 1296 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Abd al-Majid ibn Suhayl ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Said al-Khudri and from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a man over Khaybar, and he came with good quality dates. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Is all the dates of Khaybar like this? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we take a sa of this for two sas, and two sas for three. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not do so; sell the mixed dates for dirhams, then buy good dates with the dirhams. And he said the like of that concerning the scale."
"1470","1297 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Majid ibn Suhayl ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to us that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyib narrating that Abu Said al-Khudri and Abu Hurayrah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the brother of Banu Adi al-Ansari and employed him over Khaybar. He came forward with good dates, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Is all the dates of Khaybar like this? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we buy a sa for two sas. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not do so; rather like for like, or sell this and buy this with its price. And likewise the scale."
"1471","1298 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Abu Suhayl, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said and Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the brother of Banu Adi among the Ansar to Khaybar as a commander. He came to him with good dates, meaning fine ones. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is all the dates of Khaybar like this? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we buy a sa for two sas, and two sas for three sas of the mixed dates. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not do so; rather sell this and buy of this with its price. And likewise the scale."
"1472","1299 - And Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri al-Madani narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Abd al-Majid, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that Abu Said al-Khudri and Abu Hurayrah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the brother of Banu Adi ibn al-Najjar over Khaybar. He came to him with good dates, meaning fine ones. The Messenger of Allah said: Is all the dates of Khaybar like this? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we buy a sa for two sas, and two sas for three of the mixed dates. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not do so; rather sell this and buy of this with its price. Likewise the scale."
"1473","1300 - Musab narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Abd al-Majid ibn Suhayl, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah and from Abu Said al-Khudri - the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Thus it is in the book of Musab which he informed us is his father's original, from Abd al-Majid ibn Suhayl, from Abu Salih. This is contrary to what we mentioned of the hadith of Yahya ibn Uthman, from Nuaym, from al-Darawardi, because he put Abu Suhayl in place of Abd al-Majid. What Musab said in this is correct in our view, and Allah knows best. In these reports was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returning the ruling of the scale, in the entering of usury into weighed things through it, to be like its entering into the measure in measured things through it. He did not restrict in that to food or drink to the exclusion of other than them of what is neither eaten nor drunk. The apparent meaning of that necessitates what those say who say: Iron for iron, copper for copper, and lead for lead are not permissible except like for like, weight for weight; that since these things are weighed, usury enters them as it enters gold and silver, and as measured things like dates, wheat and barley in the entering of usury into them - as Abu Hanifah and his companions say concerning that. That is contrary to what the people of Madinah say about it, restricting that to measured things that are eaten and drunk specifically, to the exclusion of what is not eaten and not drunk. An objector among those who hold what the people of Madinah say about that said: Said ibn al-Musayyib held in this meaning what we held therein, contrary to what the others held therein. And he mentioned what..."
"1474","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyib say: There is no usury except in gold or silver, or in what is measured or weighed of what is eaten or drunk. And he said: To whose saying, in opposition to this saying of Said, did you go? It was said to him: To the saying of Ammar ibn Yasir which contradicts it, for his saying concerning that is higher than the saying of Said. What is narrated from Ammar concerning that is what..."
"1475","Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn al-Muthanna, from his grandfather Riyah ibn al-Harith, he said: Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: A slave is better than two slaves, a slave-girl is better than two slave-girls, a camel is better than two camels, and an ox is better than two oxen. Whatever is hand to hand, there is no harm; usury is only in deferment, not in what was measured or weighed."
"1476","And what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn al-Muthanna, from Riyah ibn al-Harith, from Ammar ibn Yasir - the like of it, except that he did not say: An ox is better than two oxen. He said in its place: A garment is better than two garments. Abu Jafar said: Since the most firmly established things in the entering of usury upon them are gold and silver, while they are neither eaten nor drunk - we understood thereby that the effective cause for which usury enters weighing in what is weighed and measuring in what is measured applies whether that is eaten or drunk, or not eaten or drunk. And Allah we ask for success."
"1477","205 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: A man may be among the people of prayer and the people of zakat, until he mentioned the shares of good, and he will only be recompensed on the Day of Resurrection according to his intellect. 1301 - Abu Umayyah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Suqayr narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man may be among the people of prayer, zakat, Hajj and Umrah, until he mentioned the shares of good, and he will only be recompensed on the Day of Resurrection according to his intellect. We examined this hadith and found that whoever prays a prayer devoting himself to it until he fulfills its humility, standing, recitation, bowing, prostration, and all else he should bring in it of its obligations and sunnahs, devoting himself to it and abandoning preoccupation with other than it - his reward for it is greater than his reward had he prayed it otherwise, abandoning humility in it and preoccupying himself with other than it, so that what he brought it upon was the opposite of the first states we mentioned of what is praised therein. In praying it upon the praiseworthy states he was mindful of it, and in praying it upon the blameworthy states heedless of it; he is recompensed according to his mindfulness therein, unlike what he is recompensed in his states of heedlessness of it. Of this, in our view - and Allah knows best - is what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book of ours, of his saying: A man prays the prayer and there is written for him of it only half of it. Then he mentioned its parts until he ended at its tenth. Likewise zakat: when he places it with those most deserving of it, of the highest ranks of its people in need of it, chronic illness, inability apart from it in what suffices from it, and modesty so that he is thought not to be among its people, abandoning asking for it and for other charities - his reward for that is unlike the reward of one who places it with one who is not of its people in that station, due to his asking people, confronting them, and his ability to earn what suffices him from it. Of it is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: And the example of those who spend their wealth seeking Allah's pleasure and steadfastness from themselves is like a garden on a hill... Then there was narrated from Mujahid concerning its interpretation what..."
"1478","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning: And steadfastness from themselves, he said: They ascertain where they place their wealth. Abu Jafar said: Meaning that with which they draw near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Whoever is like that is not like one who gives it to whoever is present without seeking this meaning therein. Likewise fasting: in abandoning idle speech therein, devoting himself to it, and abandoning foul speech and ignorance therein - the recompense of one who brings it thus is unlike the recompense of one who brings it otherwise. Likewise Hajj: whoever brings it without foul speech, sin or disputing therein, his recompense for it is unlike the recompense of one who brings it otherwise. All these praiseworthy things in the categories we mentioned are understood from their doers due to their deeds they did therein, so that they thereby deserved what was promised upon them. They were unlike one who did not do that, whom heedlessness distracted from what was due upon him therein, so that he thereby returned blameworthy in that heedlessness, ignorant of what was binding from it. Likewise all the shares of Islam are upon this meaning: the recompense of one who was mindful of them until he fulfilled them from himself is unlike the recompense of one who was ignorant of them until he neglected them. We have not found in the interpretation of this hadith anything better than what we mentioned. And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by it. And success is through Allah."
"1479","206 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: Allah does not give ear to anything as He gives ear to a prophet chanting the Quran. 1302 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not give ear to anything as He gives ear to a prophet chanting the Quran. We examined the meaning intended in this hadith and found that giving ear here is listening. Of it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: When the sky is split, and gives ear to its Lord, and is due. Meaning: It listens to what its Lord, Mighty and Majestic, commands it and to what He loves from it. Like that is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not give ear to anything as He gives ear to a prophet chanting the Quran. Meaning: He does not listen to anything as He listens to a prophet chanting the Quran, beautifying his voice with it seeking softness of his heart thereby, for what he hopes therein of his Lord's reward for him upon it. And Allah we ask for success."
"1480","207 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran. 1303 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abi Nahik, from Sad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran."
"1481","1304 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed us, from Ubaydillah ibn Abi Nahik, from Said ibn Abi Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. I heard Fahd say: Abdillah ibn Salih said to us: al-Layth said to us in Iraq, meaning in this hadith: From Sad ibn Abi Waqqas."
"1482","1305 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us. And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: It was read to Shuayb ibn al-Layth: al-Layth narrated to me. Then they all together said: He said: Abdillah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from Ubaydillah ibn Abi Nahik, from Said ibn Abi Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1483","1306 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ubaydillah ibn Abi Nahik, from Said or Sad, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1484","1307 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abi Nahik, from Sad, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. We examined the meaning of this hadith and found people concerning it upon two sayings: Some of them say: What is meant by it is being sufficed by the Quran from all things, because thereby may be the immense recompense in the Hereafter and attaining thereby from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, His immediate good in this world. And some say: It is upon beautifying the voice so that the heart of the one who recites it softens. We sought the more deserving of these two sayings by its meaning."
"1485","1308 - We found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ward narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abi Yazid. Abu Jafar said: Thus he said, but it is Ibn Abi Nahik. He said: We entered upon Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir, and there was a man shabby in house and shabby in furnishings. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran. I said to Ibn Abi Mulaykah: What of one who has no voice and does not do well? He said: He beautifies it as much as he can."
"1486","1309 - And we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Yasarah ibn Safwan ibn Jamil al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ward narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdillah ibn Abi Nahik - thus Fahd said to us: From Abdillah, but it is Ubaydullah - he said: We entered upon Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir, and we entered upon a man shabby in house, shabby in furnishings, shabby in condition. He asked us and said: Who are you? Each of us gave him his lineage. He said: Welcome, traders earning; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran. I said to Ibn Abi Mulaykah: What of one who has no fine throat? He said: He beautifies it as much as he can. The meaning of what Abu Lubabah narrated to them of this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have been due to what they saw in him of shabbiness of condition, and may have been intending beautifying the voice with the Quran. Likewise Ibn Abi Mulaykah interpreted it upon him in this hadith. Then we sought this chapter: do we find it from other than this path from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"1487","1310 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran. Then we returned to seeking the more deserving of the two sayings we mentioned. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He is not of us who does not chant the Quran, was in censure of one who does not do that. Like his saying: He is not of us who deceives us, and: He is not of us who shoots at us by night - concerning the things requited from whoever they were or will be from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we will mention hereafter in this book of ours, Allah willing, upon censure of whoever is like that. We found one who recites the Quran without beautifying his voice with it, intending by his recitation the praiseworthy states, rewarded for that and not blamed upon it. We understood thereby that the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his saying: Who does not chant the Quran, cannot be this meaning. When that meaning was negated from it, and nothing was said in its interpretation besides these two sayings, and one of them was negated - the other of them was established, namely being sufficed by it from all things besides it. And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by that saying. And Him we ask for success."
"1488","208 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: Whoever plunders is not of us. 1311 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Yala ibn Hakim narrating from Abu Labid, he said: I witnessed Kabul with Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah. People acquired sheep and plundered them. Abd al-Rahman said: Whoever plundered anything of these sheep, let him return it, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever plunders is not of us."
"1489","1312 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Abu Umayr al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever plunders is not of us."
"1490","1313 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever plunders a plunder is not of us."
"1491","1314 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr said: Jabir ibn Abdillah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever plunders a notorious plunder is not of us."
"1492","1315 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever plunders is not of us."
"1493","1316 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Anas and Humayd, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade plundering and said: Whoever plunders is not of us."
"1494","1317 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Anas, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it, and he did not mention Humayd."
"1495","1318 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Thalabah ibn al-Hakam, brother of Banu Layth, informed me that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pass by pots containing sheep meat which they had plundered. He ordered them overturned and said: Plunder is not lawful. Abu Jafar said: It was possible that what is in these reports is upon every plunder, and possible that it is upon a specific part of it. So we examined that."
"1496","1319 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Abdillah ibn Luhayy - Abu Jafar said: Abdillah ibn Luhayy is Abu Amir al-Hawzani - from Abdillah ibn Qurt, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved days to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, are the Day of Sacrifice, then the Day of Qarr (settling in Mina). I brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) five or six sacrificial camels, and they began crowding toward him, with which he would begin. When their sides fell (after slaughter), he said a hidden word I did not understand. I said to the one beside me: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say? He said: He said: Whoever wishes may cut off (and take)."
"1497","1320 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Najiyah, the keeper of the sacrificial camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall I do with what perishes among the camels? He said: Slaughter it, then dip its necklace in its blood, then strike its side with it - thus he said, though it is its side - then leave it between it and the people."
"1498","1321 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, and he did not mention Najiyah, that the keeper of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall I do with what is injured among the sacrificial animals? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Slaughter them, then cast their necklaces in their blood, then leave the people and them to eat them. Abu Jafar said: In these two hadiths was the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people for whom that sacrificial meat is lawful to take what is permissible for them to take of that meat, without intending specific people, and without intending a fixed amount of the meat for whoever takes of them. We understood thereby that the plundering he forbade in the first reports, disowning whoever does it from being of him, is other than this plundering: it is the plundering of what has not been permitted to be plundered. And Allah knows best what the intent of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that. And success is through Allah."
"1499","209 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the rest of the things, whoever has them being of him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 1322 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus, Malik and Usamah informed me that Nafi informed them, from Abdillah ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever bears arms against us is not of us."
"1500","1323 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Man ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi. And from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Abdillah ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1501","1324 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1502","1325 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah and Yahya ibn Burayd al-Ashari narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abdillah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it. He said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) denied that whoever has this meaning in him is of him."
"1503","1326 - Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shoots at us by night is not of us."
"1504","1327 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shoots at us by night is not of us. Abu Jafar said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby denied that whoever has one of these two meanings in him is of him."
"1505","1328 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam both narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn al-Khayr al-Zabadi narrated to us, from Abu Qabil, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who does not honor our elder, have mercy on our young, and recognize the right of our scholar. What is in this hadith entered into the meaning of what we narrated before it."
"1506","1329 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man selling food, and it pleased him, so he put his hand into it, and there was wet food. He said: He is not of us who deceives us."
"1507","1330 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysarah narrated to me that al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a heap of food being sold in the market, and in its bottom was wetness. He said: What is this? They said: Water struck it. He said: Did you not display it to the people? Whoever deceives is not of me."
"1508","1331 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever deceives us is not of us. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith entered into the meaning of what we narrated before it."
"1509","1332 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Awf, from Khalid al-Ahdhab, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, he said: Abu Musa al-Ashari fell unconscious, and they wept over him. He said: I disown before you what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disowned to us from, he said: He is not of us who shaves (the head in mourning), tears (clothes), or raises the voice (in wailing). Abu Jafar said: He means by his saying: Salaq - speaking what is not lawful for him to speak. Of it is the saying of Allah, Exalted: They assail you with sharp tongues."
"1510","1333 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ararah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Iyad al-Ashari, he said: When Abu Musa al-Ashari fell unconscious, he was wept over. He lifted the garment from him and said: Did you (feminine) not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He is not of me who shaves, tears, or raises the voice?"
"1511","1334 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Zubayd, from Ibrahim, from Masruq, from Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who strikes the cheeks, tears the garments, and calls with the call of the pre-Islamic era."
"1512","1335 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydah ibn Humayd al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Yazid ibn Aws, from Umm Abdillah, wife of Abu Musa al-Ashari, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who shaves, tears, or raises the voice."
"1513","1336 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abdillah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who strikes the cheeks, tears the garments, and calls with the call of the people of the pre-Islamic era."
"1514","1337 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-Amash, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. What is in these hadiths entered into the meaning of what we narrated before it."
"1515","1338 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning snakes: We have not made peace with them since we warred against them; whoever leaves them out of fear of them is not of us."
"1516","1339 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abdillah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1517","1340 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Farwah ibn Abi al-Maghra narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Malik narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn al-Hakam, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fears their retaliation is not of us. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith entered into the meaning of what we mentioned before it."
"1518","1341 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman and Mughirah al-Dabbi informed us, from Mujahid, from Abdillah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do you fast the day? He said: I said: Yes. And stand the night? He said: I said: Yes. He said: But I fast and break fast, sleep, and touch women; whoever turns away from my sunnah is not of me. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of what is in this hadith entered into the meanings of what we narrated before it."
"1519","1342 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Thalabah al-Tai narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears by amanah (trust) is not of us, and whoever alienates the wife of a Muslim man is not of us. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of what is in this hadith entered into the meanings of what we narrated before it."
"1520","1343 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah al-Ataki Abu al-Munayb narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Witr is a right; whoever does not perform witr is not of me - he said it three times. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of what is in this hadith entered into the meanings of what we narrated before it."
"1521","1344 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Shabi, from Asim al-Adawi, from Kab ibn Ujrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were sitting upon a cushion of leather. He said: There will be rulers after me; whoever enters upon them, believes them in their lies, and helps them in their wrongdoing - he is not of me, I am not of him, and he will not come to me at the Hawd. Whoever does not believe them in their lies and does not help them in their wrongdoing - he is of me, I am of him, and he will come to me at the Hawd."
"1522","1345 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Kab ibn Ujrah, I seek refuge for you with Allah from the rule of the foolish. There will be rulers; whoever enters upon them, helps them in their wrongdoing, and believes them in their lies - he is not of me, I am not of him, and he will never come to me at the Hawd. Whoever does not enter upon them, does not help them in their wrongdoing, and does not believe them in their lies - he is of me, I am of him, and he will come to me at the Hawd."
"1523","1346 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym and Abu Ghassan narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibrahim Quays, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were four Arabs and five clients. He said: Have you heard that there will be rulers after me; whoever helps them in their wrongdoing, believes them in their lies, and frequents their doors - he is not of me, I am not of him, and he will not come to me at the Hawd. Whoever does not help them in their wrongdoing, does not believe them in their lies, and does not frequent their doors - he is of me, I am of him, and he will come to me at the Hawd."
"1524","1347 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Yahya ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: May Allah, Mighty and Majestic, protect you from rulers who will be after me. He said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Whoever enters upon them in their palaces, believes them in their lies, and helps them in their tyranny - he is not of me and will not come to my Hawd. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of what is in this hadith entered into the meanings of what we narrated before it."
"1525","1348 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who has intercourse with a pregnant woman. What is in this entered into the meanings of what we narrated before it."
"1526","1349 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Khallad ibn Yahya al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Habib ibn Yasar, from Abu Ramlah, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does not trim his mustache is not of us. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of what is in this entered into the meanings of what we narrated before it. Abu Jafar said: These things, from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disowned whoever has them or in whom they are, were blameworthy things. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had chosen for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the praiseworthy matters and negated from him the blameworthy matters. So whoever does the praiseworthy matters is of him, and whoever does the blameworthy matters is not of him. As He, Mighty and Majestic, related from His prophet Ibrahim, of his saying concerning his offspring: Whoever follows me, he is of me, and whoever disobeys me, then You are Forgiving, Merciful. And as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said informing His servants in the story of His prophet Dawud (peace be upon him): Allah will test you with a river; whoever drinks of it is not of me, and whoever does not taste it, he is of me - among likes of this existing in the Book whose meaning is the meaning we mentioned. This indicates that every doer of a deed upon the law of his prophet, upon whom are his followers, is of him, and every doer of a deed which the law of his prophet, upon whom are his followers, forbids is not of him, due to his departing from what he called him to and from what he is upon, to its opposite. And Allah we ask for success."
"1527","210 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the Muslim when reaching a people and when rising from them, and whether the greeting of one who reaches them is while standing or after sitting. 1350 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, that Said ibn Abi Said informed him, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you reaches the gathering, let him greet. If it occurs to him to sit, let him sit. When he rises, let him greet, for the first is not more deserving than the last."
"1528","1351 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan informed us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they mentioned the like of it."
"1529","1352 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan informed us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1530","1353 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1531","1354 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim, meaning known as Saiqah, informed us, he said: Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Zuray, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: The people of chains, as I heard some of our companions say, approve this hadith from Abu Asim, from Yazid ibn Zuray, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Ibn Ajlan, in a way they do not approve it from him, from Ibn Ajlan directly. In what we narrated is that the greeting of the comer is upon the people when he reaches them, before sitting with them."
"1532","1355 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb informed us, he said: al-Jarud ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ajlan say: Said al-Maqburi narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sits, let him greet, and when he rises, let him greet; the first is not more deserving than the last. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that his greeting upon them is after sitting with them. An objector among those who follow such things, seeking thereby to obscure upon the ignorant of the language, said: This is severe contradiction; how is it permissible for you to accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thus? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, was that that is not upon contradiction, but upon the breadth of the language. How fitting that what you thought contradiction is from the saying of those after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and that is not objectionable, for they are Arabs, and their language accommodates this therein. The Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has come with the like of this. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: And when you divorce women and they reach their term, retain them in kindness or release them in kindness. That was mentioned with reaching the term, yet there is no retaining for the divorcers after the divorced women reach their terms, because it is the expiry of their waiting periods from them. The saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in this verse: They reach their term, is only upon nearness to reaching the term, not upon its actual reaching. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, clarified that in the other verse, His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And when you divorce women and they reach their term, do not prevent them from marrying their husbands. Like that is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When one of you reaches the people, let him greet - intending thereby the actual place of greeting; and his saying: When one of you sits, let him greet - intending thereby nearness of his sitting with them to his reaching them, not the actual sitting with them. And Allah we ask for success."
"1533","211 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his saying: A child will never repay a parent except that he finds him enslaved and buys him and frees him. 1356 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A child will never repay a parent except that he finds him enslaved and buys him and frees him. Abu Jafar said: An objector said: In this hadith is what necessitates that after buying his father he remains enslaved to him until he frees him, while the people of knowledge upon whom fatwa turns in the regions do not say this, despite the soundness of this hadith among them. In that is evidence of their weakening it and turning away from it. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that what he imagined in this hadith is not as he imagined therein. For it is permissible that his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And buys him and frees him, means: And buys him so his buying him frees him. He said: Is there evidence for that? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance, was: Our evidence for that is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every newborn is born upon fitrah; then his parents make him Jewish, Christian, or polytheist. That was not upon the meaning of their making him Jewish or Christian with a fresh making-Jewish or making-Christian they initiate in him; rather it is a cause from them necessitating that in him. Like that is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And buys him and frees him. It is not upon a freeing he initiates in him after buying him; rather his cause from him is that with which, after owning him, his ownership of him cannot remain. And Allah we ask for success."
"1534","212 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning walking in one sandal and in one sock. 1357 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you walk in one sandal; let him wear them both together or remove them both together."
"1535","1358 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade walking in one sandal and said: Satan walks in one sandal."
"1536","1359 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man walking in one sandal."
"1537","1360 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - or: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Zuhayr doubted - say: When the strap of his sandal breaks - or: Whoever's sandal strap breaks - let him not walk in one sandal until he repairs its strap, nor walk in one sock. An objector among the ignorant of reports said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you narrate from him..."
"1538","1361 - He mentioned what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Mindal narrated to us, from Layth, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, she said: I would sometimes see the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking in one sandal. He said: In this is a contradiction you should not like to attribute to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that contradiction in such is only after the chains are equivalent therein and the narrations are established. When it is otherwise, it is not as you mentioned. And some narrators of the hadith in this narration are not among those adduced as proof therein, nor among those whose narration may oppose what was narrated by those we mentioned before it in the first section of this chapter. What you mentioned from Aishah is only from the hadith of Mindal, who is not among the firmly established of those we mentioned before him in the first section of this chapter - especially since he narrated what you mentioned from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, who, though among the virtuous, his narration is not strong with the people of knowledge of chains. What is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to it, from Jabir and Abu Hurayrah, is better than people's practice, because whoever wears one sandal or one sock is thereby deemed foolish among people and they mock him. The like of this, even had there been no prohibition in it, should be avoided. And Allah we ask for success."
"1539","213 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his seeking forgiveness at Hudaybiyah for those who shaved twice and for those who shortened once. 1362 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Abdillah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, have mercy on those who shave. They said: And those who shorten, O Messenger of Allah? He said: O Allah, have mercy on those who shave. They said: And those who shorten, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And those who shorten."
"1540","1363 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Umarah ibn al-Qaqa, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive those who shave. It was said: And those who shorten? He said: O Allah, forgive those who shave. It was said: And those who shorten? He said: And those who shorten. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought forgiveness for those who shaved twice and for those who shortened once. An objector said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, permitted in His Book shaving and shortening in ihram, and described the people of Hudaybiyah entering the Sacred Mosque upon it, promising them that, saying: You will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, Allah willing, secure, shaving your heads and shortening, not fearing. So the shavers shaved by Allah's command and the shorteners shortened by Allah's command - whence were the shavers preferred therein over the shorteners? It was said to him: Due to a meaning narrated from Abdillah ibn Abbas therein."
"1541","1364 - And it is what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abi Najih narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Men shaved on the day of Hudaybiyah and others shortened. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and those who shorten? He said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and those who shorten? He said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and those who shorten? He said: And those who shorten. They said: Why did you single out those who shaved with mercy? He said: Because they did not doubt."
"1542","1365 - And what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1543","1366 - And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: Why did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) single out those who shaved thrice and those who shortened once? He said: Because they did not doubt. In what we narrated was the preferring of those who shaved over those who shortened, because they did not doubt. In that was establishing doubt from those who shortened. This objector said: What was the doubt of those who shortened in that? It was said to him: It was due to a meaning Jabir ibn Abdillah mentioned."
"1544","1367 - And it is what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yunus Abu Humah narrated to us, he said: Abu Qurrah Musa ibn Tariq narrated to us, from Zamah ibn Salih, from Ziyad ibn Sad, from Abu al-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shaved on the day of Hudaybiyah, and many of his Companions shaved when they saw him shave, while others held back and said: By Allah, we have not circumambulated the House, so they shortened. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. Men said: And those who shorten, O Messenger of Allah? He said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. Men said: And those who shorten, O Messenger of Allah? He said: May Allah have mercy on those who shave. They said: And those who shorten, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And those who shorten. In this hadith was that it was in their hearts that they saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shave in other than the place of shaving they knew shaving to be in and stood upon from his law. It was incumbent upon them that their following and emulating him in what they saw him do be firmer in their hearts than what their prior knowledge of it from him was before that. They were thereby deficient in what was due to him upon them (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. The shavers did what was incumbent upon them of complying with his deed, abandoning lagging behind emulating him. They were thereby preferred over those who lagged behind the like of it - not due to merit in shaving over shortening, but because precedence to knowledge of things necessitates merit for the preceders to them, as was necessitated for Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) by his preceding people to believing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon his coming to Bayt al-Maqdis from Makkah and his return from it to his home in Makkah in one night, when the Bedouin denied that, and upon his saying to him: Bring a witness to testify for you. When Khuzaymah testified for him with what he testified, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: How did you testify when you were not with us? He said: I testified by believing you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby made his testimony as the testimony of two men. We will mention this hadith with its chain hereafter in this book, Allah willing. All people testify to the truthfulness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but when Khuzaymah preceded them to that, he deserved merit over them therein. Like that, the shavers deserved merit over the shorteners by their preceding them to obeying the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), emulating him, taking what he gave them, the absence of doubt from their hearts in that, and their knowledge that what they witnessed from him was more deserving for them than what their prior knowledge of it from him was - although we narrated that those who shortened in that were only two men: one of Quraysh and the other of the Ansar."
"1545","1368 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us that Abu Ibrahim narrated to him, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Hudaybiyah shaved, and his Companions shaved their heads except two men: a man of the Ansar and a man of Quraysh. Abu Jafar said: We have not found this clarification in the hadith of anyone who narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir besides Ali ibn al-Mubarak. As for al-Awzai, he did not mention that in his hadith, from Yahya."
"1546","1369 - As Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Ibrahim al-Ansari, he said: Abu Said al-Khudri narrated to us, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seek forgiveness on the day of Hudaybiyah for those who shaved thrice and for those who shortened once. Abu Jafar said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak is not less than al-Awzai. And Allah we ask for success."
"1547","214 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the Mufassal of the Quran - what it is. 1370 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Harrani, meaning Suhaym, narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Habib Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: I heard Ibn Masud say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down the Mufassal upon His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah. We were pilgrims reciting it, nothing else coming down. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what indicates that Surah al-Hujurat is not of it, and that it is Madinan, because in it is Allah's prohibition of people raising their voices with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That was only in the report in which Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas al-Ansari thought of himself what he thought until he sat in his house, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him of what was the cause of his return to his sitting place. Because in it is: Do not advance before Allah and His Messenger. The cause of its revelation upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only what occurred of Abu Bakr and Umar, each one's counsel to him to appoint whom he counseled appointing, of al-Aqra ibn Habis, al-Qaqa and Mabad ibn Zurarah. We mentioned that and what was narrated therein earlier in this book. Because in it is: If a fasiq comes to you with news, verify - the verse. The cause of its revelation was concerning the one the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent as a collector to a people; they came forward to him to honor him. When he saw them coming toward him, he turned fleeing to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of them falsely. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told their true affair and Allah sent down their vindication upon him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not appoint anyone nor send a collector while in Makkah; that was only from him while in Madinah. Because in it is: And if two parties of believers fight, make peace between them - the verse. That was only due to a cause between the Ansar until they warred on account of it with what they warred, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent this down concerning that. We will mention that with its chain hereafter in this book, Allah willing. When it was negated that al-Hujurat is of the Mufassal for what we mentioned, and because al-Hujurat could only be in Madinah, its beginning was Qaf. Then we looked at what indicates that besides this hadith."
"1548","1371 - We found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yala al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abdillah ibn Aws narrated to us, from his grandfather, that he was among the delegation who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Banu Malik. Abu Jafar said: They are Banu Malik ibn Kab ibn Amr ibn Sad ibn Awf ibn Thaqif. He lodged them in a tent of his between the mosque and his family, and he would come to them and narrate to them after the last Isha. Most of what he narrated to them was his complaining of Quraysh, then he would say: Not equal; we were oppressed, humiliated in Makkah. When we came to Madinah, the fortunes of war were ours and against us. He was held back from us one night, and we said: O Messenger of Allah, you stayed from us tonight longer than usual. He said: Yes, a portion of the Quran came upon me, and I loved not to leave the mosque until I complete it. We said to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us that a portion of the Quran came upon him tonight; how did you divide the Quran into portions? They said: We divide it into three surahs, five surahs, seven surahs, nine surahs, eleven surahs, thirteen surahs, and the portion of what is between the Mufassal and below."
"1549","1372 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yala al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abdillah ibn Aws, from his grandfather Aws ibn Hudhayfah, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the delegation of Thaqif. He lodged us with him, and lodged our brethren of the allies with al-Mughirah ibn Shubah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come to us and narrate to us; most of his narration was his complaining of Quraysh. He would say: Not equal; we were humiliated, oppressed in Makkah. When we came to Madinah, war was alternate for us and against us. One night he was late to us, and we said to him. He said: A portion of the Quran came upon me, and I was dividing it into a portion. He said: I met some of his Companions and said: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divide the Quran into portions? He said: He would divide it into three, five, seven, nine, and eleven. Abu Jafar said: There dropped from this hadith of what is established in the hadith before it: And thirteen."
"1550","1373 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn al-Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yala ibn Kab al-Taifi, from Uthman ibn Abdillah ibn Aws ibn Hudhayfah, from his grandfather Aws ibn Hudhayfah, he said: I came in the delegation of Thaqif to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The allies lodged with al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lodged Banu Malik in a tent of his. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would turn to us after Isha, narrating to us standing upon his feet until he shifted between his feet due to the length of standing. Most of what he narrated to us was what he met from Quraysh. Then he would say: Not equal; we were humiliated, oppressed in Makkah. When we emigrated, the fortunes of war were between us and them; we gain over them and they gain over us. One night he was late to us beyond the time he used to come in. I said: You were late to us tonight? He said: My portion of the Quran came upon me, and I disliked coming until I complete it. Aws ibn Hudhayfah said: I asked the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How do you divide the Quran into portions? They said: Three, five, seven, nine, eleven, thirteen, and the portion of the Mufassal alone. Abu Jafar said: Abu Khalid, and he is Sulayman ibn Hayyan - we looked into it: Three surahs from the beginning of the Quran: al-Baqarah, Al Imran, and al-Nisa. Five: al-Maidah, al-Anam, al-Araf, al-Anfal, and Baraah. Seven: Yunus, Hud, Yusuf, al-Rad, Ibrahim, al-Hijr, and al-Nahl. Nine: Bani Israil, al-Kahf, Maryam, Ta-Ha, al-Anbiya, al-Hajj, al-Muminun, al-Nur, and al-Furqan. Eleven: the Tawasin, al-Ankabut, al-Rum, Luqman, al-Sajdah, al-Ahzab, Saba, Fatir, and Ya-Sin. Thirteen: al-Saffat, Sad, al-Zumar, Ha-Mim, meaning Al Ha-Mim, Surah Muhammad, al-Fath, and al-Hujurat. And the portion of the Mufassal. Abu Jafar said: In these reports we narrated is the confirmation of the affair of al-Hujurat, that it is not of the Mufassal, and that the Mufassal is what is after it to the end of the Quran."
"1551","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Suqayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr, he said: The first Mufassal of Ibn Masud was al-Rahman. Abu Jafar said: This, in our view - and Allah knows best - only came due to the difference in the arrangement of surahs with Ibn Masud and with others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who undertook writing the Quran in the era of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him and them), and it is the arrangement which is the proof. It is possible that in the arrangement of Ibn Masud, after Surah al-Rahman were Qaf, al-Dhariyat, and the other surahs between it and Surah al-Rahman, with al-Hujurat excluded from that, reverting to the like of what it is upon in the portioning of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned in the hadith of Aws ibn Hudhayfah. In the hadith of Waki which we narrated in this chapter among the hadiths of Aws ibn Hudhayfah is a wording that must be noted: his saying therein: I said: How did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divide the Quran into portions? In that is attributing the portioning to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In the hadith of others reverting to the hadith of Aws ibn Hudhayfah, Aws said: I asked the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How do you divide the Quran into portions? So he attributed the portioning to them, not to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Allah knows best how the reality is in that. And Him we ask for success."
"1552","215 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was differed over from Uthman ibn Affan and Abdillah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) concerning al-Anfal and Baraah - whether they are two surahs or one surah. 1374 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Yazid al-Farisi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I said to Uthman ibn Affan: What carried you to deliberately take al-Anfal, which is of the Mathani, and Baraah, which is of the Miin, and join them without writing between them the line: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, placing them among the seven long ones? What carried you to that? He said: Uthman said: There would come upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a period while there came down upon him surahs of those with numbers. When something came down upon him, one of those who wrote for him would enter and he would say: Put this in the surah in which such-and-such is mentioned. When verses came down upon him, he would say: Put these verses in the surah in which such-and-such is mentioned. When a verse came down upon him, he would say: Put this verse in the surah in which such-and-such is mentioned. Al-Anfal was among the first of what was sent down in Madinah, and Baraah was among the last of the Quran - Abu Jafar said: Meaning in revelation - and its story resembled its story, so I thought it was of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away without clarifying to us that it was of it. For that reason I joined them, did not write between them the line: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, and placed them among the seven long ones. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) thought they were one surah, while the verification of Ibn Abbas is that they are two surahs. If the portioning of the Quran is upon what is in the hadith of Aws ibn Hudhayfah which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, they must be two surahs as Ibn Abbas said. Their disparity in the two times in which their revelation occurred indicates they are two surahs, not one surah - for al-Anfal came down concerning Badr."
"1553","As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Abdillah al-Hammal narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I said: Surah al-Anfal? He said: It came down concerning Badr. I said: And al-Hashr? He said: It came down concerning Banu al-Nadir. Abu Jafar said: Badr was only in the year four, while Baraah is the last surah sent down."
"1554","As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Bara say: The last verse sent down: They ask you for a ruling; say: Allah gives you a ruling concerning kalalah. And the last surah sent down was Baraah. Abu Jafar said: In that is the verification of al-Bara that Baraah is a complete surah distinct from al-Anfal. This is of what is known that he (may Allah be pleased with him) did not say by opinion, since the like of it is not said by opinion; he only said it by transmission, because the like of it is only taken by transmission. There has been narrated from Abdillah ibn Abbas what enters into this meaning in which occurred the disagreement we mentioned between him and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"1555","1375 - What Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Mubashshir ibn Abdillah narrated to us, from Salim al-Aftas, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When Gabriel came down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, he knew that the surah had ended."
"1556","1376 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Said ibn Jubayr, without his mentioning it from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not know the division of the surah until: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, came down upon him. Abu Jafar said: Ibn Abbas informed in this hadith that there would come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, what he knows thereby the end of the surah. In that is what indicates that the reality in what Uthman and he (may Allah be pleased with them both) differed over, of what we mentioned their differing over, was what he said therein - due to what he stood upon of that which we narrated from him, upon which Uthman did not stand. There has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that."
"1557","1377 - And it is what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Habib ibn Hind al-Aslami, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever takes the seven is a scholar - meaning thereby the seven long ones of the Quran."
"1558","1378 - Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Amr, from Habib ibn Hind al-Aslami, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes the seven is a scholar. Do you not see that we have encompassed in knowledge that Baraah entered into that without al-Anfal, or al-Anfal entered into that without Baraah? In that is what indicates they are two surahs, not one surah. There has been narrated from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what enters into this meaning also."
"1559","1379 - What Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given the seven in place of the Torah, I was given the Miin in place of the Psalms, I was given the Mathani in place of the Gospel, and I was favored with the Mufassal. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that al-Anfal is of the Mathani, and Baraah is of the Miin, and that in that is what indicates each of them is other than its companion, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given each of them in place of what the other was given in place of, in what was mentioned in this hadith. In that is what indicates they are two surahs, not one surah. In the portioning we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter is what confirms that also. If the portioning was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is the proof which may not be opposed. If it was from his Companions (may Allah's pleasure be upon them), they are those who emulate him, follow his traces, who do not depart from what he was upon. So it is known that that, whatever was in the portioning, establishes thereby that Baraah and al-Anfal are two surahs, not one surah. Others held that their leaving the writing of: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, between al-Anfal and Baraah was for other than the meaning in the hadith of Yazid al-Farisi, from Ibn Abbas, from Uthman. They deemed it unlikely that the like of this escapes Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), due to his care for the Quran early and late until Allah took him (may Allah be pleased with him) upon that. They mention that: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, their leaving writing it between al-Anfal and Baraah was because: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, are letters of mercy, while Surah Baraah is not of this meaning of the kind intended by mercy, but rather of punishment and severity. This saying is also invalidated by what is in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and by what is in the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), repelling it. As for what is in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, repelling it: the letter of Sulayman to the queen of Saba, the letter which the queen of Saba informed her people is from Sulayman and is: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful - while she and they were polytheists. That is indicated by the saying of the hoopoe to Sulayman: I found her and her people prostrating to the sun besides Allah, Satan adorned their deeds for them and barred them from the path, so they are not guided. As for what is in the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)..."
"1560","1380 - Of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Abu Sufyan narrated to me, from his mouth, until Heraclius called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them. He read it, and in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, from Muhammad, Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, chief of Rome: Peace upon whoever follows guidance... Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. In what we mentioned is the permissibility of beginning addressing the polytheists with: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. When these other two sayings were negated, and there was nothing in this chapter besides them and besides the first saying - the first saying was established. And Allah we ask for success."
"1561","216 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning covering the private parts. 1381 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Bahz ibn Hakim informed us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, our private parts - what do we come to and what do we leave? He said: Guard your private part except from your wife or what your right hand possesses. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, when people are among one another? He said: If you can, let no one see it. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, when one of us is alone? He said: Allah is more deserving to be shy of than people."
"1562","1382 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Bahz ibn Hakim informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is more deserving to be shy of than people. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid men covering their private parts from their wives, nor from what their right hands possess."
"1563","1383 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn al-Mutamir narrated to us, from Musa - Abu Jafar said: He is Ibn Abdillah ibn Yazid al-Ansari, then al-Khatmi - from a freedwoman of Aishah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I never saw the private part of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ever. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith was what we mentioned from her therein. That, in our view - and Allah knows best - is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), though in his sunnah this was not forbidden to him, left it and applied his own practice of it. That is due to what Allah raised of his station, elevated of his rank, and made his rank surpassing the ranks of all His creation besides him. So in what he did of that covering, he was upon what befits one of this station. Those besides him among people are upon the ruling of his sunnah mentioned in the hadith of Bahz ibn Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather, from him. An objector said: How do you accept this from Aishah, while you narrate, and have with you from her, what contradicts that? And he mentioned..."
"1564","1384 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Shajari narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to me, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, she said: Zayd ibn Harithah arrived in Madinah while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in my house. He came to him and knocked on the door. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose to him naked - by Allah, I had never seen him naked before it - and kissed him and embraced him. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that this hadith of Urwah from Aishah does not contradict the hadith of her freedwoman from her which we mentioned. For what is in this is her informing that she saw him naked, and that may have been a nakedness of him in which there was no uncovering of the private part; she applied nakedness to it because most of his body was thus. The proof of this interpretation is that the rising of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith was only to meet a man whom it is not proper for him to meet with uncovered private part. In that was what indicates that the nakedness upon which he met him had no uncovering of his private part in it. What Aishah saw of him then returns to what is proper for that man to see of his body. In that is what indicates she did not see his private part then. In that is confirming what the freedwoman of Aishah narrated from Aishah of what we narrated from her in this book. And Allah we ask for success."
"1565","217 - Chapter on men divorcing their wives whom their fathers dislike - is that of their dutifulness to their fathers or not? Abu Jafar said: This meaning was problematic for Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) until he said concerning it to one who asked him about it. 1385 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Ata, and he is Ibn al-Saib, narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated to me, he said: A man among us, his mother ordered him to marry. When he married, she ordered him to part from her. He journeyed to Abu al-Darda and asked him about that. He said: I am not one to order you to divorce, nor one to order you to retain. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The mother is the middle door of Paradise; guard that door or waste it - or as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, the doubt is from Ibn Marzuq. Abu Jafar said: We thus learned that the answer was problematic for Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) in what he was asked of this. His answer therein was an answer in which nothing of retaining or parting was decided. We looked: was anything narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wherein is the reality of what is due in this meaning - what it is?"
"1566","1386 - We found Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq al-Khawlani had narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hamzah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from his father, he said: I had a wife I loved, and my father disliked her. He ordered me to divorce her, but I refused. I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O Abdillah, divorce your wife. So I divorced her."
"1567","1387 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1568","1388 - And we found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman and Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani had narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. In this hadith was what indicates that of the right of the father in this upon his son is answering his father to what he asks him of this. When that is of the right of the father upon his child, it is more obligatory of the right of a mother upon her child, more binding upon her child, because the right of the mother upon the child exceeds the right of the father upon him. That will come textually from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in its place hereafter in this book, Allah willing. Abu Jafar said: What the child is ordered with in this does not permit him therein divorcing his wife in the circumstance in which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade him divorcing her. Rather it is his divorcing her in the circumstance in which Allah permitted divorce, not in its opposite. And Allah we ask for success."
"1569","218 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning hastening death before the young generation who take the Quran as flutes, putting one of them forward to sing to them though he is the least of them in understanding. 1389 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdillah informed us, from Uthman ibn Umayr, from Zadhan Abu Umar, from Ulaym, he said: We were sitting upon a roof, with us a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Yazid said: I know him only as saying: Abs al-Ghifari - while people were leaving during the plague. Abs said: O plague, take me - saying it thrice. Ulaym said: Why do you say this? Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Let none of you wish for death, for it is at the cessation of his deeds, and he will not be returned to seek amends? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Hasten with death before six: the rule of the foolish, abundance of police, sale of judgment, treating blood lightly, severing kinship, and a young generation who take the Quran as flutes, putting one of them forward to sing to them though he is the least of them in understanding."
"1570","1390 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu al-Yaqzan, from Zadhan, from Ulaym, he said: I was with Abs al-Ghifari upon a roof. He saw people departing from the plague. He said: Who are these? It was said: They depart from the plague. He said: O plague, take me, O plague, take me. A cousin with companionship said: Why do you wish for death, when you heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Let none of you wish for death, for it is at the cessation of his deeds? Abs said to him: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, then he mentioned the rest of the first hadith. An objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when you narrated to us before it from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book that he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not give ear to anything as He gives ear to a prophet chanting the Quran - in that urging people to beautify their voices with the Quran. When that is of what they are commanded in themselves, it was evidence of their being permitted to listen to that from others. Like what was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him)..."
"1571","He mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu Salamah informed him, he said: When Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) saw Abu Musa, he would say: Remind us, O Abu Musa. He would recite with him, and Abu Musa was fine-voiced. He said: In what you narrated in this chapter is what contradicts that. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that what is in the hadith we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, of hastening with death before the young generation mentioned therein, is only due to their taking imams in prayer for their voices, while they are not fit for imamate. For the sunnah from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that the people are led by the most reciting of them of Allah's Book; if equal in recitation, the most knowing of the sunnah; if equal in the sunnah, the earliest in emigration; if equal in emigration, the eldest. We will mention that with its chain in its place hereafter in this book, Allah willing. The sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was that the people are led by whoever has this description, whether fine voice is with him or not. Whoever turns from that to other than it of fine voice turns from the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), blameworthy in his choice, of those whose likes death should meet. That is nothing of one who beautifies his voice with the Quran so his heart softens, or the hearts of his listeners soften from him. Had two joined in recitation of Allah's Book, both thereby deserving imamate from where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned their deserving it thereby, it would not be disliked to put forward the finer-voiced of them over the one without fine voice, nor would whoever does that be blamed. It became clear, by Allah's praise and help, that there is no contradiction in anything this ignorant one imagined in the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). How could that be, when Allah described him as not speaking from desire; it is only revelation revealed, taught by one mighty in power. And Allah, glorified, we ask for success."
"1572","219 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) from his saying: Every newborn is born upon fitrah - with some of its narrators uniquely adding that he said: He remains upon it until his tongue expresses from him; then his parents make him Jewish, Christian, and polytheist. 1391 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him, that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no newborn but is born upon fitrah. Then he says: Recite: The fitrah of Allah upon which He originated people; no change in Allah's creation; that is the upright religion."
"1573","1392 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every newborn is born upon fitrah; his parents make him Jewish, Christian, or Magian - like the beast produces the beast; is there among them any mutilated?"
"1574","1393 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every newborn is born upon fitrah; his parents make him Jewish, Christian, polytheist, Magian, and disbelieving. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, one who dies when born? He said: Allah knows best what they would have done. Abu Jafar said: All we narrated of these refer to Abu Hurayrah."
"1575","1394 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq al-Hilali narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: al-Aswad ibn Sari narrated, and he was the first to narrate stories in this mosque, he said: I went out on campaign with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in four expeditions. His Companions took to the offspring after killing the fighters. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was severe upon him. He said: What is with people who killed the fighters then took to the offspring? A man said: O Messenger of Allah, are they not the children of the polytheists? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your best are the children of the polytheists. Indeed no soul is born but is born upon fitrah; it remains upon it until its tongue makes clear from it; then its parents make it Jewish or Christian."
"1576","1395 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Sari ibn Yahya informed me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Except that when we examined this hadith, we found therein, he said: al-Aswad ibn Sari narrated, he said: We were in an expedition of ours; we struck and killed among the polytheists until killing reached them to kill the offspring. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: What is with people whom killing reached to kill the offspring? Shall I not kill offspring, shall I not kill offspring. It was said: Why, O Messenger of Allah, are they not the children of the polytheists? He said: Are not your best the children of the polytheists?"
"1577","1396 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Yunus al-Zayyat narrated to us. Abu Jafar said: He is the Kufan, well-known, trustworthy. And Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: al-Ashath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that al-Aswad ibn Sari narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army. They exceeded in killing the polytheists until they took to the offspring. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with people who exceeded in killing until they took to the offspring? They said: O Messenger of Allah, are they not the children of the polytheists? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are not your best the children of the polytheists? It became clear to us by these two hadiths that al-Hasan narrated what is in them, and what is in the hadith before them of the hadith of al-Aswad, from al-Aswad by hearing."
"1578","1397 - al-Harawi Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from al-Aswad, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every soul is born upon fitrah until its tongue expresses from it; then its parents make it Jewish and Christian. Abu Jafar said: We examined what was said in interpreting this hadith. We found Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz had permitted us from Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam, he said: I asked Muhammad ibn al-Hasan concerning the interpretation of this hadith - meaning the hadith of Abu Hurayrah we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter - and he said: That was at the beginning of Islam, before the obligations came down and before the Muslims were commanded with jihad. Abu Ubayd said: As if he holds that had he been born upon fitrah then died before his parents make him Jewish or Christian, they would not inherit him, for he is Muslim and they are disbelievers, nor would it then be permissible to enslave him. When the obligations came down and the practices ran contrary to that, it indicated he is born upon their religion. Abu Ubayd said: As for Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak, it reached me he was asked about its interpretation and said: Its interpretation is the other hadith, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the children of the polytheists and said: Allah knows best what they would have done - holding that they are born upon what they will come to of Islam or disbelief. Whoever was in Allah's knowledge to become Muslim is born upon fitrah; whoever His knowledge of him was to become a disbeliever dying a disbeliever. Abu Ubayd said: The two interpretations are close to one another. Abu Jafar said: We examined what we mentioned from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, to which Abu Ubayd inclined. We found in the hadith of al-Aswad ibn Sari which we narrated what repels that, because Muhammad informed that that saying was before jihad was obligated, while in the hadith of al-Aswad it was in an expedition among the expeditions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which is jihad. When they differed in this hadith's meaning upon what we mentioned, and said in its interpretation what we described, after our making it all one hadith and establishing therein his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He remains upon it until his tongue expresses from him - we considered what came of mention of fitrah in Allah's Book. We found Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said in His Book: Praise be to Allah, Originator of the heavens and earth - meaning Creator of the heavens and earth. Likewise Wallad the grammarian narrated to us, from al-Masadiri, from Abu Ubaydah. He, Mighty and Majestic, also said therein: What is with me that I should not worship He who originated me - meaning He who created me. He, Mighty and Majestic, said: The fitrah of Allah upon which He originated people - meaning the religion of Allah upon which He created people. Likewise Wallad the grammarian narrated to us, from al-Masadiri, from Abu Ubaydah, in things of these meanings. Fitrah was two fitrahs: a fitrah by which is meant the creation with which there is no worship, and a fitrah with which is the worship deserving by its doing reward and necessitating by its leaving punishment. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Every newborn is born upon fitrah, intends the fitrah whose people are addressed with worship, rewarded and punished. Its people who are thus, while immature of those created for worship, as He, Mighty and Majestic, said: I created jinn and men only to worship Me - though before their maturity reward and punishment are lifted from them. Yet when their tongues express of them something of faith or disbelief, they are of its people, though not rewarded for its praiseworthy nor punished for its blameworthy, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He remains upon it until his tongue expresses from him. For that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted the Islam of one immature, entering him among the Muslims. In that is what necessitates the exit of one among the Muslims by apostasy in that state from Islam, until he thereby deserves prevention from inheritance from his Muslim parents. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: His parents make him Jewish, Christian, or polytheist - meaning by their Judaism, Christianity, or polytheism. He is captive if his parents are belligerents, taken after maturity, being sane, with jizyah if his parents are dhimmis. This, in our view, is the interpretation of what we narrated. And Allah we ask for success."
"1579","220 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his permitting adorning the sword with silver. 1398 - Hajjaj ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The pommels of the sword of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were of silver."
"1580","1399 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim al-Kilabi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The shoe of the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silver, its pommel was silver, and what is between that were rings of silver."
"1581","1400 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The pommel of the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silver."
"1582","1401 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it."
"1583","1402 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Talut narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Kathir al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Sad narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The sword of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was Hanafi, and its pommel was silver. This Uthman ibn Sad, al-Bukhari mentioned he is Basran, Tamimi, kunya Abu Bakr, known as al-Katib, and that Shubah, Abu Asim, and this Yahya ibn Kathir ibn Dirham narrate from him. Abu Jafar said: In what we mentioned, using silver in this is like using it in rings. That is evidence that the disliked, forbidden use of silver is like the non-Arabs' use of it - eating in it, drinking in it, and what they took as vessels for them as they take copper and iron, nothing besides that. There has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) and from his son Abdillah ibn Umar, of their deeds, what enters into this chapter, what..."
"1584","Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, he said: Shababah narrated to us - from Shubah, from Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The pommel of Umar's sword was of silver, and Ibn Umar would wear it."
"1585","And what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he would wear Umar's sword, and it was adorned."
"1586","Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Usamah narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mighwal, he said: Umar's sword was adorned with silver. I said to Nafi: Did Umar adorn it? He said: I do not know; I saw Ibn Umar wearing it."
"1587","The like of that has been narrated from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him). As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and al-Asmai narrated to us, they said: Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Wahshiyyah al-Sayqal narrated to us, he said: Musab ibn al-Zubayr sent to us, and brought out to us two swords: one of them thin, its ring silver. He said: This is the sword of al-Siddiq; this is the sword of Abu Bakr."
"1588","The like of that has also been narrated from al-Zubayr. As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Hammad, meaning Ibn Zayd, narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I saw the sword of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam adorned with silver. And Allah we ask for success."
"1589","221 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his using silver as a nose-ring for his sacrificial animal. 1403 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad, and he is Ibn al-Awwam, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Yahya, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gifted the camel of Abu Jahl while in Makkah in the year of Hudaybiyah, and in its head was a ring of silver."
"1590","1404 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abdillah ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gifted in the Umrah of Hudaybiyah the camel of Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, upon it a halter of gold - and it is the bridle. He said: But the bridle is in the flesh, and the khishash is in the bone. He did that only to enrage Quraysh thereby. Abu Jafar said: We imagined that Abu Yahya in the first hadith is Ibn Abi Najih in this hadith, until we stood upon the kunya of Ibn Abi Najih - he is Abu Yasar, a client of Thaqif. We thereby understood that Abu Yahya in the first hadith is al-Qattat. The speech we brought in the chapter before this suffices us from speech in this chapter. This hadith has been narrated from Ibn Abbas from another face."
"1591","1405 - And it is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gifted one hundred sacrificial camels, among them a camel of Abu Jahl with a ring of silver in its nose."
"1592","222 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his commanding the one whose nose was struck off to take in its place a nose of gold. 1406 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ghassan ibn Ubayd al-Mawsili narrated to us. And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us. And Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, and al-Khasib ibn Nasih and Asad ibn Musa. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us. They all said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tarafah, from his grandfather Arfajah ibn Asad, that his nose was struck off on the day of Kulab in the pre-Islamic era. He took a nose of silver, and it rotted upon him. He complained of that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who commanded him to take a nose of gold, and he did."
"1593","1407 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, from Salm ibn Zarir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tarafah, from Arfajah ibn Asad, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1594","1408 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Salm ibn Zarir narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tarafah, that Arfajah ibn Asad's nose was struck off on the day of Kulab in the pre-Islamic era. Then he mentioned this hadith. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the man mentioned therein to take, in place of his nose which was struck off, a nose of gold, when he complained to him that the nose he had taken before that of silver rotted upon him. An objector said: Was this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before his forbidding wearing gold or after his forbidding wearing it? For wearing gold was permissible, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it thereafter for men."
"1595","1409 - And he mentioned in that what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Abdillah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a ring of gold, placing its stone toward his palm. People took it up, so he threw it away and took a ring of silver."
"1596","1410 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1597","1411 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik ibn Anas, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear a gold ring, then he said: so he cast it away and said: I will never wear it again, so the people cast away their rings. He (al-Tahawi) said: In this hadith is the wearing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of a gold ring when it was permissible in this hadith, and his casting it away afterwards when wearing it became unlawful. If his command to Arfajah to take a nose of gold was in the time of permissibility of wearing gold, then you have no proof in it for permitting the like of it now in the time of prohibition of wearing gold, nor do you have any evidence that what he did for Arfajah was after his prohibition of wearing gold. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that we only brought this hadith of Arfajah after the proof stood with us that the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Arfajah of what he permitted him of what is mentioned in his hadith was after his prohibition of wearing gold for men. That is because Arfajah, before his complaint to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he mentioned in his hadith, if wearing gold had been permissible for him he would have been free of asking the ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), since he knew silver and knew rust overtakes it causing stench when used where he used it, and that gold is unlike that since rust that causes stench does not overtake it as it does silver; or if he did not know that, he could have asked someone other than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) since people are equal in that. Since he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining, seeking that he permit him to take what would not stink upon him when placed where he needed to place it, that shows his need was about the ruling in religion, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him as he did and commanded him as he commanded. This is a question over which scholars differed, namely binding loose teeth with what is needed of silver or gold. From Abu Hanifah two different sayings are reported about binding them with gold: one is dislike of that, as Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from Yaqub, from Abu Hanifah to that effect, without recounting any disagreement; the other is what Jafar ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Kindi informed us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf say: Abu Hanifah said: there is no harm in binding them with gold, without recounting disagreement. In both narrations from Abu Hanifah it is that there is no harm... and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in his own view, in the narration of Muhammad ibn al-Abbas, said: there is no harm in binding them with gold. And permission to bind them with gold is reported from more than one of the early predecessors."
"1598","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad and Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Hammad, he said: I saw al-Mughirah ibn Abdullah, the governor of Kufa, had capped his teeth with gold, so I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: there is no harm in it."
"1599","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us. And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan and Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Tumah ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: I saw the yellowness of gold between the front teeth - or he said: the two front teeth - of Musa ibn Talhah."
"1600","And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Nashiti narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, he said: I saw al-Hasan binding his teeth with gold."
"1601","And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Awn al-Uqayli, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah had reached old age, children were born to him, his teeth fell out, so they were restored with a chain of gold."
"1602","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and as Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I saw Abu al-Tayyah and Abu Jamrah and Abu Nawfal ibn Abi Aqrab, they had capped their teeth with gold."
"1603","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Irfan ibn Asim ibn Irfan al-Bazzaz al-Basri narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, he said: I saw Yazid al-Rishk with his teeth laced with gold."
"1604","And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: I saw Ubaydullah ibn al-Hasan, the judge of Basra, had bound his teeth with gold."
"1605","And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Suwayd ibn Manjuf narrated to us, he said: I saw Abu Rafi with his teeth laced with gold. Abu Umayyah said to us: and I saw Badal ibn al-Muhabbar and Hawdhah ibn Khalifah and Ibrahim ibn Ziyad - Sabalan - with their teeth laced with gold. Abu Jafar said: and we know of no disagreement with this view from any of the early predecessors except what we mentioned from Abu Hanifah of the view opposed therein by scholars, especially since there was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that the permission to Arfajah which we narrated in this chapter. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1606","223 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about drinking in gold and silver vessels, and whether wooden vessels patched with silver enter into that or not. 1412 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Humayd, he said: I saw with Anas the cup of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which there was silver, or which was mended with silver. Abu Jafar said: It is possible that was done by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to it; if so, it contains the greatest proof for its permissibility. And if that was done by Anas ibn Malik after him, then it shows that in the view of Anas ibn Malik there is no harm in drinking from a vessel like that. So for those among scholars who permit this, there is the view of a distinguished learned Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith is also reported from Anas ibn Malik with an addition to this meaning."
"1607","1413 - As Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Sulayman Allan our neighbor narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sayyar al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman Abdan narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas, he said: The cup of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke, so he put in place of the crack a chain of silver. Asim said: and I saw the cup and drank from it. An objector said: How do you accept this, when you have reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)...?"
"1608","1414 - He mentioned what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik ibn Anas informed him, from Nafi, from Zayd ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, from Umm Salamah the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who drinks in a silver vessel only gulps the fire of Hell into his belly."
"1609","1415 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, he said: Ismail - meaning Ibn Ulayyah - narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Zayd ibn Abdullah, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. Then he mentioned after that what is reported from Abdullah ibn Umar about drinking from a silver-plated vessel, disliking it, because of what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about drinking in gold and silver vessels."
"1610","1416 - It is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Khusayf ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he was brought a silver-plated cup to drink from and refused to drink. Nafi said: Indeed Ibn Umar, since he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid drinking in gold and silver vessels, would not drink in a silver-plated cup. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is: What Ibn Umar would not drink from - the silver-plated vessel - is not part of what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at all; for what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only his prohibition of drinking in silver vessels, and all Muslims agree on that without differing. What we brought this chapter for is what they differ over: drinking from a wooden vessel in which there is silver like a patch and the like. Some permit that, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions, and some dislike it and forbid it, among them al-Shafii, just as before them Abdullah ibn Umar and Anas ibn Malik differed: Anas ibn Malik permitted it, and Abdullah ibn Umar prohibited it. The view of neither is worthier than the other except by proof. We have mentioned about the cup of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter what shows that the worthier of the two views is that of Anas ibn Malik. We find the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing silk but excluded silk emblems in garments of linen and cotton from pure silk. Likewise his prohibition of drinking in silver vessels excludes drinking in wooden vessels containing silver nails and patches. And there is also reported from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, what supports what we mentioned."
"1611","1417 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing gold, so she said: O Messenger of Allah, what about the bracelet, is it bound with gold? He said: No, but make it of silver, and yellow it with saffron. In this is proof that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted using silver as a bracelet, and did not forbid that as he forbade using it pure and worn as what is made for bracing is worn. And it is reported from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman and al-Bara ibn Azib, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what he forbade of silver, and Hudhayfah mentioned gold in his hadith."
"1612","1418 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, each of them said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: Hudhayfah asked for drink at al-Madain, so a dihqan brought him a silver vessel, so he threw it away, then said: I had forbidden him from it but he refused to stop; indeed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking in gold and silver vessels, and wearing silk and dibaj, and said: Leave it for them in this world, and it is for you in the Hereafter."
"1613","1419 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1614","1420 - And as Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from Muawiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us silk and dibaj, and drinking in gold and silver vessels. Abu Jafar said: In this is the prohibition by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of drinking in gold and silver vessels. Drinking in wooden vessels mixed with gold and silver is not part of this at all. It was the view of Abdullah ibn Umar that a little silk mixed in a non-silk garment made wearing it disliked, just as if it were all silk. Others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed him in that and permitted what he prohibited. Among what is reported from him, may Allah be pleased with him, of what we mentioned:"
"1615","1421 - What Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad, he narrated from Abu Umar, freedman of Asma, he said: I saw Ibn Umar buy a jubbah with a red thread in it, so he returned it. So I came to Asma and mentioned that to her, and she said: Woe to Ibn Umar. O slave-girl, hand me the jubbah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So she brought out to us a jubbah edged at the opening, sleeves and slit with dibaj. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that Ibn Umar disliked a jubbah not of silk because of the silk thread in it as he would dislike it if it were all silk? Likewise was his view about a non-silver vessel containing some silver: he disliked it as he would if it were all silver. Asma opposed him in that and argued with him by the jubbah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which was not of dibaj but edged with dibaj. She, may Allah be pleased with her, would not have argued with him except that she knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used it after his prohibition of using the like if it were all silk. Abdullah ibn Abbas also opposed him in that, and he reported in that:"
"1616","1422 - What Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan and Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, they said: Sharik informed us, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade the garment that is solid - meaning of silk. As for the warp and the emblem, then no. Abu Jafar said: So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, informed of what is intended by the prohibition of silk for men: that it is what is wholly silk, and that what is non-silk mixed with silk like emblems is outside of that, not forbidden. That is worthier with us than what we reported from Ibn Umar contradicting it, because in this is information of what is intended by the prohibition, that it is other than what Ibn Umar disliked of it. And there is reported from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in this chapter what is even clearer than this."
"1617","As Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Wabarah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amir, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: We came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wearing garments of the people of Persia - or he said: of Kisra - so he said: May Allah estrange these faces. He said: So we returned, cast them off, wore Arab garments and returned to him, and he said: You are better than people who came to me wearing garments of a people which, had Allah approved them for them, He would not have dressed them in them. Nothing is lawful of it except two fingers or three or four, meaning silk. Abu Jafar said: This is Umar saying this, and in it is proof that the like of silk in that is the silver whose use as vessels for drinking was forbidden: when it is a vessel, drinking in what is of wood mixed with silver for nails and patches does not enter into that."
"1618","And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, he said: I saw Salim ibn Abdullah brought drink in a silver-plated cup and he refused it, so he was brought a non-plated cup and drank."
"1619","Jarir said: And Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to me, from the daughter of Abu Amr, freedman of Aishah, she said: Aishah refused to permit us silver-plating vessels. So an objector said: This contradicts what you reported from Mujahid from Aishah about the bracelet in the earlier part of this chapter. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help, is that the matter is as he mentioned, but this daughter of Abu Amr is not like Mujahid from Aishah, since we never heard mention of her in other than this hadith, and she is not one with whom Mujahid can be countered, given Mujahid's eminent status in narration and great status in jurisprudence. As for what is in this hadith of Salim's dislike of what he disliked, it follows what he stood upon of his father's view therein of dislike. Had he stood upon his grandfather's view about silk applied to silver - contrary to his father's view - his grandfather's view would have been worthier with him than his father's. And Allah knows best. Salim was opposed in what he held by more than one of his peers among the Tabiin, among them Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him."
"1620","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Jabir, that he saw Muhammad ibn Ali drinking from a silver-plated cup and he gave him to drink from it. And among them is Tawus."
"1621","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi narrated to me, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: Tawus asked for drink, so he was brought a vessel patched with silver, and he said: Why was this put? Is it for a break? I said: No. He said: So he drank and handed to me."
"1622","Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah narrated to me, he said: I saw Tawus drinking from a vessel patched with silver. And among them is Ibrahim al-Nakhai."
"1623","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, that he saw no harm in drinking from a silver-plated cup so long as one does not put his mouth on the silver. And among them are al-Hakam ibn Utaybah and Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman."
"1624","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam and Hammad, that they saw no harm in drinking from a silver-plated cup."
"1625","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, he said: We know no harm in a silver-plated cup. And among them are al-Hasan and Abu al-Aliyah."
"1626","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: al-Hasan used to drink between the two patches. Qatadah said: And Abu al-Aliyah saw no harm in it, and Ibn Umar disliked it. We have mentioned earlier in this chapter what reasoning indicates about this disputed meaning: that it is as those who permit it said, not as their opponents said."
"1627","224 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about intercourse with a pregnant captive while she is so. 1423 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Khumayr narrated to me, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrating from his father, from Abu al-Darda, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a woman near a tent or pavilion in advanced pregnancy, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps the owner of this will approach her; I intended to curse him with a curse that would enter the grave with him. How can he make him inherit when he is not lawful for him, and how can he enslave him when he is not lawful for him. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is: how can he make him inherit when he is not lawful for him. In that is proof that by his intercourse with her mother while pregnant with him he does not become his son, as some interpreted that in it is proof his lineage attaches to him along with the one from whom the pregnancy began; for whoever says that makes the child inherit from both parents to whom his lineage attaches. And in this hadith: how can he make him inherit when he is not lawful for him. Then we sought this hadith from other than this route to find agreement or disagreement with what Shubah narrated."
"1628","1424 - We found Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi and Fahd ibn Sulayman both, they narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Asad ibn Wadaah, from a man he named among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and Asad was old and approved - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at a pregnant woman among the captives at Khaybar, and said: To whom does this belong? They said: To so-and-so. He said: Does he have intercourse with her? They said: Yes. He said: I intended to curse him with a curse reaching him in his grave. Woe to him, would he make him inherit when he is not from him, or enslave him when he nourished him in his hearing and sight. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is other than in the first hadith, namely his saying: would he make him inherit when he is not from him. In that is negation of any share for him in lineage. Or enslave him when he nourished him in his hearing and sight: in that is proof of forbidding enslaving him because of his intercourse with her mother while pregnant. Mak-hul used to hold that this child is freed upon the one who had intercourse with its mother while pregnant. As:"
"1629","Fahd ibn Sulayman and Harun ibn Kamil both narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me that he asked Yahya ibn Said about him - meaning one from whom came the like of what is in this hadith - and he said: Her child is not freed. And Makhul said: Her child is freed. Among what shows us that Makhul took this view from this hadith we narrated in this chapter:"
"1630","1425 - That Fahd and Harun narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abu Bakr - Abu Jafar said, he is Ibn Abi Maryam - from Makhul, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a slave-girl a man had bought while she was pregnant, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you have intercourse with her while she is pregnant? He said: Yes. He said: You nourish in his hearing and sight, so when he is born, free him; for his ownership is not lawful for you. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade intercourse with a pregnant woman. Abu Jafar said: Meaning: pregnant from other than the one seeking intercourse. But in this hadith is what contradicts Makhul's view we reported, that her child is freed; for in this is that he commanded that her child be freed, showing before he frees him he is not free. Yet it is possible that what we reported from Makhul, his saying her child is freed, was not ضبط by the one we took it from, and in reality it is: he frees her child - that he newly frees him after his mother's birth - so his view and what he reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agree. Abu Jafar said: It is also possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by freeing that child out of caution lest what appeared of pregnancy from her be not in reality so, then he had intercourse and she conceived from him, so he disliked enslavement for that and recommended freeing out of caution that he be his son without lineage attaching, since it was not certain he is his son. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1631","225 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who touches pebbles in prayer. 1426 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from his uncle, he said: al-Ahwas or Abu al-Ahwas narrated to me in the gathering of Said ibn al-Musayyib - Yaqub said: I think it is Abu al-Ahwas - he said: Abu Dharr said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you stands for prayer, let him not move pebbles, for mercy faces him."
"1632","1427 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abu Dharr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you stands for prayer, mercy faces him, so let him not wipe pebbles."
"1633","1428 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Nasr informed us, he said: Abdullah - meaning Ibn al-Mubarak - informed us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas, freedman of Banu Layth, narrating to us in the gathering of Ibn al-Musayyib - with Ibn al-Musayyib sitting - that he heard Abu Dharr say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty remains facing the servant in his prayer so long as he does not turn; when he turns his face away, He turns away. Then we found from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission to wipe once in prayer."
"1634","1429 - As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Dharr, he said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about everything, even I asked him about wiping pebbles. He said: Once, or leave."
"1635","1430 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Muayqib narrated to me, he said: I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): One who wipes pebbles in prayer? He said: If you must do it, then once only."
"1636","1431 - And as Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, he said: Muayqib narrated to me, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Wiping pebbles? He said: If you must do it, then once."
"1637","1432 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Muayqib, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about wiping, and he said: If you must do it, then once. In this hadith is proof that the permitted single wipe is for necessity, not otherwise."
"1638","1433 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Shurahbil. Abu Jafar said: he is Ibn Sad, kunya Abu Sad - from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For one of you to withhold his hand from pebbles is better for him than to have a hundred she-camels, all black-eyed. If Satan overcomes one of you, let him wipe once. Abu Jafar said: This hadith makes clear that the single wipe the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the praying one is only at necessity, not otherwise. For the praying one stands before his Lord, as befits such in humility, lowliness, neediness, emptying his heart for what he is in, with no distraction from completing his prayer nor haste from perfecting it. Wiping pebbles is departure from that. In that is proof of its prohibition for him and forbidding it except at overwhelming necessity - when his heart is busied by it, so wiping until that ceases is lighter than persisting in it and being overcome. In what we mentioned is proof that one intending prayer should level pebbles before entering it, to be free of that in prayer, needing nothing of it, with his heart unbusied. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1639","226 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever sleeps past his portion, or part of it, and recites it between dawn and noon prayer, it is written for him as if he recited it at night. 1434 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that al-Saib ibn Yazid and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed him, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sleeps past his portion, or part of it, and recites it between dawn prayer and noon prayer, it is written for him as if he recited it at night."
"1640","1435 - Harun ibn Kamil ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, then he mentioned the like of it in its chain and text."
"1641","1436 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: Abu Safwan Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, that al-Saib ibn Yazid and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed him, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... and he mentioned the like of it."
"1642","An objector said: This hadith Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak reported from Yunus ibn Yazid as mawquf. He mentioned what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Saib ibn Yazid and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, they informed him, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say... then he mentioned the like of it, except he did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but stopped it at Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: In this is disagreement entering this hadith's chain. It was said to him: Has anything entered requiring the correctness of Ibn al-Mubarak's narration and dropping others'? If Ibn al-Mubarak in stopping it at Umar is a proof, then al-Layth, Abdullah ibn Wahb and Abu Safwan are worthier to be proof in raising it, especially as they are three who reported it from Yunus as marfu, and three are worthier of memorization than one. He said: Mamar reported it from al-Zuhri, stopping it at Umar as well."
"1643","And he mentioned what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rafi informed us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that Ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said... and he mentioned the like of it, without raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: This confirms Ibn al-Mubarak's stopping of this hadith. It was said to him: Even though Mamar stopped it at Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, Uqayl ibn Khalid raised it from Umar to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1644","1437 - As Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh informed us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, he said: Ibn Shihab said: al-Saib ibn Yazid ibn Ukht Namir and Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it exactly. So this hadith returns as marfu to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Uqayl ibn Khalid's hadith, and in the majority's hadiths from Yunus ibn Yazid. The one opposing them in raising it, stopping it at Umar, is one - Mamar. Two are worthier of memorization than one, especially as each of them alone reporting a hadith would be accepted. When so, their addition of raising over others must be accepted. What is intended from this hadith, which we must explain in this chapter, is that night prayer was obligatory upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Muslims by Allah's saying: O you wrapped up, stand the night except little, half of it or lessen a little, or add to it and recite the Quran distinctly. Allah Almighty said: Indeed your Lord knows you stand less than two-thirds of the night and half and a third, and a group of those with you. That was their obligation therein. Then Allah abrogated that by His saying: He knew you would not count it, so He turned to you. His turning to them therein was lifting that obligation from them. And it is reported in that:"
"1645","1438 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, from Hisham - meaning al-Dastuwai - from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, that Sad ibn Hisham asked Aishah: O Mother of the Believers, inform us about the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) night prayer. She said: Do you not recite: O you wrapped up. He said: I said: Yes. She said: The beginning of the surah was revealed, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions stood until their feet swelled, and its ending was withheld twelve months in heaven. Then the concession came down: night prayer became voluntary after being obligatory. Abu Jafar said: Then Allah Almighty said: So recite what is easy of the Quran. That with us - and Allah knows best - is that He did not leave them without urging a share of night prayer for its merit and the reward attained thereby from Him. He explained in lifting its obligation in another verse, His saying to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And from the night, pray tahajjud as extra for you; perhaps your Lord will raise you to a praised station. That is the noblest reward. If night prayer for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is voluntary, it is worthier to be so for his nation. When Allah returned what He urged of night prayer to what He returned it, He increased His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his nation in latitude therein, since illness, travel or other interrupters might cut them off for part of the day. So He made recitation therein like recitation at night, as favor and mercy from Him to them, and increase to what conveys them to His praised promise and reward. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1646","227 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Religion is sincere advice, and his answer to one who said to him: To whom, O Messenger of Allah? 1439 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Religion is sincere advice - three times. It was said: To whom, O Messenger of Allah? He said: To Allah Almighty, His Book, His Messenger, the leaders of the Muslims and their common folk."
"1647","1440 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to me, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, and from Sumayy, and from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it."
"1648","1441 - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Said al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Ajlan narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, and from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it."
"1649","1442 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Tamim al-Dari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This chain is among what scholars of chains say Ali ibn Qadim erred in: he inserted therein Abu Suhayl, who is Abu Salih, between Suhayl and Ata ibn Yazid. They say the origin of this chain is from Suhayl from Ata himself."
"1650","1443 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to us, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Tamim al-Dari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Among what proves what they said in that:"
"1651","1444 - What Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Religion is sincere advice, then he mentioned the like of it without mentioning after Abu Salih. He took to narrating it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Sufyan said: So I met Suhayl ibn Abi Salih and said: A hadith Amr ibn Dinar narrated to me from al-Qaqa from your father - did you hear it from him? He said: What is it? I said: The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Religion is sincere advice. Suhayl said: I heard it from the one your father heard it from. He said: I heard a man from Syria called Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi narrating it to my father from Tamim al-Dari, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Religion is sincere advice... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: That shows the origin of the hadith from Abu Salih is from Ata ibn Yazid from Tamim - unless Abu Salih heard it from Ata ibn Yazid and also from Abu Hurayrah. Abdullah ibn Nafi reported this hadith from Malik from Suhayl, differing from people in its chain."
"1652","1445 - As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: I read to Abdullah ibn Nafi, he said: Malik informed me, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the hadith just as we mentioned."
"1653","1446 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Tamim al-Dari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned this hadith as Fahd mentioned it from Abu Ghassan from Zuhayr from Suhayl. Abu Jafar said: It became strong in hearts that the origin of this hadith from Suhayl is as Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah and Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated it from him, not as others narrated - especially as Ibn Uyaynah clarified about him what we mentioned from Bakkar from Ibrahim ibn Bashshar in this chapter. We found this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than Abu Hurayrah and Tamim al-Dari."
"1654","1447 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hammam al-Dallal narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam and Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Religion is sincere advice... then he mentioned the rest like his hadith from Safwan we mentioned in this chapter. So an objector said: How do you accept and authenticate this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with: Religion is sincere advice? How can religion be sincere advice, when you find Allah Almighty said in His Book: Indeed religion with Allah is Islam. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not contradict what he recited from Allah's Book; for sincere advice is part of Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledged upon it those he pledged upon Islam."
"1655","1448 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, he said: I heard Jarir ibn Abdullah say: I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon hearing and obedience and advice to every Muslim. Jarir said: And I am indeed an adviser to you."
"1656","1449 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, he said: I witnessed Jarir ibn Abdullah... then he mentioned from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. In what we mentioned is proof that sincere advice is part of Islam. This objector said: Is it all of Islam which is religion, as in these reports you narrated in this chapter? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is: It is not all of religion, but it holds an eminent place in religion. Everything eminent of a genus may be given the name by which that genus is called, mentioned thereby as that genus is mentioned. Of that is your saying: people are the Arabs, though among them are non-Arabs, due to the eminence of Arabs among people and their distinction by their خاصية; so it is allowed to say: they are the people. Of that is their saying: wealth is date-palms, due to the eminence of date-palms among properties, though properties include other than date-palms. Likewise the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Religion is sincere advice, is due to the eminent place of advice in religion, though religion includes other than it. This objector said: What is the meaning of his saying in those reports: and to His Book? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that with us it is teaching His Book and advising those taught it in their teaching what they need to know of its clear and ambiguous parts, what they act upon and what they stop at; for people in early Islam learned the Quran thus."
"1657","1450 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to me, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ibn Masud, he said: We would learn from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ten verses, and we would not learn the ten after them until we learned the deeds revealed in these ten."
"1658","1451 - And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Our companions who taught us informed us, they said: We would learn ten verses, and not pass beyond them until we knew the deeds therein."
"1659","1452 - And as Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih al-Harithi narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Our companions would recite to us and teach us and inform us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite to one of them ten verses, and he would not pass beyond them until he learned the deeds therein. He said: And they said: We learned Quran and deeds together."
"1660","1453 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Qasim ibn Awf, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar say: We lived a time when one of us was given faith before Quran, and a surah would come down upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he would learn its lawful and unlawful, its commands and prohibitions, and what ought to be stopped at therein, as you today learn Quran. Then I have seen today men given Quran before faith: one recites from its opening to its end, not knowing its commands nor prohibitions nor what ought to be stopped at, scattering it like inferior dates. In what we reported is how people were taught Quran and how they took it, and the hardship therein for teacher and learner evident to hearers of these reports. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed the asker about the advice mentioned in these reports - to whom it is. In that is advice to Allah's Book, and advice to it is advice to those taking it in learning from the one they take it from. In what we mentioned is explanation of this meaning. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1661","228 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Time has turned as it was the day Allah Almighty created the heavens and the earth. 1454 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Zayd informed me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed in the Farewell Pilgrimage, saying: Time has turned as it was the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. The year is twelve months, of them four sacred: three consecutive: Dhu al-Qadah, Dhu al-Hijjah, al-Muharram, and the other Rajab between Jumada and Shaban."
"1662","1455 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih, he said: I read to Ibn Nafi, he said: Malik informed me, from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dili, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed in the Farewell Pilgrimage... then he mentioned the like of it, without mentioning in its chain after Thawr ibn Zayd anyone."
"1663","1456 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Bakrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, and he mentioned the like of it letter for letter, except he said: And Rajab of Mudar between Jumada and Shaban."
"1664","1457 - Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: al-Salt ibn Masud al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tufawi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Arabs would make one year a month and one year two months, so they would not hit Hajj in Hajj days except once in twenty-five years. That is the nasi mentioned by Allah in His Book. When Abu Bakr performed Hajj with the people, that year coincided with Hajj, so Allah called it the Greater Hajj. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj the next year, and people faced the crescents. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Time has turned as it was the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith of Jafar traced to Abdullah ibn Amr is proof of time's turning until it became as it was the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. In it is the meaning intended by Allah's saying: And an announcement from Allah and His Messenger to the people on the day of the Greater Hajj, that His saying: Greater in this verse is an adjective of Hajj, not of other than it over which people differed. Some said: It is the day of sacrifice, though they reported that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1665","1458 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad - meaning Ibn Sirin - from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, he said: When that day came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed. He mentioned the Prophet's address that day, in it: Which day is this day of yours? He said: So we fell silent until we thought he would name it by other than its name. Then he said: Is it not the day of the Greater Hajj."
"1666","1459 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his address that day, that the day of the Greater Hajj is the day of sacrifice."
"1667","1460 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Some said: The day of the Greater Hajj is the day of Arafah, with no narration therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); they only reported it from Ibn Abi Awfa. The meaning of the two hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned in this chapter is what is in the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr we reported therein: his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the day of the Greater Hajj is an adjective of Hajj, not of the day, so the meanings of these reports hold together and agree, none contradicting another. An objector said: You have reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts this. He mentioned:"
"1668","1461 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi al-Bahrani narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, that Abu Hurayrah said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sent me among those announcing on the day of sacrifice at Mina that no polytheist shall perform Hajj after this year, and no naked one shall circumambulate the House. And the day of the Greater Hajj is the day of sacrifice; the Greater Hajj is Hajj. It was only called the Greater Hajj because of people's saying: the Lesser Hajj. He said: In this hadith is that the day of the Greater Hajj is the day of sacrifice. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that what in this hadith is verified to be from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not contradict what we reported before it in this chapter; for his saying: and the day of the Greater Hajj is the day of sacrifice may mean Greater is an adjective of Hajj, not of the day, agreeing with the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr we reported in this chapter, the day being annexed to it, so all these reports hold sound without contradiction. Then this objector said: In this hadith is: it was only called the Greater Hajj because of people's saying: the Lesser Hajj. So he inferred that Hajj of that year was called the Greater Hajj because of people's saying: the Lesser Hajj. He said: This contradicts the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amr you reported. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that what in this hadith of people's saying the Lesser Hajj is unknown what it is, nor from which narrator it is reported. It may be from al-Zuhri's words, for he often mixed his own words with hadith so it is thought part of it though not. Hence Musa ibn Uqbah said to him: Separate the Messenger of Allah's words from your own. Since that speech bears what we mentioned, what we reported from Abdullah ibn Amr of the true meaning is worthier. What he said is intelligible: when after time's turning Hajj returned to a specific month upon which people's Hajj runs to the Day of Judgment, it became an imam for them; it was the Greater among Hajj to which other Hajj after it to the Day of Judgment returns, by its people's following what is therein. In that is established what Abdullah ibn Amr said therein. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1669","229 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: A believer is not stung from a hole twice. 1462 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb and Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer is not stung from a hole twice. In Ayyub's hadith: from one hole."
"1670","1463 - And Muhammad ibn Uzayr al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Muhammad ibn Muslim - meaning al-Zuhri - that Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to him, that Abu Hurayrah informed him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The believer is not stung from one hole twice."
"1671","1464 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found all who narrated it to us of those mentioned in this chapter and others not mentioned narrated it to us: Let no believer be stung from a hole twice, with jazm: yuldagh. That with us - and Allah knows best - is on its apparent: it is a command. Some took its meaning that no punishment for a sin he committed is doubled upon a believer. For jazm occurring here makes it a command, nothing else. Of that is Allah Almighty's saying: No, do not obey him; prostrate and draw near; and His saying: And do not obey from them a sinner or disbeliever, and many likes in the Quran. Others rejected that upon its sayers, saying: The origin of the hadith is: No believer is stung from a hole twice, with yuldaghu, making it a report, like Allah's saying: And no bearer bears another's burden; and His saying: And He fears not its outcome; and His saying: They hear therein no idle talk - all as reports with rafa. They argued against the first party: Had the interpretation been as you say, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have needed to single out the believer; for punishment of a disbeliever's sin is not doubled either, nor a hypocrite's. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled out the believer because he explains thereby a meaning other than hypocrite and disbeliever: when a sin comes from him it grieves him and he fears its sequel, causing him never to return to it. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said therefore: The believer is not stung from a hole twice - i.e., he does not commit a sin fearing its punishment then return to it after. They made his saying: the believer is not stung from a hole twice, in the meaning of: the believer is not to be stung from a hole twice; and so are the verses we recited from Allah's Book in this meaning: they mean laysa. This with us - and Allah knows best - is the likelier of the two senses in this chapter. I heard Yunus say after narrating this hadith: I said to Ibn Wahb: What is its explanation? He said: A man falls into something he dislikes and does not return to it. That was summary from Ibn Wahb, its meaning upon the sense we inclined to: though he did not mention its parsing, he held its meaning requiring rafa, not jazm. What also supports what we mentioned is that Allah mentioned in His Book the repentance He commanded His believing servants, saying: O you who believe, turn to Allah with sincere repentance."
"1672","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Israil ibn Yunus, from Simak - he is Ibn Harb - he said: I heard al-Numan - he is Ibn Humayd - say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Sincere repentance is that a man avoids the evil deed he used to do, turning to Allah Almighty from it, then never returns to it. That shows what we mentioned of this hadith's interpretation. Of that is what is reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about remorse that it is repentance."
"1673","1465 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam informed me, from Abdullah ibn Maqil, he said: I entered with my father upon Abdullah ibn Masud, and my father said to him: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Remorse is repentance? He said: Yes."
"1674","1466 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: And Ibn Wahb narrated it to us, from Malik, from Abd al-Karim, from a man, from his father, from Ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it. So remorse for that prevents return to its like, proving what we mentioned. And Allah is the granter of success."
"1675","230 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: People are but like a hundred camels among which you hardly find a riding camel. 1467 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Numan ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People are but like a hundred camels among which you find no riding camel."
"1676","1468 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah narrated to me, that Abdullah ibn Umar said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: People are but like a hundred camels among which a riding camel is hardly seen."
"1677","1469 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah - meaning Ibn al-Mubarak - informed us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... and he mentioned the like of it."
"1678","1470 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Muhammad al-Kufi informed us, from Sufyan, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what we mentioned from him therein. Its apparent is generality to all people, but we understood he did not intend them all thereby; for among them are those who carry for others what the praised among people carry for the rest - like riding camels distinguished by carrying from other camels that do not carry. An objector said: Is it allowed in language for something said of people to intend a خاص subset دون the rest? It was said to him: Yes, this is allowed therein. Allah Almighty said: Those to whom people said: people have gathered against you, so fear them. In that is His mention of the sayers by people, and His mention of those gathered by people also, while there are other people - those to whom that saying was said. Since that is allowed in language as we described, it is also allowed that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: People are like a hundred camels intends a خاص subset: those with no benefit in them nor use for others - like a hundred camels with no riding camel carrying what people need carried. Camels with no riding camel are like people with no benefit of knowledge taken from them nor other needs one people seek from others. And among people besides them - by Allah's praise and favor - are many in guiding people to their right course, teaching them their religion, directing them in their affairs, and bearing burdens for them. This is also reported from Ibn Umar from another route with other wordings than these:"
"1679","1471 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman ibn Affan, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People are like a hundred camels; do you see among them a riding camel? Or: when will you see among them a riding camel? He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We know nothing better than a hundred like him except the believer. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of this hadith is like what we reported before it at the head of this chapter. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): do you see among them a riding camel, or: when will you see among them a riding camel, may be negation - that you see no riding camel - or existence at a distant time. Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby. And Him we ask for guidance."
"1680","231 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the occasion for which was revealed: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you. 1472 - Ali ibn Shaybah al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Muslim al-Qurashi informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed, saying: Allah has made Hajj obligatory upon you. A man said: Every year, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Had I said yes, it would have become obligatory, and you could not. Then he said: Leave me as I leave you; those before you perished only by their asking and differing with their prophets. When I forbid you something, abstain from it; when I command you something, do of it what you can."
"1681","1473 - Yahya ibn Uthman and Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu Iyad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When was revealed: To Allah upon people is pilgrimage of the House. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, every year? He fell silent. The man repeated thrice, each time he was silent. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I said every year, it would have become obligatory, and had you left it you would have disbelieved. Then Allah Almighty revealed: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you."
"1682","1474 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Ghamr narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Yahya Abu Muti narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, he said: Sulaym ibn Amir narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Umamah al-Bahili say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among people saying: Hajj is prescribed upon you. An Arab man stood saying: Every year? The Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) speech was cut off, he fell silent and grew angry. He remained long, then spoke: Who is this asker? The Arab said: I am. He said: Woe to you, what secures you that I say yes? By Allah, had I said yes, it would have become obligatory, and had it become obligatory you would have disbelieved. Those before you were destroyed only by imams of hardship. By Allah, had I permitted you everything on earth and forbidden you a camel-foot's spot thereof, you would have fallen into it. He said: So Allah Almighty revealed: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you, to the end of the verse. Abu Jafar said: In what we reported is that this verse came down for the occasion mentioned in these reports we narrated therein. It is also reported that its occasion was other than that."
"1683","1475 - As Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out angry, his face red, sat upon the pulpit and said: You do not ask me about anything but I will tell you. A man stood to him saying: Where is my father? He said: In the Fire. Another stood saying: O Messenger of Allah, who is my father? He said: Your father is Hudhafah. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, stood saying: We are pleased with Allah Almighty as Lord, Islam as religion, Quran as imam, and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Prophet. O Messenger of Allah, we were fresh from ignorance and polytheism, and Allah knows best who our fathers are. He said: So his anger subsided and was revealed: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you."
"1684","1476 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from Anas, that they asked the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day until they pressed him with asking. So he came out one day, ascended the pulpit and said: You do not ask me today - I think he said - about anything but I will inform you of it. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared some grave matter had come. I kept looking right and left finding every man wrapping his head in his garment weeping. He said: A man arose who was taunted and attributed to other than his father, saying: O Prophet of Allah, who is my father? He said: Your father is Hudhafah. Then Umar arose - or he said: Umar began - saying: We are pleased with Allah Almighty as Lord and Islam as religion, and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Messenger, seeking refuge with Allah from the evil of trials - or he said: I seek refuge with Allah Almighty from the evil of trials. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I never saw like today in good and evil ever: Paradise and Hell were pictured to me until I saw them below the wall."
"1685","1477 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah, from Qatadah, from Anas likewise. He said: Qatadah would mention this hadith when asked about this verse: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is that this verse came down for the occasions mentioned therein. An objector said: These reports contradict the first reports. How can this verse have come down for both these occasions together, when we do not find it in Allah's Book in two places? Had it come down for each occasion it would be mentioned in two places, as His saying: O Prophet, strive against disbelievers and hypocrites and be harsh with them, is mentioned in two places, since it came down twice, each place intending other than the other. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that these mentioned questions in these two sections of this chapter may all have been before this verse came down; then Allah revealed after that this verse, forbidding them these questions and informing them they need no answers with the realities intended thereby, since hearing them would grieve them, and they ask thereby what benefits them not, ignorance of which harms them not. Benefit in asking is learning obligations upon them in their religion and what draws them near to their Lord Almighty. That is the knowledge benefiting them, ignorance of which harms them; about it they must ask until they know. Proof that He only disliked their asking about what benefits them not and what grieves them when known - not other religious matters they need to know to perform obligations and draw near to their Lord - is what is reported from Muadh ibn Jabal showing that."
"1686","1478 - That Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Said al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Masruq narrated to us, from Ayyub. Abu Jafar said: he is Ibn Abdullah ibn Mikraz, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Muadh ibn Jabal, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I wish to ask you about a matter, but the place of this verse prevents me: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you. He said: What is it, O Muadh? I said: The deed that admits to Paradise and saves from Hell. He said: You have asked a great thing, yet it is easy: testimony that there is no god but Allah, and that I am Allah's Messenger, establishing prayer, giving zakat, Hajj of the House, and fasting Ramadan. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that when Muadh mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that this verse prevents him from asking him about something he needs to know, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it is not of the things whose knowledge is disliked and asking about them, he answered him. That shows the things forbidden to ask about in the verse we recited are things they cannot grasp in knowledge nor have reward therein, and things leading to reward and righteous deeds are not included in what this verse intends. It is reported from some predecessors about the occasion of this verse contrary to all these meanings."
"1687","And it is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah - he is Ibn Amr - from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Ikrimah, about this verse: O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would grieve you. He said: It is about the man who asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Who is my father? As for Said ibn Jubayr, he said: It is about those who asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about bahirah and saibah. As for Miqsam, he said: It is about what nations asked their prophets of signs. He said: The meaning of what is reported from Ikrimah agrees with some of what we narrated earlier in this chapter. As for what is reported from Said ibn Jubayr, its meaning with us - and Allah knows best - is of the genus of meanings we reported earlier in this chapter; for those who did the things they asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about of those meanings were sons of the hearers of the answers, and some present besides them were sons of some doers informed of their place therein."
"1688","1479 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I saw Amr ibn Amir al-Khuzai dragging his intestines in the Fire. He was the first to free saibah. Ibn al-Musayyib said: Saibah that was freed so nothing was carried upon it; bahirah whose milk was withheld for idols so none milked it; wasilah the young she-camel first bearing a female at the first birth of camels then a second female, so they named them for idols calling it wasilah joining one to the other; hami the stallion camel covering ten she-camels, when finishing his covering they left him for idols, exempting him from carrying, carrying nothing upon him, naming him hami."
"1689","232 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Do not say of grapes: karm, but say: vineyards. 1480 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not say: karm, for karm is only the Muslim man; but say: vineyards."
"1690","1481 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not name grapes karm; for karm is only the believer; but say: habalah."
"1691","1482 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Wail, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not say: karm for grapes; but say: habalah, or: habl. An objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when you have reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"1692","1483 - He mentioned what Yazid ibn Sinan and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no charity in any crops, date-palms or vines until they reach five awsuq, nor in silver until it reaches two hundred dirhams. He said: In this the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned vineyards by karm. How may you accept from him what he forbade to say? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is that this saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) naming vineyards karm may have been before he forbade what he forbade in the other reports; then he forbade what he forbade in the other reports, so the ruling returned to what is in the other reports. For things not yet forbidden were free in sayings and deeds; when forbidden they return to prohibition and prevention of doing and saying them. We find Allah's Book naming grapes by the name in the prohibition reports, His saying, Almighty: And lush gardens. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1693","233 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about entertainment at festivals. 1484 - Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Azdi al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Jabir, from Amir, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, he said: I attended a festival at al-Anbar, saying to them: Why do I not see you entertaining as they used to entertain in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) time?"
"1694","1485 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shayban and Israil narrated to us, from Jabir, from Amir, from Qays ibn Sad, he said: Nothing in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) time but I saw it done after him except one thing: entertainment on Fitr day, meaning play. Abu Jafar said: What we reported of this hadith only goes back to Jabir ibn Yazid al-Jufi absolutely, without mention of his hearing it from Amir al-Shabi. What of Jabir's hadith lacks mention of his hearing it from whom he narrates, and what proves that, is not strong with one inclined to him, let alone one averse. For I heard Fahd ibn Sulayman say: I heard Abu Nuaym say: Sufyan said: Whatever Jabir says to you therein: I heard, or narrated to me, or informed me - hold fast to it; what is other than that, therein is what therein is."
"1695","1486 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, from Sharik, from Mughirah, from Amir, from Iyad al-Ashari, he said: I attended a festival at al-Anbar, saying: Why do I not see you entertaining; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say it. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith al-Shabi traces it to Iyad al-Ashari, and this Iyad is a Tabii. So the hadith thereby becomes disconnected, and it is worthier than what we reported before it in this chapter; for Mughirah from al-Shabi is firmer than Jabir from al-Shabi, though al-Shabi narrated from Qays ibn Sad other than this hadith."
"1696","1487 - As al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Husayn, from Amir, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, he said: I came to al-Hirah. He said: I saw them prostrating to a marzuban of theirs. Speech dropped, which is: When I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came to al-Hirah and saw them prostrating to a marzuban of theirs. I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is worthier that we prostrate to him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I commanded anything to prostrate to anything, I would have commanded women to prostrate to their husbands. Abu Jafar said: Qays ibn Sad died late; al-Shabi's meeting him is not unlikely. Muhammad ibn Sad, Sahib al-Waqidi, mentioned in his Tabaqat: Qays ibn Sad died at Medina at the end of Muawiyah's caliphate. As for entertainment in the first hadith we mentioned in this chapter, there is no disagreement among linguists and others from whom the like is taken that it is play and amusement not disliked, like what is allowed at weddings, though what is done at festivals and weddings of them differ. That - and Allah knows best - is so People of the Two Books know there is latitude in Islam. If an objector says: How do you accept this when you reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts it? He mentioned:"
"1697","1488 - What Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Medina when they had two days they played in during ignorance. He said: Allah has replaced them for you with better than them: the day of Fitr and the day of Adha."
"1698","1489 - And as Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... and he mentioned the like of it. It was said to him: In this is nothing contradicting what we mentioned before it; for what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them in this hadith is only Allah's replacing for them the two days they played in during ignorance with the day of Fitr and the day of sacrifice. It may mean he intended thereby that they put therein of play what they did in those two days of play in ignorance. That with us - and Allah knows best - is upon permissible play like it, not prohibited play like it, as they were permitted at their weddings the play permitted therein."
"1699","1490 - As Abu Umayyah and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would address standing, then sit, then stand and address standing, two addresses. Slave-girls when married would pass with drums and flutes, so people would rush and leave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing. So Allah reproached them, saying: And when they see trade or amusement, they disperse to it and leave you standing, the verse. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that Allah did not forbid them the amusement like what He permitted in what that amusement of theirs was in? Likewise the play He permitted at festivals is not included in the like of amusement He forbade them in other than festivals. So by Allah's praise and favor it is clear there is no contradiction in any reports we mentioned in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1700","234 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The greatest of Muslims in crime against Muslims is one who asks about a matter not unlawful, so it becomes unlawful due to his asking. 1491 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sad, that he heard Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The greatest of Muslims in crime against Muslims is one who asks about something not unlawful, so it becomes unlawful due to his asking."
"1701","1492 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it. We examined this hadith to know what is intended thereby, Allah willing. We found whoever asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about something sought thereby Allah's answer therein; for what he answered them with is what Allah Almighty revealed to him. Allah Almighty revealed to him: And do not hasten with the Quran before its revelation is completed to you. He commanded him to wait for what comes down of His rulings until He sends it. What He forbade him thereof, his nation is forbidden thereof. What comes to him from Allah as answer to what is asked - though not Quran - is in the meaning of Quran also. Among what was revealed to him: We neglected nothing in the Book. The Quran would come down after that as before. We understood thereby that His saying: We neglected nothing in the Book, means: We shall neglect nothing in the Book. And Allah knows best. Among what supports what we mentioned is what was from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when wine's prohibition came down, his saying: O Allah, clarify to us about wine a healing clarification. Then came down: They ask you about wine and gambling; say: In them is great sin and benefits for people, the verse. He said: Umar: O Allah, clarify to us about wine a healing clarification. Then came down: O you who believe, do not approach prayer while intoxicated until you know what you say. Umar was called and it was recited to him. He said: O Allah, clarify to us about wine a healing clarification. Then came down: O you who believe, wine, gambling, idols and divining arrows are filth of Satan's work... to His saying: Will you then desist? Umar was called and it was recited to him. He said: We desist, we desist."
"1702","1493 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated it to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, he is Abu Maysarah, from Umar... then he mentioned this hadith. His saying Almighty: Will you then desist, intends thereby asking about the like of this, until Allah sends it down upon His Messenger initially; for the Book containing it is not neglected until all things are gathered therein. Since asking about what we mentioned was forbidden to people, whoever asked thereof wronged himself; for his asking preceded Allah's command - which he ought not precede. Allah Almighty mentioned among His punishing Jews for their wrongdoing His saying: For wrongdoing of those who Judaized, We forbade them good things permitted to them, the verse. Whoever's asking returns wronging is not secure from being forbidden thereby what was lawful to him; for all things are upon freedom and permissibility until Allah newly makes prohibition therein, returning unlawful. When what the asker we mentioned asked about returns unlawful due to his asking upon him, it returns unlawful upon all people. In that is his great crime among them. We found no interpretation of this hadith worthier than this meaning we mentioned therein. Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby. Abu Jafar said: If an objector says: Do Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned askings in Abu Maysarah's hadith from him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Allah sent answers of the mentioned verses enter the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in Sad's hadith, may Allah be pleased with him: The greatest of Muslims in crime is one who asks about something not unlawful, so it becomes unlawful due to his asking? It was said to him: That does not enter anything of this hadith of Sad; for Sad's hadith is about one who asks about what was lawful, so it becomes unlawful due to his asking. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in Abu Maysarah's hadith we mentioned only asked about something Allah had already forbidden before that. Do you not see he says therein: When wine's prohibition came down, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Allah, clarify to us about wine a healing clarification. That from him, may Allah be pleased with him, may mean he intended what Allah explained in answer to him in informing the people whose hearts held wine's prohibition great due to its eminent place with them before that: that Allah only forbade it to them for their welfare; for it is filth; in it great sin; it prevents prayer. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caller when prayer came would call: Let no intoxicated one attend prayer."
"1703","1494 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated it to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur al-Saluli narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caller call: When prayer is established, let no intoxicated one approach prayer. He, may Allah be pleased with him, informed that by drinking it they reached a state preventing them approaching prayer; and that it cast enmity and hatred among them, since it caused what befell Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, when he and a group of Ansar drank it and boasted thereat until some said: Muhajirun are better, and some said: Ansar are better. So he took a camel's jaw and struck therewith Sad's nose; his nose was gashed."
"1704","1495 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us thereby, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Musab ibn Sad, from Sad... Abu Jafar said: In that is the great benefit of Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, asking Allah Almighty for Muslims, until they knew by his asking that Allah's forbidding wine was better for them than its permissibility remaining; for its permissibility cast among them enmity, hatred and injuries of some upon others, while its prohibition has none of that. Let them know that is a favor from Allah Almighty upon them whose cause was Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, asking Him Almighty - not a punishment upon them thereby. And Allah is the granter of success."
"1705","235 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the occasion for which came down: Other than those hurt, after there came down before it: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way with their wealth and selves, the verse. 1496 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, that Miqsam, freedman of Abdullah ibn al-Harith, narrates from Ibn Abbas, that he heard him say: Not equal are those sitting among believers from Badr, and those going out to Badr. He said: When the Badr campaign came, Abd ibn Jahsh al-Asadi Abu Ahmad and Ibn Umm Maktum said: We are blind, O Messenger of Allah, is there concession for us? So came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt..."
"1706","1497 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Salih ibn Kaysan narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, that he said: I saw Marwan ibn al-Hakam sitting in the mosque. I came until I sat beside him. He informed us that Zayd ibn Thabit informed him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dictated to him: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way. He said: Ibn Umm Maktum came to him while he was dictating it to me, saying: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, had I power for jihad I would strive - he was blind. So Allah sent down upon His Messenger, his thigh upon my thigh, growing heavy until I feared it would crush my thigh. Then it was lifted from him, and Allah sent down: Other than those hurt."
"1707","1498 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us... then he mentioned with a like chain."
"1708","1499 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that tranquility overcame the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Zayd said: with me beside him - so the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thigh fell upon my thigh. I never felt anything heavier than the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thigh. Then it was lifted from him, and he said to me: Write: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way with their wealth and selves, the whole verse. Zayd said: So I wrote that on a shoulder-blade. Ibn Umm Maktum, blind, stood when he heard the striving ones preferred over the sitting, saying: O Messenger of Allah, what of believers unable to strive? Kharijah said: Zayd said: No sooner had Ibn Umm Maktum finished his speech than tranquility overcame the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his thigh fell upon my thigh, feeling the second time like the first in weight. Then it was lifted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Read. So I read: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Other than those hurt. So I appended it, as if I see its appendage at a crack of the blade."
"1709","1500 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami and Rawh ibn Ubadah al-Qaysi narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: When this verse came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers, Ibn Umm Maktum came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), complaining his blindness. So came down: Other than those hurt."
"1710","1501 - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: When came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way, Ibn Umm Maktum said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you command me, for I cannot strive? So Allah sent down in its place: Other than those hurt."
"1711","1502 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: When came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called a man. He came with tablet and ink - or shoulder-blade - saying: Write: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way. Behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was blind Ibn Umm Maktum. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I am blind of sight. He said: There came down in its place: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt, and those striving in Allah's way."
"1712","And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib about Allah Almighty's saying: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt. Ibn Umm Maktum said:"
"1713","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, they said: Abu Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abu Nadrah narrated to us, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about Allah Almighty's saying: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt, the verse. Ibn Abbas said: People detained by illnesses and pains; those were those hurt. The sitting sick is more excused than the sitting healthy."
"1714","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Abu Aqil, from Abu Nadrah, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about Allah Almighty's saying: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt. He said: There were people overtaken by pains and illnesses. Abu Jafar said: If an objector says: Is what in this hadith of Abu Nadrah from Ibn Abbas contrary to what in Miqsam's hadith from Ibn Abbas we reported in this chapter? For therein came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way, then after it: Other than those hurt. In Abu Nadrah's hadith that is mentioned in sequence, ظاهر requiring they came down together. It was said to him: No contradiction between them; for Miqsam's hadith is Ibn Abbas informing of the occasion of its coming down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) how it was. Abu Nadrah's hadith is Ibn Abbas informing of its interpretation upon which its matter settled - that from him after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Each is in a meaning other than its companion's, though what the verse settled upon in both is agreeing, not differing."
"1715","1503 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman both narrated to us, they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from al-Falatan ibn Asim al-Jarmi, that he said: We were sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Revelation came down upon him. When it came down, his gaze would remain fixed, eyes open, his hearing and sight devoted to what came to him from Allah Almighty. When finished, he said to the scribe: Write: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way; Allah preferred the striving with their wealth and selves over the sitting by a degree. The blind man stood saying: O Messenger of Allah, what is our sin? So Allah sent down upon him. We said to the blind man: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is receiving revelation. He said: He remained standing saying: I turn to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: To the scribe: Write: Other than those hurt. An objector said: How do you accept these reports, establishing thereby that this verse first came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way - therein preferring strivers in Allah's way over sitting ones, excused and unexcused? The sitting excused did not sit choosing to leave jihad; they sat unable for jihad. How may the strivers' merit over the sitting excused equal them with excuse like unexcused sitting ones without excuse? How may those hurt among the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Companions - in jurisprudence as they are, Quran also in their language - think of Allah Almighty that He equaled therein them with excuse and unexcused sitting ones from jihad without excuse, when they heard Allah Almighty say: Allah burdens no soul except what He gave it - and Allah gave them no strength for jihad - and heard Him say: Allah burdens no soul except its capacity? Greater, that these reports be as mentioned therein. He said: Impossible that this verse came down except as he recites it: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt, and those striving in Allah's way with their wealth and selves, the verse. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that these reports we reported are sound, established; scholars do not reject their soundness, impugn their chains, nor differ that the mentioned verse first came down: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way with their wealth and selves. When Ibn Umm Maktum and Abu Ahmad ibn Jahsh mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) their inability for jihad due to their hurt, Allah sent down: Other than those hurt. So the verse became: Not equal are those sitting among believers, other than those hurt, and those striving in Allah's way. That with us - and Allah knows best - is not that Allah intended them and their likes by this verse with their inability for the meaning therein preferring strivers over sitting ones other than those hurt. Rather that escaped them until came from them what is mentioned in these reports to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Allah Almighty sent down thereupon to His Messenger: Other than those hurt, informing them He did not intend them nor their likes of those hurt by that preferring of strivers over sitting ones. How may the matter be otherwise, when they heard Allah Almighty say: There is no blame upon the blind, nor upon the lame, nor upon the sick - meaning in lagging from jihad with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? If an objector says: May the like of this of Allah Almighty's intent by this verse escape them? It was said to him: What do you deny of this, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon revelation about fasting: Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread, recited it to them and they took it as Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi mentioned of their taking until Allah Almighty sent down to His Messenger what informed them His intent is other than what they thought of Him Almighty?"
"1716","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, he said: When came down: Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread, a man would take a white thread and black thread, placing them under a pillow, looking when he distinguishes them, then leaving food. He said: So Allah explained that, and came down: of dawn. In this hadith is Allah's explanation that what He intended by the white thread and black thread is other than what they thought He intended thereby. Likewise Adi ibn Hatim al-Tai in what is reported from him in this meaning."
"1717","1504 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Adi ibn Hatim."
"1718","1505 - And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn and Mujalid informed us, from al-Shabi, he said: Adi ibn Hatim informed us, he said: When this verse came down: Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread, I took to two hobbles, one black. I kept looking at them, the white not distinct to me from the black. In the morning I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informing him of what I did. He said: Your pillow is wide indeed; that is only the whiteness of day and blackness of night. Do you not see that when they heard His saying Almighty: Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread, they took it as they took it until Allah Almighty explained to them in His Book and upon His Messenger's tongue that what He intended is contrary to what they thought? Likewise the story of Ibn Umm Maktum and Abu Ahmad: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to them what he recited, they thought they are of the less meritorious in what he recited to them. So Allah Almighty explained to them by sending down to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Other than those hurt, that He did not intend them nor their likes of those hurt; He only intended others without hurt. In what we mentioned is proof that the recitation therein is as those who recite it with rafa recite - they are: Asim, al-Amash, Abu Amr, Hamzah - not as their opponents recite it: Other than those hurt, with nasb - they are: Abu Jafar, Shaybah, Nafi, Ibn Kathir, Abdullah ibn Amir. Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam inclined to the recitation of these Medinans, saying along with that: rafa is a possible, unobjectionable face in Arabic. Likewise al-Farra held its correctness in Arabic, saying: It is an adjective of the sitting ones. He said: Whatever is their adjective, its parsing is likewise with rafa, nothing else - as He Almighty said: Or followers without desire. So His adjectiving them is like what He mentioned them with of jarr, nothing else. And Allah we ask for guidance. Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam said about the reason he chose: Other than those hurt, with nasb: He said: It is reported from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by more than one he mentioned, that its coming down was upon exception, so it must be mansub. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that it is not reported from any Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: it came down as exception from what came before it. What is reported from them about its occasion is what we reported at the head of this chapter. Had all of it come down together, exception would be possible and nasb worthier than rafa. But what came down first thereof was His saying Almighty: Not equal are those sitting among believers and those striving in Allah's way. And we know with certainty that Allah Almighty did not mean the chronically ill with intention - that had they power for jihad they would strive. When so, the strivers are not better than them; for they strove with their strength, while the others lagged from jihad unable. Allah Almighty said: No blame upon the weak, nor the sick, nor those finding nothing to spend, when sincere to Allah and His Messenger; no way against the doers of good, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful; nor upon those who when they came to you for mount you said: I find nothing to mount you upon. Then He informed after that that the way is against others than these by His saying Almighty: The way is only against those asking your permission while rich, pleased to be with the laggards. And He Almighty said: No blame upon the blind, nor the lame, nor the sick. Whoever takes the matter otherwise than we mentioned says a grave saying, attributing to Allah Almighty imposing on His creatures what they are unable to do. Since the coming down of what we recited is as we mentioned, what Allah Almighty sent down after thereof of His saying: Other than those hurt, is clarification of what He sent before thereof of the sitting ones over whom He preferred the strivers. So rafa is worthier for it than otherwise. An asker asked: Ibn Umm Maktum excused himself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he excused, yet on the day of al-Qadisiyyah in the same state for which he excused himself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he carried the banner fighting disbelievers - why did he not offer that of himself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? And he mentioned what [source breaks off here, continued in the next row]."
"1719","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said - he is Ibn Abi Arubah - narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum on the day of al-Qadisiyyah had with him a black banner, wearing armor."
"1720","And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I saw Ibn Umm Maktum in one of the Muslims' battles, in his hand the banner. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that Ibn Umm Maktum on the day came from him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what came when he did not then know carrying the banner well; then he knew it after that, undertaking it for Muslims when he knew it, leaving undertaking it before when he did not know it. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"1721","236 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him on the day of Mecca's conquest: granting safety to all people except the four men he named, and the two singing-girls he named with them. 1506 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal al-Hafari narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Suddi claimed, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father, he said: On the day of Mecca's conquest, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted safety to people except four men and two women, saying: Kill them even if you find them clinging to the Kaabah's curtains: Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, Abdullah ibn Khatal, Miqyas ibn Subabah, and Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh. As for Abdullah ibn Khatal, he was caught clinging to the Kaabah's curtains. Said ibn Hurayth and Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with them, raced to him; Said outran Ammar, being the stronger, and killed him. As for Miqyas ibn Subabah, people caught him in the market and killed him. As for Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, he boarded the sea; a violent wind struck them. The ship's crew said to its people: Be sincere, for your gods avail you nothing here. Ikrimah said: By Allah, if only sincerity saves me at sea, only it saves me on land. O Allah, I pledge if You save me from what I am in, I shall come to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placing my hand in his, finding him pardoning, generous. So he was saved and embraced Islam. As for Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh, he hid with Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called people to pledge, he brought him until stopping him before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, pledge Abdullah. He raised his head, looking at him thrice, each time refusing. He pledged him after three. Then he turned to his Companions saying: Was there not among you a man to rise to this when he saw me withhold my hand from his pledge and kill him? They said: We knew not, O Messenger of Allah, what was in yourself. Why did you not wink to us with your eye? He said: It is not for a prophet to have treacherous eyes. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded about these four named men what he commanded, absolutely. Then Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl and Abdullah ibn Sad left that by their Islam, which protected their blood. the other two were killed upon the disbelief they persisted in. That shows the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command about them was excepted by their leaving the cause for which he commanded to its opposite - Islam. That was exception by Law though not by tongue. That shows likewise rulers' punishments are excepted by what lifts punishments by Law, though they do not except by tongues. And Allah Almighty is the granter of success."
"1722","237 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: No Qurashi shall be killed after today in captivity. 1507 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from al-Shabi, he said: Abdullah ibn Muti said: I heard Muti say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Mecca's conquest say: No Qurashi shall be killed in captivity after this day to the Day of Judgment."
"1723","1508 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub - meaning Ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad - narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Shubah narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Shabi, from Abdullah ibn Muti ibn al-Aswad, from his father - his name was al-Asi, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Muti - he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he commanded killing these people at Mecca say: Mecca shall not be raided after this year ever, nor shall a man of Quraysh be killed in captivity after the year. Abu Jafar said: This saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - what the narrator did not mention to us its wording parsed - therein lies the ambiguity. For if it were: let none be killed, that is a command, contradicting Allah Almighty's rulings mentioned in other than this hadith; for Allah's rulings are that a Qurashi is killed in retaliation for intentional murder, and stoned if fornicating while married. Far be it that the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wording with that letter departs from these rulings. Rather with us - and Allah knows best - it is: none shall be killed, nominative, so it is a report - like what we mentioned earlier in this book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: No believer is stung from a hole twice, where we brought what requires it be a report, not a command, sparing repetition here. An objector said: We saw countless Qurashis killed in Islam in captivity, knowing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says no falsehood. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help, is that his intent (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his saying: No Qurashi shall be killed after the year in captivity, is only that after that year no Qurashi shall be killed in captivity upon what he permitted of killing the four Qurashi men mentioned in Sad's hadith that year; for it was killing upon belligerency - those killed thereof upon disbelief. That - by Allah's praise - never was after that year in a Qurashi returning disbelieving, belligerent against Allah and His Messenger in a house of disbelief to this day of ours, nor shall be to the Day of Judgment; for Allah Almighty does not break His promise to His messengers. And among what proves what we said thereof is what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith about Mecca."
"1724","1509 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, from al-Shabi, from al-Harith ibn al-Barsa, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say on the day of Mecca's conquest: Mecca shall not be raided after this day ever. Sufyan said: Its explanation is that they shall never disbelieve, nor be raided upon disbelief. Abu Jafar said: Likewise the meaning of: no Qurashi shall be killed after the year in captivity, intends this meaning: they shall not return disbelievers to be raided until killed upon disbelief, just as Mecca shall not return a house of disbelief to be raided thereupon. And Allah Almighty is the granter of success."
"1725","238 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to one he called while praying, who did not answer until finishing his prayer, then came answering: What prevented you answering me? He said: I was praying. He said: Did you not find in what Allah Almighty sent down to me: O you who believe, respond to Allah and the Messenger when he calls you to what gives you life? We mentioned of what enters this chapter in the chapter explaining the problematic reports about Allah Almighty's saying: And We gave you seven of the oft-repeated, and the hadith of Abu Said ibn al-Mualla entering this chapter. 1510 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn al-Mutarrif narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, freedman of al-Huraqah, narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to Ubayy ibn Kab while he was praying, saying: O Ubayy. Ubayy turned but did not answer him. Then he prayed, shortening, then turned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Peace upon you, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And upon you peace. What prevented you answering me when I called you? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I was in prayer. He said: Did you not find in what Allah revealed to me: Respond to Allah and the Messenger when he calls you to what gives you life? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, and I shall not return, Allah willing."
"1726","1511 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Uthman al-Uthmani narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the like of it. Abu Jafar said: In what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his obliging one he calls while praying to answer him, leaving his prayer - that being worthier for him than persisting in his prayer - with what he is blamed for of what Allah Almighty sent down upon him. For the praying one can leave his prayer for the merit attained in answering the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he calls him. An objector said: Does answering a man his mother when she calls him while praying enter into that? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is that it is not unlikely to be so; for he can leave his prayer, answer his mother in what he must answer her therein, and return to his prayer. His prayer if missed he makes up; his dutifulness to his mother if missed he cannot make up. What is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Jurayj the monk proves that."
"1727","1512 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz. He said: Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman called her son while he was in a hermitage, saying: O Jurayj. He said: O Allah, my mother or my prayer? She said: O Jurayj. He said: O Allah, my mother or my prayer? Until that was thrice from her. She said: O Allah, let not Jurayj die until he looks into the faces of prostitutes. A shepherdess sheltering at his hermitage pasturing sheep gave birth. It was said to her: From whom is this child? She said: From Jurayj. So he came down from his hermitage. Jurayj said: Where is this one claiming her child is mine? He said: O infant, who is your father? He said: My father is the shepherd. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that Jurayj was punished for leaving answering his mother when she called him while praying, persisting in his prayer, by being punished as he was for that. That shows answering his mother and returning to his prayer after that was better for him than persisting in his prayer and leaving answering his mother. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"1728","239 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the disbeliever who was among his Companions' foes: a man of them vowed if empowered over him to kill him, but his Islam prevented that, so he did not kill him therefore. 1513 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar al-Juddi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghalib narrated to us, from Anas, he said: We raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). A disbelieving man was hardest upon the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Companions. A man among the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Companions said: If Allah empowers him over him, he shall strike his neck. He said: Allah gave Muslims victory over them; they would bring them captives, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would accept their pledge until that man was brought. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withheld from his pledge so the man might fulfill his vow. The man disliked to rise and strike his neck before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him doing nothing, he pledged him. The man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: What do I do, O Messenger of Allah, with my vow? He said: I withheld from him for you to fulfill your vow, but you did nothing. He said: O Messenger of Allah, why did you not wink to me? He said: It is not for a prophet to wink. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is proof that what was from the mentioned man - if Allah empowers him over him he shall strike his neck - was upon a vow, and that he missed it by his Islam, not fulfilling his vow. That shows vows of things of this genus are cut from fulfillment by the like cutting that vower from fulfilling his vow of that disbeliever by his Islam. An objector said: Is there upon him with that expiation when he does not fulfill his vow? Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance, is that upon him is expiation for missing fulfilling his vow, by the Law preventing him from fulfilling that. In that is proof that prevention by Law is like prevention by lack. There is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what supports what we mentioned."
"1729","1514 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever vows to obey Allah Almighty, let him obey Him; whoever vows to disobey Allah Almighty, let him not disobey Him."
"1730","Hafs said: And I heard Ibn Mujabbar while with Ubaydullah, and he mentioned it from al-Qasim, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. And he said therein: He expiates his oath. Abu Jafar said: This hadith in reality Ubaydullah ibn Umar did not hear from al-Qasim; he only took it from Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik al-Ayli, from al-Qasim, from Aishah."
"1731","1515 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever vows to obey Allah Almighty, let him obey Him; whoever vows to disobey Allah Almighty, let him not disobey Him. So we understood thereby that between Ubaydullah and al-Qasim in this hadith is Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik. What we brought this hadith for is what therein is of Ibn Mujabbar's narration from al-Qasim from Aishah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning expiation. This Ibn Mujabbar is a man of Umar's family, may Allah be pleased with him, eminent. Malik ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from him. He has a son whose hadith is questioned; later ones reported from him. Since one vowing to disobey Allah Almighty is commanded with expiation for what the Law prevents him from, one vowing what the Law permits him, then the Law prevents him from it after that, is worthier of expiation for his vow unable to fulfill. And Allah is the granter of success."
"1732","240 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his commanding one who broke fast a day of Ramadan deliberately to make up a day, with the expiation he commanded therein. Abu Jafar said: All reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Abu Hurayrah's hadith about him in this chapter lacks mention of making up a day in place of the day of that breaking, except what we shall report therein, Allah willing. 1516 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, thus he said: From Abu Hurayrah, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I fell upon my wife in Ramadan? He said: Free a slave. He said: I find none, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Fast two consecutive months. He said: I cannot. He said: Feed sixty poor. He said: I find it not, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a basket with about fifteen sa of dates. He said: Take this and give it in charity. He said: Upon needier than me and my household? He said: Eat it, you and your household, fast a day in its place, and seek Allah Almighty's forgiveness."
"1733","1517 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Marwan al-Uthmani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he informed him, that Abu Hurayrah... then he mentioned this hadith, except he did not say therein: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Make up a day in its place."
"1734","1518 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Abu Marwan narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Ibn Shihab with this chain the like of it. And he said to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Fast a day in its place."
"1735","1519 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this hadith, and that he said to him: And make up a day in its place."
"1736","1520 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said and Ata al-Khurasani informed me, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... likewise. An objector said: How do you accept this from its narrator from Abu Hurayrah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about making up a day in its place, when you report from Abu Hurayrah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"1737","1521 - He mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Umarah ibn Umayr narrating from Abu al-Mutawwis - Habib said: I saw Abu al-Mutawwis - from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever breaks fast a day of Ramadan without a concession Allah permitted him, fasting the whole year would not make up for it."
"1738","1522 - And as Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Habib, from Ibn al-Mutawwis, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Except he did not mention Habib's saying: I saw Abu al-Mutawwis."
"1739","1523 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said and Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned a word meaning: from Habib, he said: Abu al-Mutawwis narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever breaks fast a day of Ramadan without concession or illness, fasting the whole year would not make up for it even if he fasts it. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, is that this hadith does not contradict the first hadith; for the first hadith mentions making up, while this hadith is that year-long fasting does not attain for that day its fasting had it fasted other than that day - as one leaving a prayer without excuse until its time lapses must make it up, without attaining by making up what he would attain had he prayed it in its time. Likewise the breaker in Ramadan is commanded to make up, without attaining by that making up what he would attain had he fasted it in itself. So by Allah's praise and favor it is clear there is no contradiction in these two hadiths; each is in a meaning other than its companion's. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1740","241 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is intended by Allah Almighty's saying: And those in authority among you. 1524 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah, Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn al-Qasim al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Simak Abu Zumayl, he said: Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me in a hadith: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) swore to his wives not to enter upon them for a month. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, if you divorced them, Allah Almighty, His angels, Jibril, Mikail are with you, and I, Abu Bakr and the believers are with you. Seldom did I speak - and I praise Allah - a speech but I hoped Allah Almighty would confirm my saying. He said: The verse of choice came down: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will replace for him wives better than you; and: If you both support against him, Allah is his protector and Jibril, the verse. And about this came down the verse: When there comes to them a matter of security or fear, they spread it; had they referred it to the Messenger and to those in authority among them, those who derive it among them would know it. He said: I was the one who derived that matter, and Allah Almighty sent down the verse of choice. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is Umar's informing that he derived what he mentioned deriving therein, and that intended by the deriving ones mentioned in the verse therein are people of good and knowledge from whom religious matters are taken. The like of that is reported from Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari."
"1741","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil, from Jabir, regarding Allah's saying: {And those in authority among you}: He said: People of goodness. And the like of that was also narrated from those after them among the Tabi'in."
"1742","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur — meaning Ibn ZadHan — narrated to us, from al-Hasan and 'Abd al-Malik, from 'Ata', regarding Allah Almighty's saying: {And those in authority among you}: They both said: People of understanding and knowledge."
"1743","'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn KunAsah al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, regarding Allah Almighty's saying: {If you dispute over anything, refer it to Allah and the Messenger}: He said: Referral to Allah Almighty is to His Book, and referral to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he has passed away, is to his Sunnah."
"1744","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik, from 'Ata': {And those in authority among you}: He said, people of understanding and knowledge; and obedience to Allah and the Messenger is following the Book and the Sunnah. Abu Ja'far said: Then someone said: But something has been narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas contradicting this, and he mentioned it."
"1745","1525 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj said: Ya'la ibn Muslim informed me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas: {O you who believe, obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you}: He said: It was revealed concerning 'Abdullah ibn Hudhafah ibn Qays ibn 'Adi, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him on a military expedition. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that this does not contradict what was narrated from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in what we mentioned earlier, since 'Abdullah ibn Hudhafah was among the people of goodness and companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among the people of understanding; had he not been so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have put him in charge of what he put him in charge of, since what he put him in charge of involved rulings for Allah that none grasp except people of understanding who know their likes. And what was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas in another hadith points to this interpretation we mentioned."
"1746","As Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah al-Basri and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Kufi narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn 'Abbas: {Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you}: He said: Those in authority are the people of obedience to Allah Almighty who teach people the meanings of their religion, command them to good and forbid them from evil, so Allah made obedience to them obligatory upon the servants. Do you not see that Ibn 'Abbas described those in authority with obedience to Allah Almighty, teaching people the meanings of their religion, commanding good and forbidding evil, so that points to what we mentioned. And something was also narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in the interpretation of that."
"1747","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, regarding Allah Almighty's saying: {Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you}: He said: Commanders of military detachments."
"1748","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah: {And those in authority among you}: He said: They are the rulers. Abu Ja'far said: That shows that those in authority whose obedience is commanded are whoever has this description, whether they are rulers or others. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1749","242 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: Modesty is part of faith. 1526 - 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he heard a man admonishing his brother about modesty, so he said: Modesty is part of faith."
"1750","1527 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man of the Ansar while he was admonishing his brother about modesty, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him, for modesty is part of faith."
"1751","1528 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1752","1529 - And Yazid narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Then someone said: How can modesty be part of faith, when modesty is an inborn disposition in its people, while faith is acquired by its people through their words and deeds, and modesty is opposite to that — so how can it be part of it? Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that we found modesty holds its possessor back from committing sins in word and deed, just as faith holds its people back from the like of that; and since modesty and faith in what we mentioned perform one deed, they are as one thing, and each of them is from its companion. The Arabs would put a thing in place of a thing that is like it or similar to it. Do you not see that they called supplication prayer, including Allah Almighty's saying: {And supplicate for them; indeed your supplication is comfort for them}, meaning His command to him to supplicate for them, and including Allah Almighty's saying: {Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet; O you who believe, send blessings upon him and greet him}, so Allah called supplication prayer, since it is performed in prayer."
"1753","1530 - And from that is the narrated hadith: {When one of you is invited while fasting, let him respond; if he is not fasting let him eat, and if he is fasting let him pray} — i.e. supplicate. 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated it to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in what we mentioned is clarity that a thing may be named by the name of a thing, when each of them does what the other does. Likewise modesty is mentioned as being part of faith, since there comes from it what comes from faith. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1754","243 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Shabbiness is part of faith} — meaning austerity. 1531 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah, that he came to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b, and 'Abd al-Rahman said to him: I heard your father narrating that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Shabbiness is part of faith} — meaning austerity. Abu Ja'far said: And this 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah is Ibn Abi Umamah al-Ansari of Banu Harithah, who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Whoever cuts off a Muslim's property by his oath, Allah forbids Paradise to him and makes Hell obligatory for him}. Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Shabbiness is part of faith} is that it is among the marks of the people of faith, since with them are asceticism, humility and abandoning arrogance, as the prophets, Allah's blessings upon them, were before them in the like of that."
"1755","1532 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, from his father, he said: The prophets, Allah's blessings upon them, used to wear wool, ride donkeys, and milk sheep; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a donkey called 'Ufayr. So the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Shabbiness is part of faith} is that it is among the character of the people of faith, so he made it thereby part of faith. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1756","244 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: Among what we have retained of the speech of the first prophethood: If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish. 1533 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri and Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, he said: I heard Abu Mas'ud say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Among what we have retained of the speech of the first prophethood: If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish}."
"1757","1534 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1758","Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, but he did not attribute it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he stopped it at Abu Mas'ud."
"1759","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it and stopped it at Abu Mas'ud, and did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it."
"1760","1535 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Dabbi informed me, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from Abu Mas'ud, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... and he mentioned the like of it."
"1761","1536 - And Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad — and he is Ibn al-'Awwam — narrated to us, from Abu Malik al-Ashja'i, from Rib'i, from Hudhayfah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The last of the speech of the first prophethood to be held to: If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish}."
"1762","1537 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn Ghannam narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mansur, from Shaqiq — so he said — from Abu Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The greatest of what people have retained of the speech of the first prophethood: If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish}."
"1763","1538 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abu Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Among what people have retained of the speech of the first prophethood: If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish}. Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of that, and Allah knows best, is encouragement to modesty and commanding it, and informing people that if they are not among its people they will do whatever they wish — not that they are commanded in any situation to do whatever they wish. This is like the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in Hell} — it is not that when he lies he is commanded to take himself a seat in Hell, but that when he lies about him he will take his seat in Hell. And the like of this is much in their speech. Likewise this hadith: If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish, meaning: if you feel no shame you will do whatever you wish. And it may be by way of threat, and threat has the wording of command while in reality it is otherwise. From that is Allah Almighty's saying: {Do whatever you wish}, and His saying Almighty: {And incite whomever you can of them with your voice and rally against them your cavalry and infantry and share with them in wealth and children and promise them}, then Allah Almighty followed that with what clarified to them the meaning that brings its people to what it brings them to and enters them into what it enters them into, by His saying Almighty: {And Satan promises them nothing but delusion}. So its wording was the wording of command while its inward meaning was prohibition and threat. Likewise what we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {If you feel no shame, do whatever you wish} — its wording is the wording of command, and its inward meaning is prohibition and threat. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1764","245 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: Whoever establishes a good practice and those after him act upon it, he has its reward and the reward of whoever acts upon it after him, nothing diminished from their rewards; and whoever establishes an evil practice and those after him act upon it... then he mentioned of its burden and the burden of whoever acts upon it after him the like of what he mentioned for the good one. 1539 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Abu Wa'il, from Jarir, that some Bedouin people came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing patched woolen garments, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged people to give charity, and it was as if they were slow until that was seen on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then a man of the Ansar came with a piece of gold ore and threw it down, and people followed one after another until that was recognized on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever establishes a practice — as if meaning a good one — and those after him act upon it, he has the like of the reward of whoever acts upon it, without anything diminished from their rewards; and whoever establishes an evil practice and those after him act upon it, upon him is the like of the burden of whoever acts upon it, without anything diminished from their burdens}."
"1765","1540 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Muslim ibn Subayh and 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hilal al-'Absi, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah, he said: {Some Bedouin people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he saw need and hardship upon them, so he praised Allah and extolled Him, then commanded them to give charity and urged them to it and encouraged them in it; they were slow until that was seen on his face. Then a man of the Ansar came with a handful of silver and gave it to him, then another came, then people followed one another in charity until joy was seen on his face. So he said: Whoever establishes in Islam a good practice}, then he mentioned the rest of what is in the hadith before it."
"1766","1541 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-'Allaf narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sawa' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-Asadi, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah al-Bajali, that he narrated to them in the quarter of the Kufa mosque, that a man of the Ansar stood to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a purse of gold filling what is between the fingers, and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is in Allah's path. Then Abu Bakr stood and gave, then 'Umar stood and gave, then the Muhajirun and Ansar stood and gave, so the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brightened until we saw joy on his face. So he said at that: Whoever establishes a practice... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith before it. Abu Ja'far said: And we already narrated of what enters this chapter, from what we put earlier in this book of ours, hadiths in this chapter in which we chose the reading of whoever reads at the beginning of Surat al-Nisa': {and the wombs} with accusative, beside the reading of whoever reads: {and the wombs} with genitive, so we are spared repeating them here. Then someone said: How can he have its reward like the reward of whoever acts upon it after him, when with the doer is the burden of performing it that is not with the one who established it — so it would be reasonable for him to be above, in reward for acting upon it, the reward of the one who established it. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, after he argued against us with something narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter from other than the path of Jarir ibn 'Abdullah, which pointed him, as mentioned, to what he said."
"1767","1542 - And it is what Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, from his father, he said: {A beggar stood and begged in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people held back; then a man of the people gave, and the people gave. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever establishes good and it is followed, he has its reward and from the rewards of whoever follows him, nothing diminished from their rewards; and whoever establishes evil and it is followed, upon him is its burden and from the burdens of whoever follows him, nothing diminished from their burdens}. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help: It may be that what is meant by his saying: {and the like of the reward of whoever acts upon it}, and his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): and from the reward of whoever acts upon it, is one meaning, and the particle 'from' is connective — and this is permissible in the language. From that is Allah Almighty's saying: {Is there any creator other than Allah}, meaning: is there a creator other than Allah; and from that His saying Almighty: {And there is no god but Allah}, meaning: and there is no god but Allah. So the meaning of the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from the rewards of whoever acts upon it, in Hudhayfah's hadith, returns to the meaning: and the rewards of whoever acts upon it, in Jarir's hadith, so they agree and do not contradict. Then this questioner said: But something was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud indicating contrary to what you mentioned."
"1768","1543 - And he mentioned what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No soul is killed unjustly except that upon the first son of Adam is a share of it}."
"1769","1544 - And what Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdan ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah — and he is al-Sukkari — narrated to us, from al-A'mash... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and added: Because he established killing. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that the share is the like, as Allah Almighty said: {And whoever intercedes an evil intercession will have a share of it}, meaning a like of it of its kind; and like His saying: {He will give you two shares of His mercy}, i.e. two likes, as:"
"1770","Wallad narrated to us, he said: al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu 'Ubaydah: {Two shares of His mercy}: He said: Two likes. So what this opponent argued against us with became proof for us against him, as we mentioned. And among what points to what we held in this chapter and carried its meaning upon is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the one who guides to good, that he is like its doer."
"1771","1545 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf al-Azraq narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The one who guides to good is like its doer}."
"1772","1546 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban — meaning al-Nahwi — narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Sa'd ibn Iyas — Abu Ja'far said: and he is Abu 'Amr al-Shaybani — from Abu Mas'ud, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1773","1547 - And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn 'Abdullah al-Hammal narrated to us, he said: Ya'la and Muhammad, sons of 'Ubayd, narrated to us, they said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Iyas, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari — and Ya'la said: from Abu 'Amr al-Shaybani, from Abu Mas'ud, he said: {A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my mount is exhausted, so carry me. He said: I find nothing to carry you upon; go to so-and-so. So he went to him and he carried him. Then he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who guides to good has the like of the reward of its doer}. This is Muhammad's wording."
"1774","1548 - And as Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-'Ayshi narrated to us, he said: 'Imran ibn Yazid al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The one who guides to good is like its doer}. Abu Ja'far said: And since the guide by his guiding to good deserves what the doer of that good deserves, then whoever establishes a good practice, guiding people by acting upon it so they act upon it after him, is in his establishing it for them, in reward, like them in their acting upon it; and likewise in burden, his establishing it for them is, in their acting after him upon it, in burden, like them in it. And among what strengthens that also:"
"1775","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, he said: The son of Adam who killed his brother shares with the people of Hell half the punishment of Hell in exact division. That points to what we mentioned in the first hadith. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1776","246 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Whoever builds for Allah a mosque, Allah builds for him a house — or a mosque, according to what was narrated in that — in Paradise}. 1549 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever builds for Allah Almighty a mosque, even like a sandgrouse's nesting hollow, a house is built for him in Paradise}."
"1777","1550 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, raised it, the like of it. Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith: Ibn Yunus said: None of al-A'mash's companions raised it except Abu Bakr. Ahmad said: So it was said to Abu Bakr: None but you raised it. He said: I heard it from al-A'mash when he was young."
"1778","1551 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he raised it, the like of it."
"1779","1552 - And Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb al-Nasha'i narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, from his brother Ya'la, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1780","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Yazid ibn Sharik al-Taymi, from Abu Dharr, and he did not raise it; then he mentioned the like of it, and added: And a good deed is written for him."
"1781","1553 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from his father, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever builds for Allah a mosque, Allah builds for him the like of it in Paradise}."
"1782","1554 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Mahmud ibn 'Amr, from Asma' bint Yazid, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever builds for Allah Almighty a mosque, Allah builds for him one more spacious than it in Paradise}."
"1783","1555 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from 'Ammar al-Duhni, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: {Whoever builds for Allah a house, even like a sandgrouse's nesting hollow, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise}."
"1784","1556 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-'Amiri narrated to us — Abu Ja'far said — and he is the one known as al-Mu'adhdhin — he said: 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah narrated to me, he said: 'A'ishah narrated to me, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever builds a mosque, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise. So I said: O Prophet of Allah, and these mosques built on the road to Makkah? He said: And those}."
"1785","1557 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Nashit, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: {Whoever builds a mosque like a sandgrouse's nesting hollow or smaller, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise}. Then someone said: This hadith has come confused — some narrated it: Allah builds for him a house in Paradise, and some narrated it: Allah builds for him a mosque in Paradise, and this is confusion from the narrators. Our answer to him in that: This is not confusion from them, may Allah be pleased with them. You should have taken what the group narrated as worthier than what one narrated, so that the reports agree and do not contradict. Since you did not do that — and Allah is the one sought for help — then that, in our view, is with a meaning whose intent escaped you; for mosques are only built as houses, then become mosques by prayer in them, and before prayer in them they are houses not mosques, even if those who built them as houses intended them to be mosques; they are not so until prayer is performed in them, so they are houses that are mosques. And since that is so in this world, it is permitted that what Allah Almighty rewards one who built a mosque in this world with — building for him in Paradise as reward for that mosque — and what he built in this world was not a mosque by his building it intending it as a mosque, until the Muslims prayed in it; and what Allah built for him in Paradise as reward for it is not of what is prayed in in Paradise, for Paradise is not a house of deeds but a house of recompense. So after Allah Almighty builds it for him, it remains with the like of the name of the mosque he built in this world before people prayed in it — and it is a house, as in the other hadiths: Whoever builds for Allah a house, Allah Almighty builds for him a house in Paradise. So by Allah's praise there is in nothing narrated in this chapter contradiction or disagreement. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1786","247 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying at the opening of prayer: And with that I was commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims. 1558 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Nasr ibn al-Mu'arik narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan informed us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun, from his uncle, from al-A'raj, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he opened prayer, said: I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and earth, upright, Muslim, and I am not of the polytheists. Indeed my prayer and my worship and my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, no partner has He; and with that I was commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims}."
"1787","1559 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: And this uncle of al-Majishun is Ya'qub ibn Abi Salamah, Abu Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Majishun."
"1788","1560 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' al-Ghudani narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi and 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, from al-Majishun and 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from al-A'raj, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1789","1561 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from al-A'raj, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Then someone said: How do you accept from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is attributed to him of his saying in this hadith: And I am the first of the Muslims, when before him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there were Muslims of the prophets, Allah's blessings upon them, who were before him, and of others besides them. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that his saying: And I am the first of the Muslims means he is the first of the Muslims of the generation he was sent among; and his Lord Almighty commanded him with that by His saying: {Indeed my prayer and my worship and my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, no partner has He, and with that I was commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims}. And the like of that is Musa's saying (peace be upon him), when he awoke from his swoon after asking his Lord Almighty to show him that he might look at Him, from his saying: {Then when he awoke he said: Glory be to You, I repent to You, and I am the first of the believers} — meaning thereby the believers who believed in him; and before him (peace be upon him) there were believing prophets, Allah's blessings upon them, and non-prophets of those who believed in what the prophets brought them. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1790","248 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying in opening prayer after what we mentioned from him in the first chapter: O Allah, You are the King, there is no god but You; You are my Lord and I am Your servant; I wronged myself and confess my sin, so forgive me all my sins — none forgives sins but You; guide me to the best of character — none guides to its best but You; turn its evil from me — none turns its evil from me but You; here I am at Your service, blessed are You and exalted; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. 1562 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated it to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: My uncle informed me, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with what we mentioned in the title of this chapter."
"1791","1563 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' said... 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi and 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, from al-Majishun and 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, from al-A'raj, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So we considered his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And evil is not toward You, and found it may mean: And evil is not intended toward You; for whoever does good intends thereby toward Allah Almighty, hoping His reward and fulfillment of what was promised for it, and whoever does evil does not intend thereby toward Allah Almighty — even though each of good and evil is from Allah Almighty, as He said Almighty: {And if good befalls them they say: This is from Allah, and if evil befalls them they say: This is from you; say: All is from Allah} — i.e. all that is from Allah: so the people of happiness are facilitated to good and do it, and He rewards them and recompenses them for it; and the people of wretchedness are facilitated to evil and do it, and He punishes them for it, except that He pardons them for what pardon of its like is permissible — and that is what is besides associating partners with Him. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1792","Harun ibn Muhammad al-'Asqalani permitted us, from al-Ghallabi, from Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ma'in, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl said: And evil is not toward You — its explanation: And evil is not used to draw near to You."
"1793","249 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about his permitting al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib to spend nights at Makkah during the nights of Mina on account of the water service. 1564 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah and 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, {that al-'Abbas asked the Prophet's permission (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to spend at Makkah the nights of Mina on account of his water service, so he permitted him}."
"1794","1565 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib to spend at Makkah the days of Mina on account of his water service}."
"1795","1566 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, he said: Nafi' narrated to me — and I know it only from Ibn 'Umar — that {al-'Abbas asked the Messenger of Allah's permission (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to spend the nights of Mina at Makkah on account of the water service, so he permitted him}. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) release of al-'Abbas to spend at Makkah the nights of Mina on account of the water service, for its need of him in maintaining it for people. In that is what shows that anyone besides him among people, whom the water service has no need of in that, is contrary to him. Someone said: But you narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what he used to do in those nights, what contradicts this — and he mentioned:"
"1796","1567 - What Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ar'arah narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham handed me a book, which I did not hear from him, and said: I heard it from my father, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, from Ibn 'Abbas, {that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit the House every night of the nights of Mina}. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that this hadith in our view does not contradict the first hadith; for what is in the first hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) release of al-'Abbas to spend at Makkah, for the water service's need of that from him — and proof of barring others from the like of that, of those the water service has no need of. And what is in Ibn 'Abbas's hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visiting the House in every night of the nights of Mina, and in that is not his spending the night (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Makkah; for it is permissible that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would visit the House, then return and spend that night at Mina, so he is of those who spent the night there. And in that is what shows that what was intended from the pilgrim is spending the nights of Mina at Mina, and it was not intended from them not to leave Mina in those nights. Do you not see that it is permitted for them to leave it at night until they come to Makkah and circumambulate the House the circumambulation of visitation, then return to it and spend the night there, without thereby missing spending the night there. And likewise is customary in spendings of the night: do you not see that one who swore not to spend this night in this house, then stayed in it less than half of it, then left it to another, staying in it the rest until morning — he has not broken his oath, for he did not spend the night in it; and that had he stayed in it more than half of it, then left to another, staying in it the rest until morning, he has broken it, for he spent the night in it. Such is customary. Do you not see that one who meets a man at night before half of it has passed, it is permitted to say to him: Where will you spend tonight? And that had he met him after half of it passed, it is permitted to say to him: Where did you spend tonight? Likewise what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his visiting the House in every night of the nights of Mina, is in our view — and Allah knows best — upon his returning from it to Mina before half the night passes, so he is there until morning in it, so thereby he is spending the night in it. So by Allah's praise and blessing this hadith and the meaning of the first hadith agree and do not differ. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"1797","250 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his forbidding a man to say: My slave and my slave-girl, and commanding him to say in its place: My youth and my maiden. 1568 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah — Qabisah said: I think he raised it — he said: {Let none of you say: My slave, for all of you are slaves; but let him say: My youth}."
"1798","1569 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, freedman of al-Huraqah, narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Let none of you say: My slave and my slave-girl, for all of you are slaves of Allah, and all of you are slave-girls of Allah; but let him say: My lad and my slave-girl and my youth and my maiden}. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated was {the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding anyone to say to his male slave: My slave, nor to his female slave: My slave-girl, and his commanding him to say in its place: My youth and my maiden}. Then someone said: How do you accept this, when Allah's Book came with release of what this hadith barred? Allah Almighty said: {Allah sets forth a parable of a slave owned, unable over anything} — so He mentioned him with servitude and ownership, and described him as unable over anything; and Allah Almighty said: {And marry the unmarried among you and the righteous of your male slaves and your female slaves}. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that we deem all that sound, and do not make part of it contradict part; and we take what is in His saying Almighty {and the righteous of your male slaves and your female slaves} upon attribution of others of them to them, and we take what is forbidden in the reports we narrated upon the owners attributing them to themselves — that they are their slaves and their slave-girls — since that returns to the meaning of their arrogance over them, even though all of them are to Allah Almighty slaves. And someone said: Allah Almighty's saying: {Allah sets forth a parable of a slave owned, unable over anything} is only upon His mentioning the slave being of what may be upon the non-owned slave, and of what may be upon the owned slave, so Allah Almighty clarified the slave He meant by His saying: owned, so it be known thereby that he is the owned slave, not the slave who is not owned. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"1799","251 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from Abu Hurayrah, of what there is no doubt he did not say from his opinion, but only said it from taking it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), since the like of it is not said by opinion — and it is his saying: {Let none of you say: My lord — meaning to his owner — but let him say: My master}. 1570 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah — Qabisah said: I think he raised it — he said: {Let none of you say: My lord — meaning to his owner — but let him say: My master}. Then someone said: How do you accept this so as to bar slaves from saying this to their owners, when Allah's Book came with release of the like of that? Allah, exalted His praise, said in what He related from His prophet Yusuf (peace be upon him), in his interpreting the dream told to him: {O two companions of prison, as for one of you, he will give his lord wine to drink} — meaning: his owner who is a chief over him. And when the like of this chief over a non-owned subordinate is so, it is more permissible from an owned subordinate to one who owns him. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that Yusuf's saying (peace be upon him) this is only upon addressing one who called the one whose dream he told him 'lord', so he addressed him with that according to what he is with him upon — not that he is with Yusuf (peace be upon him) so; and likewise Musa the prophet of Allah's saying (peace be upon him) to the Samiri: {And look at your god to which you remained devoted; we will surely burn it, then scatter it in the sea in scattering} — it is not that he was with Musa a god, but he was with the Samiri so, so Musa addressed him with that according to what he is with him upon. And the slave does not make his owner a lord to him so as to be addressed with that, like what each of Yusuf and Musa addressed whom he addressed with of what we mentioned from him. So it was forbidden to say that to him, and commanded to put in its place what has no lordship in it. And if someone says: But you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said about the lost camel: What is it to you? With it are its water-skin and its sandals; it comes to water and eats trees until its lord finds it}."
"1800","1571 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that. Our answer to him in that is that beasts are not held accountable as the sons of Adam are held accountable; so beasts are thereby in the meaning of goods whose attribution to their owners is permitted, and that they are lords of them."
